/* Generated by js/buildPracticeIndex.mjs from the lessons' own drills. Do not edit by hand. */
window.TL_DRILLS = [{"slug":"active-portfolio-management","title":"Active Portfolio Management","date":"2007-08-08","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2007-08-08\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 8 August 2007</div> <h3>Thousands of independent bets are all losing at once</h3> <p>You run a quant long/short equity book: a small edge on each of thousands of names, market beta hedged out, sized on the breadth those thousands of bets were supposed to give you. Since Monday the book has lost heavily, day after day, and the losses have little to do with which way the broad market is moving. Your models call the moves many-standard-deviation events. Your risk limits are close. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the positions. Thousands of independent bets cannot all be wrong, so this is a fluke.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The bets are plainly not independent: they are losing together, which independent bets never do. The \"many sigma\" reading comes from a risk model that assumed the breadth was real. Adding doubles down on one crowded bet.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Dump the whole book at the market today.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You would be selling the same names as every other crowded fund, into the worst prices of the move. Forced, all-at-once selling is exactly the mechanism deepening the losses.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-measure the book's risk as if it were one crowded bet, and cut gross in steps to a size you could survive if it lost again tomorrow.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson read honestly: effective breadth has collapsed towards one, so size it like one bet. Cutting in steps keeps you alive without dumping everything at the bottom, and leaves you holding part of the book if prices recover.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Hold the full size and wait for it to pass.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The positions may well recover, but you are holding one bet sized as if it were thousands. If the unwind runs another week, your limits force you out at even worse prices.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Many quant funds hit their limits and cut the same positions, deepening the move. Goldman Sachs's Global Equity Opportunities fund was reported to have lost roughly 30% in early August, and on 13 August Goldman and outside investors put $3 billion into it. Prices rebounded sharply on Friday 10 August, after many funds had already cut. Khandani and Lo's best-documented account traces it to the hypothesised fast unwind of one large quant book.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000886982/000089534507000454/ex99.htm\">Goldman Sachs press release on the $3 billion GEO investment, 13 August 2007 (SEC Form 8-K exhibit)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/research/conference/2007/liquidity/Khandani_Lo.pdf\">Khandani and Lo, \"What Happened to the Quants in August 2007?\", 2007</a>; Bloomberg, reported fund losses, August 2007.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"alpha-beta-separation","title":"Alpha vs. Beta Separation","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · autumn 2008</div> <h3>Margin calls on the beta leg, and the alpha leg is gated</h3> <p>You run a pension plan's portable alpha programme. The equity beta is held through S&amp;P 500 futures, which need only a slice of the money as margin; the rest sits in funds of hedge funds and an enhanced-cash credit portfolio, as the alpha. Equities are falling fast. The futures demand cash margin every day, the hedge-fund sleeve is down too and pays out only quarterly, and some managers have put up gates. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Wait. Submit redemptions to the hedge funds and meet the calls when that cash arrives.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Margin is due now; the redemptions arrive in months, if the gates lift at all. The two legs have different liquidity, and the beta leg will not wait for the alpha leg.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add more futures. The alpha leg is uncorrelated with the market, so the programme is still sound, and stocks are cheap.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The alpha leg is clearly not uncorrelated: it is losing in the same crisis. More futures means bigger margin calls with no liquid money to pay them.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the two legs as one exposure: cut the futures overlay to a size your truly liquid assets can fund, and meet calls from cash and liquid holdings now.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The residual turned out to carry credit and liquidity risk that the one-factor regression never measured. Sizing the overlay to the cash you can actually raise stops the programme from becoming a forced seller.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Close the whole futures position at once to stop the margin calls.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It ends the cash drain, but it locks in the beta loss and leaves the plan with the wrong equity exposure. A controlled cut to a fundable size does the same job with less damage.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In 2008 the S&amp;P 500 fell by more than a third. Portable alpha programmes lost on the futures beta leg and on the \"alpha\" leg at the same time: the HFRI Fund Weighted Composite hedge fund index had its worst year since it began in 1990, and enhanced-cash portfolios holding structured credit lost money too. Some plans had to sell other assets at bad prices to meet collateral calls, and after 2008 many institutions scaled back or wound down these programmes.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://publishedresearch.cambridgeassociates.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/Is-the-Hedge-Fund-Business-Model-Broken-Jan-2009.pdf\">Cambridge Associates, \"Is the Hedge Fund Business Model Broken?\", January 2009 (HFRI returns through November 2008)</a>; S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices, S&amp;P 500 calendar-year return for 2008 (about −38% on price).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"anomaly-detection-isolation-autoencoders","title":"Anomaly Detection: Isolation Forests &amp; Autoencoders","date":"2015-01-15","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-01-15\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 15 January 2015</div> <h3>Every EUR/CHF tick is flagged at once</h3> <p>You run an Isolation Forest over every incoming FX tick, trained on months of recent data. For years EUR/CHF has traded just above the 1.20 floor the Swiss National Bank has defended. This morning, within minutes, almost every EUR/CHF tick scores near 1: prices far below 1.20, spreads blown out, quote rates unlike anything in the training data. Your pipeline is set to drop flagged ticks automatically. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Let the pipeline drop them. The detector says they are anomalies, so they are bad data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A score measures rarity, not correctness. A genuine, sharp news-driven move scores exactly like a data error. Dropping these ticks would erase a real move from your prices, your models and your risk numbers.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Stop the automatic drop, have a person check the flagged ticks against the news and other venues, and keep them if the move is real.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the human-judgment step the lesson insists on. The model surfaces a ranked list of unusual ticks; a person decides whether unusual means wrong. Here a quick check shows a policy announcement, not a broken feed.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy the move. A score this high means the market is mispriced.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Nothing about an anomaly score implies the pattern is profitable or repeatable. It describes rarity relative to history. It is not a forecast of edge.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Retrain the detector on today's data so it stops flagging everything.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Sensible later, once the new regime is understood. A model trained on the calm, floor-defended regime will keep flagging the new one. But retraining in the middle of the event does not answer today's question: are these ticks real?</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 15 January 2015 the Swiss National Bank announced it was abandoning the 1.20 minimum exchange rate for EUR/CHF. The franc jumped and EUR/CHF traded well below 1.00 within minutes. The ticks were unusual and entirely real. A detector trained on the floor years was right that nothing like it had been seen, and a pipeline that equated \"anomalous\" with \"wrong\" would have deleted some of the most important prints of that year.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bis.org/review/r150116a.htm\">Thomas Jordan, SNB press conference remarks on discontinuing the minimum exchange rate, 15 January 2015 (BIS reprint)</a>; Swiss National Bank press release, \"Swiss National Bank discontinues minimum exchange rate and lowers interest rate to –0.75%\", 15 January 2015.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"arima-var","title":"ARIMA &amp; Vector Autoregression (VAR)","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative lead-lag result</div> <h3>The spread Granger-causes EUR/USD. Now what?</h3> <p>You fit a VAR(2) to daily changes in the 2-year DE–US yield spread and daily EUR/USD log returns, both stationary. You test both directions. Two days of lagged spread changes significantly improve the EUR/USD forecast; lagged EUR/USD returns do not help forecast the spread. What do you do with this?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Conclude that the spread drives EUR/USD, and trade it at full size on the full-sample result.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Granger causality is a forecasting claim, not a mechanism: a common driver could produce the same result. And a full-sample fit can hide a regime where the link reverses. Nothing here has been tested out of sample.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the spread as a candidate leading indicator with a two-day window, then test that timing out of sample, across regimes, against a random-walk benchmark.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That uses what the VAR found, a direction and a horizon, without over-reading it. The out-of-sample test is where the claim earns trust, and in FX the random walk is the bar to beat.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Refit the VAR on raw yield and price levels to get a stronger fit.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Fitting trending levels invites spurious regression: impressive fit statistics and significant-looking coefficients that are a statistical mirage.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Use the spread only as a same-day confirmation check.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Cautious, and it will not blow up. But it throws away the one useful thing the test found: that the spread's effect shows up over the following two days.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What it means.</strong> This is an illustrative result, the same hypothetical one the lesson uses, not a documented fit. The documented fact that frames it: Meese and Rogoff (1983) found that exchange-rate models, VARs included, did not beat a random walk out of sample. A one-directional Granger result is a reason to test a timing rule, not proof that one works.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"attention-transformers-time-series","title":"Attention &amp; Transformers for Time Series","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research review</div> <h3>A transformer backtest that looks too good</h3> <p>A colleague trained a transformer on two years of daily EURUSD data to forecast the next day's direction. The backtest hit rate is far above anything the team's tuned LSTM ever managed. Reading the code, you notice two things: the attention layer has no causal mask, and nobody ran the tuned LSTM or a linear model on the same walk-forward split. What do you do before any money goes near it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Allocate to it. Transformers dominate language and vision, so a big jump over the LSTM is what you would expect.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Reputation in another domain is not evidence on this series. Two years of daily data is short and noisy, exactly where a transformer's extra capacity tends to buy more convincing overfitting rather than real accuracy.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Add a causal mask, rerun it walk-forward out of sample, and compare it with the tuned LSTM and a linear baseline on the same split.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Without the mask, each day can attend to later days, so the backtest may be pure look-ahead. Once masked, the only evidence that counts is a fair, leakage-free comparison with simpler models on the same data.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Read the attention weights to check the model is relying on sensible past days.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Useful for understanding the model, and weight on future days would expose the leak. But a sensible-looking weight pattern is not proof of edge, and it does not replace the masked, out-of-sample comparison.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add more heads and layers to push the result further.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More capacity on a short series makes overfitting easier, and it does nothing about the missing mask. You would be polishing a result that may not exist.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This is an illustrative case, not a historical event. The lesson's two rules decide it: a forecaster without a causal mask can attend to the future, so a spectacular backtest is the first sign of leakage, not of edge; and a transformer earns its place only by beating a well-tuned simpler model out of sample on the same series.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"bayesian-conjugate-priors","title":"Conjugate Priors &amp; Bayesian Updating in Practice","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative trading log</div> <h3>Fifteen occurrences in, and the setup looks good</h3> <p>You log one setup: price touches the prior day's high with volume confirmation, and you note whether it holds as resistance. You started with an honest, mild \\(\\text{Beta}(3,3)\\) prior. After 15 occurrences, 9 held, so your belief is \\(\\text{Beta}(12,9)\\), mean about 0.571. This morning the setup appears again. What do you do with the estimate?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Treat 57% as the true hold rate and size the trade as if it were known.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">\\(\\text{Beta}(12,9)\\) still has a spread of about 0.11. A true rate anywhere from the high 40s to the high 60s fits what you have seen. The width of the curve is the confidence, and right now it is wide.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Trade it small, add today's result to the counts, and let the curve narrow before you size up.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what conjugate updating is for. Each occurrence is one more count, and the spread shrinks with every one. You size to the belief you have, not the one you hope to have.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch to a heavier, more optimistic prior, now that you can see the setup works.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Choosing the prior after peeking at the data is p-hacking in a Bayesian costume. The prior should be stated before the evidence that updates it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Ignore the log until you have 100 occurrences.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safe, but it wastes what you already know. The posterior tells you exactly how unsure you are at 15; you do not need a magic sample size to use it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What to take from it.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's own trader log, not a historical event. In the lesson, 60 more occurrences (38 held) take the belief to \\(\\text{Beta}(50,31)\\): mean about 0.617, with the spread down from about 0.11 to about 0.05. The mean barely moved; the confidence did. Even then, a hit rate says nothing about profit after costs.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"bayesian-inference","title":"Bayesian Inference &amp; Updating","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative morning</div> <h3>The trend-day flag has just fired</h3> <p>Your regime classifier, validated at 80% accuracy on both trend days and reversion days, has flagged today as a trend day. In this market only about 20% of days are trend days. You are deciding how to trade the session. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade it as an 80% trend day and size up.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">80% is \\(P(E\\mid H)\\), how often the flag fires on real trend days. What you need is \\(P(H\\mid E)\\), and with a 20% base rate that is only 50%. Sizing on 80% badly overstates your edge.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat it as roughly a coin flip, 50%, and look for independent evidence before leaning harder.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the posterior: 160 real flags against 160 false ones out of every 1,000 days. The flag moved you from 20% to 50%, a Bayes factor of 4. A second, genuinely independent signal could move you further.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Ignore the flag. The classifier is no better than a coin.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Too harsh. Going from 20% to 50% is real evidence. Throwing it away wastes information. The flag should change your belief, just not as far as the headline number suggests.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Check a second indicator built from the same daily pivots. If it agrees, treat the day as a near-certain trend day.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Two signals reading the same thing are one piece of evidence counted twice. Chaining them as if independent inflates the posterior beyond what the evidence supports.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the arithmetic says.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's worked example, not a dated event. Out of 1,000 days, 200 are trend days and the classifier flags 160 of them; it also flags 160 of the 800 reversion days. Of 320 flags, 160 are real: a 50% posterior. If trend days were 50% of days, the same classifier would be right 80% of the time when it fires.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"black-scholes","title":"Black-Scholes-Merton Option Pricing","date":"1987-10-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1987-10-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 October 1987</div> <h3>Your insurance is a formula, and the market just fell 4.6%</h3> <p>It is Friday evening. The market has slid all week, and today the Dow fell about 4.6%. You run portfolio insurance for pension funds: no bought puts, just the replication argument. You hold stocks and cash and sell S&amp;P 500 index futures as the market falls, matching a put's delta. Your deltas already say you are behind, and on Monday they will tell you to sell more. Many other insurers run the same rules. What do you plan for Monday?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Follow the model as usual. It says you can adjust a little at a time, near the last price.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the model's assumption, not a fact about Monday. Continuous prices and a hedger too small to move them are exactly what fails when everyone running the same rule has to sell at once.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Plan for a gap: assume fills far below the model's prices, cut the size you are protecting where you can, and warn clients the floor may not hold.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This takes the lesson's limits seriously. Replication only works while you can trade smoothly at prices you do not move. Planning for a gap is the honest response when your own hedging is part of the flow.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Do nothing until Monday's prices are in. The delta will tell you what to do.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The delta will tell you to sell, into the same falling market as every other insurer. Waiting hands your decision to the feedback loop the formula has no term for.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Replace part of the synthetic protection with real index puts bought from someone else.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Real puts do not need you to trade during the crash, so the protection holds. But after a week of falls they cost more, and buying them is not the same as admitting the synthetic floor may fail for the rest of the book.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On Monday 19 October 1987 the Dow fell 22.6%, still its largest one-day percentage fall of the modern era, and the S&amp;P 500 fell about 20%. The Brady Commission found that portfolio insurers' futures selling, with other mechanical selling, was a significant source of the pressure. Futures traded far below the stocks they tracked, fills came in far below the model's prices, and clients got less protection than promised. Out-of-the-money index puts have carried higher implied volatility ever since.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1987\">Federal Reserve History, \"Stock Market Crash of 1987\"</a> (Dow −4.6% on 16 October, −22.6% on 19 October); Report of the Presidential Task Force on Market Mechanisms (the Brady Commission), January 1988.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"bond-pricing-ytm","title":"Bond Pricing &amp; Yield to Maturity","date":"2022-11-02","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-11-02\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 2 November 2022</div> <h3>Your bonds are down a quarter, and your deposits could leave</h3> <p>You run the bond portfolio of a bank. In 2020 you parked deposits in low-coupon Treasuries, including the 0.625% 10-year note sold that August, bought close to par. Today the Fed raised rates by 0.75 points for the fourth meeting in a row. The 10-year yield, about 1.5% in January, is now around 4%. At that yield the formula prices your note near $77 per $100. Nothing it promises has changed. Your depositors are few and large, and they could move money in a day. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. It is only a paper loss, and the note pulls to par at maturity.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">True only for a holder who can wait. With deposits that can leave in a day, you may be forced to sell before maturity, and then the discount is a real loss. The formula prices the cash flows, not your ability to keep them.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy more long, low-coupon bonds. Yields are high, so they are cheap.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">They are cheaper, but you are doubling the very exposure that hurts if you have to sell early. More long bonds means a bigger forced loss if the deposits go.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Plan for having to sell: line up other funding or capital now, and cut the rate risk, so the bonds are never sold at the worst moment.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The loss is only on paper while you can hold to maturity. Making sure you can hold, or that you are not forced to sell into a run, is what keeps it on paper.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Sell the whole portfolio now and take the loss.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It removes the risk of a forced sale later, but it turns a mark into a certain loss of about a quarter on those notes. Defensible if funding really is fragile; costly if it was not.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The Fed ended 2022 at 4.25–4.50%, and the broad US investment-grade bond index lost about 13% that year, its worst on record. Banks that had parked deposits in low-coupon bonds sat on large unrealised losses. In March 2023 one of them, Silicon Valley Bank, sold about $21 billion of bonds at an after-tax loss of about $1.8 billion to raise cash as deposits drained. Depositors ran, and it failed within days. Holders who could wait still receive par at maturity.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20221102a.htm\">Federal Reserve press release, 2 November 2022</a> (target range raised to 3-3/4 to 4 percent); <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/719739/000119312523070254/d487554d8k.htm\">SVB Financial Group Form 8-K, 8 March 2023</a> (about $21 billion of securities sold, after-tax loss of about $1.8 billion); Bloomberg US Aggregate index 2022 return, as reported by Bloomberg and Morningstar.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"bootstrapping-monte-carlo","title":"Bootstrapping &amp; Monte Carlo Simulation","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative risk-committee pitch</div> <h3>A tight bootstrap interval, presented as robustness</h3> <p>A researcher bootstraps a strategy's trade returns 10,000 times and computes the Sharpe ratio on each resample. The 95% interval is tight: the resampled Sharpe barely moves. They want to tell the risk committee the strategy is \"robust\". You review the slide. What do you ask for?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. 10,000 resamples with a tight interval is strong robustness evidence.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Every resample was built from the same trades, made by the same signal over the same period. A tight interval shows the sample is internally consistent, not that the signal works on data it has never seen.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Relabel the interval as sample consistency and expectation-setting, and require a walk-forward test on unseen data before anyone calls it robust.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That keeps the bootstrap for what it does well, an honest error bar, and sends the generalization question to the only test that can answer it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Ask for a block bootstrap, since daily returns cluster.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A real improvement: a plain bootstrap on clustered returns gives an interval that is too narrow. But a block bootstrap still never leaves the same sample, so it still cannot speak to unseen data.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Ask for 100,000 resamples, or Monte Carlo paths from a model fitted to the same returns.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More resamples only cut simulation noise. Monte Carlo paths inherit their settings from the same history. Neither adds a single new observation.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What it means.</strong> This is an illustrative review, the lesson's own scenario, not a documented case. The tight interval was genuine evidence that no single outlier trade carries the result. Whether the strategy works out of sample is a separate question, and only data the strategy has never touched can answer it.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"calculus-for-finance","title":"Calculus for Finance: Derivatives, Integrals &amp; Taylor Series","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative overnight gap</div> <h3>Delta says you lose $6. Do you?</h3> <p>You have sold the lesson's call option: price $4.30, underlying at $100, delta 0.60. For this drill, suppose its gamma is 0.03. Overnight the underlying gaps up $10, to $110. Your quick risk screen uses delta only and says the option gained about \\(0.60 \\times 10 = \\$6.00\\), so you lost about $6.00 per option. What do you expect the real loss to be?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">About $6.00. Delta is the derivative, so it gives the answer.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Delta is only the first-order term: the tangent line. A $10 gap is not a small step, and the option's price curve bends away from that line.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">More than $6.00. Add gamma's term, \\(\\tfrac12\\gamma(\\Delta S)^2\\): half of 0.03 times 100 is $1.50, so about $7.50. Then confirm by repricing.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the second-order Taylor expansion: \\(\\Delta C \\approx \\delta\\,\\Delta S\\) \\(+\\, \\tfrac12\\gamma\\,(\\Delta S)^2\\). For a short option, the curvature always works against you, whichever way the big move goes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Less than $6.00. Big moves are rare, so the estimate is probably too high.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Rarity has nothing to do with it once the move has happened. A call's value curves upward, so for a rise it gains more than the tangent line says, not less.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">No idea until the option is fully repriced.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Full repricing is the most exact answer and worth doing. But the Taylor terms tell you the direction and rough size immediately, which is the point of having them.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What to take from it.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a historical event: the price, spot and delta come from the lesson's example, and the gamma of 0.03 is assumed for the drill. With those numbers the delta-gamma estimate is \\(6.00 + 1.50 = \\$7.50\\), a quarter more than delta alone. The bigger the move, the more the second-order term matters.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"central-bank-policy","title":"Central Bank Policy","date":"2012-07-26","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2012-07-26\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 26 July 2012</div> <h3>\"Whatever it takes\"</h3> <p>The euro-area debt crisis is at its worst. You are short Spanish 10-year government bonds, a bet that their yield, now above 7%, keeps rising. This morning, at an investment conference in London, ECB President Mario Draghi said that within its mandate the ECB is ready to do \"whatever it takes\" to preserve the euro. No policy has changed. What do you do with the short?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Stay short. Nothing was announced; words are cheap.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This reads the stance, not the surprise. A credible promise from the one buyer that can create euros changes what the market prices for the worst outcomes, and your short was a bet on exactly those outcomes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Close or sharply cut the short. The priced path for Spanish yields has just changed, even with no action yet.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Forward guidance and jawboning work by moving what is priced today. The surprise is against what the market expected the ECB to tolerate, and it points straight at your position.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the short. \"Within its mandate\" means the ECB cannot really follow through.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You are betting against a central bank's stated willingness to act, on a legal reading, at the moment the market is repricing its credibility. That is the hard way to be right.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Halve the short and wait for the details of an actual programme.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Sensible risk control, but it treats the promise as noise until action arrives. The lesson's point is that a credible promise moves prices on its own.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In September 2012 the ECB announced Outright Monetary Transactions, a programme to buy the bonds of countries that requested support under conditions. Spanish and Italian yields fell a long way over the following months. OMT has never been used: the market repriced on a credible promise, and the action was never needed.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/key/date/2012/html/sp120726.en.html\">Mario Draghi, speech at the Global Investment Conference, London, 26 July 2012 (ECB)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/pr/date/2012/html/pr120906_1.en.html\">ECB press release announcing Outright Monetary Transactions, 6 September 2012</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"change-point-detection","title":"Change-Point Detection","date":"2020-02-24","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-02-24\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 24 February 2020</div> <h3>The market is falling hard, and your detector is still quiet</h3> <p>You watch S&amp;P 500 daily returns with a CUSUM. You tuned its allowance and threshold so it stayed quiet through 2019, a calm year with plenty of single bad days that led nowhere. The index closed at a record on Wednesday 19 February. Over the weekend the outbreak in northern Italy showed the virus had spread well beyond China, and today, Monday, markets are falling hard. Your CUSUM has not crossed its threshold. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Wait for the alarm. It was tuned on a full year of data, so trust it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The detector was tuned to ignore single bad days, so by design it needs several large deviations to build up before it crosses. Silence on the first big day is the detection delay, not an all-clear.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the quiet alarm as late by design. Check volatility directly, cut risk as it jumps, and read any later alarm as confirmation.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This uses the tool for what it is. A mean-shift CUSUM tuned on a calm year is slow, and it is watching only the mean while the variance is shifting too. Acting on the volatility jump does not wait for evidence to pile up inside the delay, which is where the costliest part of the move sits.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Lower the threshold right now so the detector fires today.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You are right that the alarm is late, but this is your judgement wearing the detector's clothes. A threshold picked after seeing today's move will also fire on every ordinary bad day later, so the alarm stops meaning anything.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Fit an offline change-point model to the year so far and act on where it puts the break.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">An offline fit decides where the break was by using data from after it. Today there is almost no post-break data, so it cannot see the break yet. Weeks later it will place it neatly at 20 to 24 February: that is hindsight, not a signal you could have traded.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> By 27 February the S&amp;P 500 was more than 10% below its record, the fastest correction from an all-time high on record, in six trading days. On 16 March it fell about 12% in one session and the VIX closed above 82, a record. By 23 March the index was roughly a third below its February high. An offline fit later put the break at 20 to 24 February. An online detector quiet enough for 2019 could only flag it after several large days had piled up, so its alarm mostly confirmed what the P&amp;L already showed.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\">S&amp;P 500 daily close via FRED (record 3,386.15 on 19 February 2020; 2,978.76 on 27 February, 12.0% below it; 2,386.13 on 16 March, down 12.0% on the day; 2,237.40 on 23 March, 33.9% below the record)</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\">Cboe VIX daily close via FRED (82.69 on 16 March 2020)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200323a.htm\">Federal Reserve FOMC statement, 23 March 2020</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"clustering-market-structure","title":"Clustering for Market Structure: k-Means &amp; Hierarchical Clustering","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research meeting</div> <h3>Three regimes, found with no labels</h3> <p>A researcher ran k-means with \\(k=3\\) on two years of daily features: realized volatility, trend strength, cross-asset correlation and credit-spread momentum. The three clusters look like \"calm trending\", \"calm range-bound\" and \"stressed\" days, and the plot is beautifully separated. The proposal is to switch strategies by cluster from Monday. What do you ask for first?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing more. The clusters are well separated and match intuition, so the regimes are real.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">k-means returns three groups whenever you ask for three, from any data, including noise. A clean picture proves only that the algorithm reduced its objective. Matching intuition is the algorithm confirming its own output.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Check the \"stressed\" days against dates known to be volatile from other evidence, and see whether the split survives a perturbed feature set and \\(k=4\\).<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the independent sanity check the lesson asks for. Groups that line up with evidence the clustering never saw, and that hold up when the inputs shift a little, are a finding. Anything less is a hypothesis.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Rerun k-means from several random starts and keep the lowest-WCSS result.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Necessary practice, because one run can land in a poor local optimum. But it only makes the clustering more stable. It still says nothing about whether the groups mean anything.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Pick \\(k\\) by the highest silhouette score instead. If it says 2, use two regimes.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The silhouette score is a heuristic, not a calculator for the right \\(k\\), and it can favor a small \\(k\\) that is useless for the question. It also does nothing to validate the groups.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own regime-discovery scenario, not a historical event. Clustering is unsupervised: there is no answer key inside the process. A regime map earns trust only by surviving checks against evidence the algorithm never had.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"coherent-risk-measures","title":"Coherent Risk Measures: VaR, CVaR &amp; the Artzner Axioms","date":"2012-01","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2012-01\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · late January 2012</div> <h3>The book is over its VaR limit, and a new model says it isn't</h3> <p>You sit on the risk team that oversees a big bank's large credit-derivatives book, which the bank describes as a hedge. This month the book has been over its VaR limit. The office running it has just switched to a new VaR model it had been developing, and the reported VaR has roughly halved. The book is back inside its limit. Not one position has been cut. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Accept the new number. The book is inside its limit, so there is nothing to do.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The positions did not change; only the measurement did. A model that halves the risk of an unchanged book, chosen by the office the limit is meant to constrain, is exactly the number to check before you trust it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Move the limit to Expected Shortfall on the new model. It is coherent, so the problem is solved.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Coherence is a property of the formula, not of the data under it. An Expected Shortfall computed from the same flawed model would understate the tail just as badly.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the halving as a red flag. Run the old and new models side by side, get the new one checked, and keep the old limit binding, cutting positions if needed, until the gap is explained.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The risk measure is one quantile from one model, so the whole gap between \"over\" and \"inside\" the limit is one modelling choice. Until you know why the number halved, the honest assumption is that the risk did not.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the new model, but ask the office to trim positions a little anyway, to be safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than nothing, but it skips the real question: is the new number right? A token cut leaves an unknown, possibly large, risk on the book.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> This was JPMorgan's Chief Investment Office, and the book later known as the London Whale. The positions stayed on. The bank's own task force later found the new model ran on spreadsheets filled in by copying and pasting, and that one formula divided by a sum where it should have divided by an average, which muted the volatility it measured. In May 2012 the bank disclosed the losses and went back to its old model, under which first-quarter VaR came out at roughly double the reported figure. Losses for the year passed $6 billion, a US Senate subcommittee investigated, and regulators in the US and the UK fined the bank.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/imo/media/doc/031314%20-%20PSI%20JPMorgan%20Whale%20Trades%20Hearing%20and%20Report.pdf\">US Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations, press release on the Whale Trades report, 14 Mar 2013</a>; Report of JPMorgan Chase &amp; Co. Management Task Force Regarding 2012 CIO Losses, 16 Jan 2013; JPMorgan Chase first-quarter 2012 Form 10-Q, 10 May 2012.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"cointegration","title":"Cointegration &amp; Statistical Arbitrage","date":"1998-08-24","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1998-08-24\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 24 August 1998</div> <h3>Your spread has a real leash, and it is stretching anyway</h3> <p>A week ago Russia devalued the rouble and defaulted on its domestic debt. You run convergence trades on spreads with real economic leashes, like the newest 30-year Treasury against a nearly identical older one. You are levered about 25 to 1. Investors are rushing into the most liquid bonds, so your spreads have stretched far beyond anything in the sample you sized them on, and your losses are mounting. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the trades. The z-scores are the most extreme the pair has ever shown.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The z-score is measured against a \\(\\sigma_\\epsilon\\) from years with no liquidity panic in them, so it understates how far the spread can go. Adding at 25 to 1 raises the chance that the spread outlasts your funding.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Re-run the Engle-Granger test. It still passes, so hold the full size.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The test says the spread is bounded over the long run. It says nothing about how far it can stretch this month, or whether your lenders will wait. Passing the test is not a reason to keep this leverage.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut the book to a size you could carry if the spread stretched as far again, and take the loss now.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This sizes for the stretch you have just learned is possible, not for the old sample. It locks in a painful loss, but it keeps you alive to collect the convergence if and when it comes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Halve the positions and wait for calmer markets.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A real step in the right direction. But half of 25 to 1 is still heavily levered against a spread that has already broken its historical range, and the forced selling around you can push it further.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Long-Term Capital Management kept most of its positions. Other arbitrage desks holding similar trades cut them, which widened the spreads further. LTCM lost about $4.6 billion in less than four months. On 23 September 1998 the Federal Reserve Bank of New York brought its main creditors together, and 14 banks and brokers put in about $3.6 billion to take over the portfolio and unwind it. The portfolio was unwound over the following year or so. Many of the spreads did narrow again later, but by then LTCM no longer held them.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.cftc.gov/sites/default/files/tm/tmhedgefundreport.htm\">President's Working Group on Financial Markets, <em>Hedge Funds, Leverage, and the Lessons of Long-Term Capital Management</em>, April 1999</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/boarddocs/testimony/1998/19981001.htm\">Alan Greenspan, testimony to the House Committee on Banking and Financial Services, 1 Oct 1998</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"collateral-margin-repo-markets","title":"Collateral, Margin &amp; Repo Markets","date":"2020-03-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 March 2020</div> <h3>Your \"safest\" trade needs cash today</h3> <p>You run a Treasury cash-futures basis trade: long Treasury bonds financed overnight in repo on a thin haircut, short the matching futures, with initial margin posted at the exchange. The dash for cash is on, and investors everywhere are selling even Treasuries. Volatility has jumped. The exchange has raised initial margin on Treasury futures, variation margin is due every day as prices swing, and your repo lenders want bigger haircuts or will lend less. Yesterday, Sunday, the Fed said it would buy Treasuries in size. What do you do this morning?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold everything. The trade is hedged and the bond and future must converge.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It is hedged against prices, not against funding. Margin and haircut calls must be met in cash today, whatever happens at expiry. A hedged trade you cannot fund is a trade someone else will close for you.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to it. Bonds have cheapened, so the gap should pay more when it converges.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A bigger position needs more margin and more haircut cash at exactly the moment both are rising. This is how a margin spiral turns a small gap into a forced sale.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut the position to a size you can fund under the new margins and haircuts, while the Fed's buying means there is a buyer for the bonds.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This sizes leverage to the haircut you face now, not the one you had. Selling into a market with a large buyer costs less than being forced to sell later into one without.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Meet the calls by posting other assets you hold as collateral.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It buys time, but those assets face bigger haircuts too in a volatility spike, and it ties up the last of your spare collateral. It delays the decision rather than making it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Funds running the basis trade had to raise cash for higher futures margin, daily variation margin and tighter repo terms on the same days, and the way to raise it was to sell the bonds. Dealers, with balance sheets already full, could not absorb the flow, and Treasury yields rose sharply in mid-March while stocks were falling. From 15 March the Fed bought Treasuries in enormous size: its holdings rose by more than a trillion dollars in about a month, from $2.52 trillion on 11 March to $3.63 trillion on 8 April. It also greatly expanded its repo operations. Economists at the Bank for International Settlements (Schrimpf, Shin and Sushko, 2020) called it a margin spiral.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200315a.htm\">Federal Reserve FOMC statement, 15 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.bis.org/publ/bisbull02.htm\">Schrimpf, Shin and Sushko, \"Leverage and margin spirals in fixed income markets during the Covid-19 crisis\", BIS Bulletin No 2, 2 Apr 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/TREAST\">Federal Reserve H.4.1, Treasury securities held outright (FRED: TREAST)</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"convex-optimization","title":"Convex Optimization","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Monday</div> <h3>\"Just add a max-10-names rule\"</h3> <p>You run a minimum-variance optimizer across a 60-name universe. It is a convex quadratic program with a no-short rule, and it solves to a guaranteed optimum in under a second. Your portfolio manager says the mandate now allows only 10 positions at once, and asks you to \"just add that as a constraint\" before the afternoon rebalance. What do you tell them?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">\"Easy. I'll add it as one more line and the solver will hand back the optimum as usual.\"<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">\"At most 10 nonzero weights\" is not a linear rule. It is a choice among about 75 billion subsets of 60 names, and the allowed set is no longer convex. The convex solver cannot even express it, and nothing guarantees that a local answer is the best one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">\"I'll cap every weight at 10%. That does the same job.\"<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A 10% cap is linear and keeps the problem convex, but it does the opposite job. With weights summing to 100%, a 10% cap forces you to hold <em>at least</em> 10 names, never at most 10.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">\"That rule breaks convexity. I'll pick 10 names with a greedy selection, then re-solve the convex problem on those 10. It's a strong candidate, not a proven optimum. If we need a better guarantee, a mixed-integer solver can run overnight.\"<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This keeps the convex solver as the engine, delivers on time, and is honest about what the answer is. It is the standard practitioner pattern from the lesson: heuristic selection on top, convex re-solve underneath.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">\"I'll add an \\(\\ell_1\\) penalty to push the portfolio towards fewer names.\"<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The \\(\\ell_1\\) relaxation keeps the problem convex and does encourage sparsity, so it is a sensible tool. But it does not force exactly 10 or fewer names; you would still have to tune it and check the count.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happens.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, built on the lesson's own scenario, not a specific historical event. The point it makes is general: a rule that limits <em>how big</em> weights are (caps, no shorting, budgets) keeps the problem a bowl; a rule that limits <em>how many</em> names you hold turns it into an egg carton. Spot which kind a request is before promising a turnaround.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"copulas","title":"Copulas — Modeling Dependence Beyond Correlation","date":"2007","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2007\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · early 2007</div> <h3>Your \"near riskless\" slice depends on one corner of the model</h3> <p>You run a bank's structured-credit book and hold a large super-senior CDO tranche. It loses money only if a large share of the mortgage-backed pool defaults at the same time. Your Gaussian copula, calibrated on calm years, marks it as near riskless. Two things bother you: tranches on the same pool imply different correlations for the junior and senior slices, and US house prices have stopped rising. What do you do with the position?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep it. The model says most of the pool defaulting together is close to impossible.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the zero-tail-dependence assumption talking. The super-senior slice is a bet on the joint lower tail and almost nothing else, and the Gaussian copula prices that corner at zero by construction.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-value it under a tail-dependent model and a nationwide house-price fall, and cut it to what you could survive in that stress.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The position's value depends on the one corner the model cannot see, and the tranche quotes already show the model misfits. Sizing to a stress where defaults cluster is the honest test.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Raise the copula correlation a little and re-mark the tranche.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A step in the right direction, but a Gaussian copula with any correlation below 1 still has zero tail dependence. One flat correlation could not even fit every tranche of a single pool at the same time.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy more. The spread is thin, but it is close to free money.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A thin spread on a slice that loses only in a joint crash is exactly the trade where a thin-tailed model hides the risk. More size means more exposure to the corner you have not measured.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> US house prices fell nationwide, a common shock to every region at once. Mortgage defaults rose together, the lower slices inside CDOs were wiped out together, and losses reached the super-senior slices the models had treated as safe. Rating agencies downgraded top-rated CDO tranches in large numbers through 2007 and 2008. UBS, Merrill Lynch and Citigroup took write-downs running to tens of billions of dollars, much of it on super-senior CDO positions they had kept, and the chief executives of Merrill Lynch and Citigroup left in late 2007.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/newsevents/news/banking/2008/SSG_Risk_Mgt_doc_final.pdf\">Senior Supervisors Group, <em>Observations on Risk Management Practices during the Recent Market Turbulence</em>, 6 Mar 2008</a>; UBS, Shareholder Report on UBS's Write-Downs, 18 Apr 2008; Merrill Lynch press release, 30 Oct 2007; Citigroup press release, 4 Nov 2007.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"cornish-fisher-expansion","title":"Cornish-Fisher Expansion","date":"2020-03-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 March 2020</div> <h3>Your skew-adjusted VaR keeps getting beaten</h3> <p>You run a desk's daily 99% VaR: mean and standard deviation from a one-year look-back, with a Cornish-Fisher nudge for the window's skew and kurtosis. The look-back year was calm, so the nudge has been small. Today the S&amp;P 500 fell about 12%, its worst day since October 1987, and the VIX closed above 82. Losses beyond your VaR are piling up far faster than 1 day in 100. What do you do with the model?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep it as it is. Cornish-Fisher already corrects for skew and fat tails.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It corrects for the skew and kurtosis in the sample you feed it. A calm year had almost none, so the corrected quantile still sits close to the Gaussian one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Re-estimate skew and kurtosis from the last week alone and plug them straight in.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Higher moments from a handful of days are dominated by one or two extreme observations. Extreme inputs can also push the expansion into the range where it is no longer a valid quantile, and the \"adjusted VaR\" becomes an artifact.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Report the exceptions honestly, treat the model as broken for this regime, and set risk off a number that already contains a stress period, cutting size to it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The correction can only fix the tail shape its window has seen. Measuring against a window that includes a real crisis, and sizing to that, deals with the actual problem instead of tuning the patch.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Shorten the look-back window to a month so the moments catch up faster.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It reacts to the new regime, which helps. But a month of data gives noisy skew and kurtosis, and the model will swing back to calm as soon as the window rolls past the crash.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Days of 5% and more, up and down, came one after another. Many bank trading desks that report a 99% daily VaR recorded exceptions far more often than 1 day in 100, clustered in a few weeks. Under the Basel rules, too many exceptions in a year raise the multiplier a bank applies to its VaR when it sets market-risk capital, so capital was about to rise in the middle of the crisis. In April 2020 the European Central Bank temporarily cut the part of that multiplier its supervisors set, for the banks it supervises, to offset the rise from the exceptions, citing the extraordinary market volatility.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bankingsupervision.europa.eu/press/pr/date/2020/html/ssm.pr200416~ecf270bca8.en.html\">ECB Banking Supervision press release, 16 Apr 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\">S&amp;P 500 close, 13 and 16 Mar 2020 (FRED: SP500)</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\">Cboe VIX close, 16 Mar 2020 (FRED: VIXCLS)</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"corporate-actions-series-continuity","title":"Corporate Actions &amp; Series Continuity","date":"2020-04-20","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-04-20\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 20 April 2020</div> <h3>The front-month oil contract has just settled below zero</h3> <p>You maintain a continuous WTI crude series for a trend-following backtest. It is ratio-adjusted and rolls at expiry. Today the May contract settled at −$37.63, after about $18 on Friday, while June is around $20. Your series is due to roll. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Ratio-adjust across the roll as usual.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The ratio is new price over old price, and the old price is negative. Every earlier price in the history gets multiplied by a negative number and flips sign.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the front-month series and compute today's percentage return.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">From about $18 to −$37.63 is a return of roughly −300%, which a simple long position in an asset can never reach. A percentage return through zero is meaningless.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Use a back-adjusted (Panama) series for this backtest, measure P&amp;L in dollars on the contract held, and record the roll rule alongside the results.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">For futures, dollar P&amp;L on the contract actually held is the honest unit, and it is exactly what the Panama series preserves. Writing down the roll rule lets anyone see which version of the day the backtest used.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch to a vendor series that rolls a few days early, so it never shows a negative price.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Fine if your strategy really rolls early, and many funds did. But it hides an event that hit anyone who held the front month into expiry, so know which rule you are using and why.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Lockdowns had cut oil demand and storage at Cushing, Oklahoma, the delivery point, was filling. The May contract's −$37.63 was the first negative settlement in its history, while June stayed positive. A retail oil product sold by Bank of China left its customers with heavy losses; the largest US oil ETF had already moved into later months. Depending on its roll rule, a continuous series shows that day as −$37.63, about $20, or sign-flipped.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.cftc.gov/PressRoom/PressReleases/8315-20\">CFTC press release on its interim staff report on the 20 April 2020 WTI price moves, 23 Nov 2020</a>; Caixin, on the regulator's fine of Bank of China over its crude oil product, 5 Dec 2020.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"correlation-networks-mst","title":"Correlation Networks &amp; Minimum Spanning Trees","date":"2020-03-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 March 2020</div> <h3>Your diversifier has stopped diversifying</h3> <p>You run a currency book across EUR, GBP, CHF and AUD, and you added JPY because a tree built on calm data showed it hanging far from the hub. It is Monday. Last night the Federal Reserve cut rates to zero. Funds facing margin calls are selling whatever has a bid, Treasuries and gold included, and the dollar is surging. The VIX will close at a record 82.69 today. What do you do with your tree?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trust it. JPY is the outlier in your tree, so your book is still diversified.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The tree was built on a window that described a market that no longer exists. When correlations rush toward 1, every distance shrinks toward 0, and the outlier moves into the clump. The calm tree is not the crisis tree.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Assume the distances have collapsed, treat the book as closer to one trade, and cut gross size until you can re-estimate on recent data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the transform says. If correlations go to 0.9, every distance falls to about 0.45, shorter than your tightest calm edge of 0.63. Five positions then behave like one large one, so the size that was right for five separate bets is too big.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Rebuild the tree on the full past year so the estimate is more stable.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A longer window is steadier, but it is steadier because it is mostly calm data. It will under-report how far correlations have converged this week. Better than nothing, but it answers the wrong question.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add Treasuries and gold as hedges, since they sit far from currencies and stocks in a calm tree.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">These are exactly the assets being sold for cash this week. Long Treasury yields were rising while equities fell, and gold was falling with stocks. A hedge chosen from a calm-window distance can fail together with the risk it hedges.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The dash for cash ran on through mid March. The S&amp;P 500 bottomed on 23 March, about a third below its February peak, the same day the Fed made its asset purchases open-ended, after large Treasury buying and wider dollar swap lines. Only after that did the old relationships start to come back. A book sized on its calm-window tree was far less diversified than it looked.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200315a.htm\">Federal Reserve FOMC statement, 15 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200323b.htm\">Federal Reserve press release on open-ended purchases, 23 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\">S&amp;P 500 close, 19 Feb and 23 Mar 2020 (FRED: SP500)</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\">Cboe VIX close, 16 Mar 2020 (FRED: VIXCLS)</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"covered-call-equals-short-put","title":"The Covered Call Is a Short Put","date":"2023-01","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2023-01\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · January 2023</div> <h3>\"It fell less last year, so it must be the safe one\"</h3> <p>The Nasdaq-100 has just fallen by roughly a third in 2022. A widely held Nasdaq-100 covered-call ETF, which owns the index and sells at-the-money index calls every month, fell too, but by less. A friend wants to move all of their Nasdaq savings into it: \"It held up better and it pays a big income, so it's the conservative choice.\" What do you tell them?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree. It fell less in a bad year, so it is the lower-risk way to hold the index.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It fell less only by the premiums collected. A covered call keeps the full downside, less one premium a month. It is not protected, only cushioned.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Suggest a put-writing fund instead, since puts look like they pay more premium.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">At the same strike and tenor, put-call parity makes that the same exposure, up to dividends and fees. It changes the label, not the payoff, so it does not answer the real question.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy both funds, to diversify between the call side and the put side.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Two doors into the same room. Holding both doubles one exposure; it does not spread it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Explain the trade: they keep almost the whole downside and sell every up-month above the strike. Only switch if they accept lagging badly in a strong rally.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the payoff says. An at-the-money call written each month hands over everything above that month's starting level. In a year that goes up most months, the fund keeps the premium and gives away the trend.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In 2023 the Nasdaq-100 rose more than 50%. Covered-call funds on the same index captured only part of that and trailed it by a wide margin. Both years are the payoff diagram played out month by month: cushioned on the way down, capped on the way up.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/NASDAQ100\">Nasdaq-100 index closes, 31 Dec 2021, 30 Dec 2022 and 29 Dec 2023 (FRED: NASDAQ100)</a>: −33.0% in 2022, +53.8% in 2023, price only.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"data-architecture-storage-versioning","title":"Data Architecture for Systematic Trading: Storage, Versioning &amp; Reproducibility","date":"2012-08-01","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2012-08-01\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 1 August 2012</div> <h3>New router code, eight servers, and error emails before the open</h3> <p>You run a large US equity market maker's automated order router. The New York Stock Exchange's new Retail Liquidity Program starts today, and new router code has been deployed for it. The router runs on eight servers. The new code reuses an old flag that once switched on a retired function. Before the open, automated emails start arriving that flag errors. The bell is minutes away. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Open on schedule. The deployment was checked on the servers you looked at.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">\"The servers you looked at\" is the gap. A copy you did not verify is a second version of the truth, and a reused flag means whatever the code on that copy says it means.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Hold the router back until every server is confirmed on the same code version and the reused flag means the same thing everywhere.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This treats the error emails as a version check failing. One authoritative version, verified on every copy before it trades, is the single-source-of-truth rule applied at market speed. Missing the first minutes of a new program costs little next to an unbounded order loop.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Open on schedule, and if anything misbehaves, roll every server back to the previous version.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A rollback of code alone is only safe if the meaning of every flag rolls back with it. Here the old code would read the new flag as \"run the retired function\", so a rollback spreads the fault instead of removing it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Open on schedule but with tight order-rate and position limits on the router.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Limits shrink the damage, which is worth a lot. But you would still be trading on a version mismatch you were warned about, and hoping the limits catch whatever it does.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Knight Capital opened on schedule. The new code had reached seven of eight servers; on the eighth, the reused flag still started the old Power Peg function, which could no longer track fills and kept sending orders. The error emails were not acted on. Staff then removed the new code from the seven good servers, which made all eight behave like the stale one. In about 45 minutes the router produced more than 4 million executions in 154 stocks. The loss was about $440 million by the firm's own estimate; the SEC's 2013 order put it at more than $460 million and fined Knight $12 million. Knight needed emergency outside capital within days and was later acquired.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf\">SEC order against Knight Capital Americas LLC, Release No. 34-70694, 16 Oct 2013</a>; <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/news/press-release/2013-222\">SEC press release 2013-222, 16 Oct 2013</a>; Knight Capital Group press release on the trading loss, 2 Aug 2012.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"data-cleaning-outliers-missing-data","title":"Data Cleaning: Outliers, Bad Ticks &amp; Missing Data","date":"2005-12-08","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2005-12-08\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 8 December 2005</div> <h3>Your filter lights up on a brand-new Tokyo listing</h3> <p>You run the cleaning pipeline for Japanese equities. J-Com listed on the Tokyo Stock Exchange's Mothers market this morning. A sell order for 610,000 shares at ¥1 has hit the market, with only about 14,500 shares in issue, and the price has collapsed to the daily limit. Your rolling MAD filter flags the whole session. What do you do with those prints?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Delete them as fat-finger ticks and forward-fill the last sensible price.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The error was in the order, not in the data. The prints record trades that happened. Deleting them, and forward-filling a calm price over a real crash, erases a real event and manufactures fake calm.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep the flag, keep the prints, and check them against the exchange's record of which trades stood or were cancelled.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Detection said \"unusual\"; only the venue's record says \"did this happen\". Keep what the venue kept, drop only what it voided, and log the decision.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Exclude the whole session from the research sample.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Honest, because it invents nothing. But it removes a real extreme event from the history your models learn from, and a strategy's behaviour on days like this is exactly what you need to see.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Interpolate between yesterday's close and tomorrow's open across the session.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It invents a smooth path through a session that was anything but, and it uses tomorrow's open, a value no live system would have had yet.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> A Mizuho Securities trader had meant to sell 1 share at ¥610,000. A flaw in the exchange's system stopped the cancellation going through, and the order kept filling. The trades stood; shares that did not exist were settled in cash. Mizuho's loss was roughly ¥40 billion, the exchange's president resigned within weeks, and in 2009 a Tokyo court ordered the exchange to pay Mizuho about ¥10.7 billion for the failed cancellation.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: Reuters, \"Tokyo Stock Exchange president and executives to resign\", 20 Dec 2005; NBC News (Associated Press), \"President, execs at Tokyo bourse resign\", Dec 2005; Reuters and Bloomberg reports of the Tokyo District Court ruling in Mizuho Securities v. Tokyo Stock Exchange, 24 Jun 2009 (the court put about 70% of the blame on the exchange).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"data-pipeline-raw-to-trading-ready","title":"The Data Pipeline: Raw Feeds to Trading-Ready Data","date":"2010-05-06","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2010-05-06\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 6 May 2010</div> <h3>Your backtest loves the Flash Crash afternoon</h3> <p>You are building one-minute bars for US stocks to backtest a buy-the-dip strategy. Your vendor's tape for 6 May 2010 shows Accenture trading at one cent in the afternoon, and the backtest books a spectacular profit buying it there. You know the venues later cancelled many trades from that afternoon. What do you do with the data?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the raw prints in the bars. They really printed, so they are real data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">They printed, and many were then cancelled. A bar built from them has a low no one could have traded at the size a backtest assumes. The profit is an artefact of the data, not of the strategy.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Delete the whole day from the dataset so it can't distort anything.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Removing a stressed day quietly makes the backtest look calmer than history was. The strategy's behaviour on a day like that is exactly what you need to see, on a version of the tape that reflects tradeable prices.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep the raw capture, apply the venues' cancellations as a separate, dated cleaning step, build bars from the corrected tape, and record which version the backtest used.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Capture first, correct in a logged step. You can serve either version, explain the difference, and the backtest no longer books fills at prices that were cancelled.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Swap in a vendor's already-corrected tape and discard the raw one.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The bars will be closer to tradeable, but you lose the record of what a live system would have seen that afternoon, and you can't audit how the correction was made.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> According to the joint SEC–CFTC staff report, a large fund's algorithm sold 75,000 E-mini S&amp;P 500 contracts, about $4.1 billion, into a thinning market. The Dow fell close to 1,000 points, about 9%, then recovered most of it in roughly half an hour. After the close the exchanges and FINRA cancelled over 20,000 trades in more than 300 securities that were more than 60% from each stock's 2:40 p.m. price. Single-stock circuit breakers and a ban on stub quotes followed later that year.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/news/studies/2010/marketevents-report.pdf\">CFTC and SEC staff, <em>Findings Regarding the Market Events of May 6, 2010</em>, 30 Sep 2010</a>; SEC and FINRA notices on the clearly erroneous trade cancellations, May 2010.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"decision-trees-random-forests","title":"Decision Trees &amp; Random Forests","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Monday</div> <h3>The forest looks brilliant on its own report card</h3> <p>You trained a random forest on years of daily FX rows: yesterday's realised volatility, some moving-average distances, a rate differential and a day-of-week dummy, predicting next-day direction. Its out-of-bag accuracy is well above a coin flip, and the feature-importance chart ranks volatility and one moving-average distance at the top. A colleague wants to start trading it next week. What do you do first?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade it. Out-of-bag error is an honest held-out estimate, so the edge is real.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Out-of-bag rows are only honest when rows are exchangeable. Daily market rows are not: neighbouring days share regimes, and a row-wise bootstrap lets a tree learn from the days either side of the day it is scored on. The lesson is explicit that OOB error is not a substitute for a time-aware split here.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Grow ten times as many trees first, to push the variance down further.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More trees only shrink the part of the variance above the correlation floor, and they do nothing about leakage or about whether the pattern exists out of sample. It is the wrong lever for the question being asked.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-test it with a purged, walk-forward split on later data, with costs, before any money goes in.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A well-made method and a real edge are separate claims. Only a time-aware, leak-free test on data the model never touched, net of costs, speaks to the second one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch to permutation importance to check which features really matter.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A good habit, since it is less biased than impurity-based importance. But it still describes what the model leaned on, not whether that reliance pays out of sample.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This day is illustrative, not a historical event. It is built from the lesson's own trading scenario and pitfalls: a forest's importances and out-of-bag score describe the model, and only a purged, walk-forward test with costs tells you whether the features carry an edge.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"deep-q-networks","title":"Deep Q-Networks &amp; Function Approximation","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research meeting</div> <h3>\"Sync the target network every step, it trains faster\"</h3> <p>A colleague's DQN chooses between buy, hold and sell from a state of thirty market features. Training swings wildly: the loss falls, then jumps, then falls again. They propose two changes to speed things up: copy the online weights into the target network after every gradient step, and shrink the replay buffer to the last few hundred transitions so it only holds \"fresh\" data. What do you say?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Do both. Fresher targets and fresher data should help.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Syncing every step removes the target network's whole purpose: the target would move every time the weights do. A tiny buffer is dominated by recent, near-identical transitions, so replay no longer decorrelates anything. Both changes bring back the instability the fixes exist to stop.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep a periodic sync and a large buffer sampled at random. If anything, sync less often, and check the reward and simulator before chasing speed.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The staleness of the target is the mechanism, not overhead. Random draws from a large buffer are what break the correlation. And a stable training curve still proves only that the network optimized this reward in this simulator.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the buffer but switch to prioritized replay, sampling the most surprising transitions more often.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A real refinement, but it changes the sampling distribution and needs importance weights to stay unbiased. It also leaves the every-step sync proposal unanswered, which is the bigger problem.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Drop the target network and use a larger learning rate to converge before the target drifts.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A bigger step makes the chase faster and wilder. With the target built from the same weights, each larger step moves the goalposts further, which tends to make the oscillation worse, not better.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this shows.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a real episode. The answer is the lesson's own pitfall list: skipping the target network \"because it trains faster\" and a buffer too small to decorrelate are the two standard ways to undo DQN's fixes.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"delta-hedging-gamma-scalping","title":"Delta Hedging &amp; Gamma Scalping","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative week (not a historical episode)</div> <h3>Five quiet days, and the option is bleeding</h3> <p>This is a made-up situation with computed numbers. You bought the lesson's 21-day at-the-money call at 20% implied ($2.30, stock $100) because you expect the stock to move more than 20% a year implies. You hedge once a day. Over the last five days the stock has moved only 0.4% a day, up, down, up, down, up, and is now $100.40. Rent has exceeded gamma gains, you are down about $0.26 a share on the hedged position, and implied volatility has also slipped from 20% to 18%, which costs a further $0.20 or so. Sixteen trading days remain. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Double the position so a single big move would make the money back.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Doubling doubles the rent as well as the gamma. The $0.26 already lost is gone whatever you do next, and chasing it turns a small bleed into a bigger bet on the same unproven view.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Stop hedging until the stock moves, to save trading costs.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">With delta still near 0.5 you are no longer hedged. You are running a directional position you did not mean to hold, and you give up exactly the buy-low, sell-high trades that make the strategy work. The cost saved is a few cents.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep hedging by your rule, and judge the trade on what you now expect over the remaining 16 days, against 18% implied.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The five quiet days are sunk. What matters is whether you still expect realised volatility to exceed what the option now prices. If yes, hold: the rent per day is the same and the price of the view is lower. If your forecast has changed, close it. Either way the decision comes from the forecast, not from the bleed.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Close it now to stop the bleed.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible if your forecast of volatility has come down. It is not defensible as a reaction to five quiet days alone: on a one-month trade, five quiet days are well within the normal spread of outcomes, as the simulation showed.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this shows.</strong> This is an illustrative scenario, not a recorded trade. The numbers come from repricing the option each day with the stated moves: five days of ±0.4% cost about $0.26 per share, and the lost two vol points are worth roughly $0.10 a point at 16 days. The point is the discipline. A hedged long-gamma position has a known daily cost and an uncertain payoff, so the useful questions are about the forecast and the size, not about the days already gone.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"disposition-effect","title":"Selling Winners, Holding Losers","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative sale day</div> <h3>You need $10,000 by Friday. Which one goes?</h3> <p>You need $10,000 in cash this week. Two holdings are each worth about $10,000 today. One is up 40% and you feel it has run its course. The other is down 30% and you feel it is due a bounce. Realised gains are taxed at 20%, and realised losses can offset gains elsewhere. What do you sell?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell the winner. It has done its job, and the loser just needs time.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the disposition effect in one decision. Your entry prices are choosing, not the stocks' futures. You also realise a taxable gain of about $2,857, around $571 of tax, while a usable loss sits idle.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Ask of each: if I had $10,000 in cash, would I buy this today? Sell the one you would not buy. If you would buy both equally, sell the loser for the tax offset.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The fresh-eyes test takes the entry price out of the decision. Only the future matters. When the two are a toss-up, tax breaks the tie: the loser's realised loss of about $4,286 can offset up to about $857 of tax at 20%.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Sell half of each, to be fair to both.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than selling only the winner, and it banks some of the tax offset. But splitting the difference still lets the entry prices vote instead of asking which holding you actually want to own.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Wait for the loser to get back to even, then sell it, and borrow for now.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the break-even effect from the previous lesson. \"Back to even\" is a reference point the market does not know about, and borrowing to wait for it adds cost and risk to a decision you are avoiding.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What tends to happen.</strong> This sale day is illustrative, not a real account. In Odean's 10,000 real accounts, the winners investors sold beat the losers they kept by about 3.4 percentage points over the following year, and the habit also paid tax that selling losers would have saved. The only month the pattern reversed was December, when a tax deadline made taking losses feel worthwhile.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"domino-chain-cross-asset","title":"The Domino Effect: One Shock Through the Whole Board","date":"2022-09-26","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-09-26\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 26 September 2022</div> <h3>Gilt yields are soaring, and sterling is falling</h3> <p>On Friday the UK government announced a \"mini-budget\" of large, unfunded tax cuts, without the usual independent fiscal forecast. Long-dated gilt yields are rising at a speed rarely seen in a major bond market. In early trading this Monday, sterling has touched an all-time low against the dollar. A colleague says: \"UK yields are now much higher than they were. Higher yields attract money, so sterling is a buy.\" What do you make of it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree and buy sterling for the higher yield. The rate link always wins in the end.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the textbook domino, and the screen is already telling you it has broken. Yields up with the currency down means the higher yield is a credibility premium. Money is leaving, not arriving.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Stay out of sterling and wait for the next scheduled policy meeting to make things clearer.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Staying out avoids the wrong trade, but it misses what the broken link is already saying. It also ignores the second risk: leveraged holders of gilts may be forced to sell, and that does not wait for a meeting.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Read the broken link as a credibility shock, do not buy the yield, and look for who is forced to sell gilts as yields rise.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the diagnostic use of the chain. A yield rise driven by doubt is a risk premium. And when collateral calls force sellers into the market, a chain can turn into a loop that only a buyer with no balance-sheet limit can stop.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Many UK pension schemes used liability-driven investment funds that hedged with leveraged gilt and swap positions. As yields jumped, they faced urgent collateral calls and sold gilts to raise cash, pushing yields up further. On 28 September the Bank of England announced temporary purchases of long-dated gilts on financial-stability grounds, explicitly to stop the spiral rather than to ease policy. The purchases ended on 14 October. The Chancellor was replaced that day, and the Prime Minister resigned on 20 October.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/news/2022/september/bank-of-england-announces-gilt-market-operation\">Bank of England, \"Bank of England announces gilt market operation\", 28 Sep 2022</a>; Bank of England Financial Policy Committee record, 5 Oct 2022 and Financial Stability Report, Dec 2022 (on the LDI collateral spiral); Reuters and BBC reports of sterling's record low against the dollar, 26 Sep 2022; UK government announcements of the Chancellor's replacement (14 Oct 2022) and the Prime Minister's resignation (20 Oct 2022).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"drawdowns-risk-of-ruin","title":"Drawdowns, Risk of Ruin, and Why Good Systems Get Abandoned","date":"2020-10-30","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-10-30\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 30 October 2020</div> <h3>Your value strategy is thirteen years under water</h3> <p>You run a systematic value strategy: buy cheap stocks, short expensive ones. The value premium was documented across decades and countries long before you started. But since around 2007 it has been in the longest and deepest drawdown in its recorded history, and the worst leg came in 2018 to 2020 as big growth and technology names kept outrunning everything cheap. Money is leaving value funds, and serious people are asking in print whether value is dead. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Shut it down. Thirteen years is long enough to prove the edge is gone.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is judging a system by its recent results, at what may be its low. A drawdown alone cannot tell a real premium from no edge at all, and closing near the bottom locks in the whole loss.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Move the money into the growth and technology stocks that have been winning.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Switching to what just worked is the classic way to buy a strategy's good run just as it may be ending. It also swaps a tested idea for a recent feeling.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Test the live record against the rule you wrote before the drawdown. If it has not tripped, keep running it at a size you can sit through.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's whole method. The decision rests on evidence stated in advance, not on how the last few years felt. If the size is too big to bear, the fix is a smaller bet, chosen calmly, not an exit at the low.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep it, but halve the size until it starts working again.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You survive, which matters. But cutting size because of pain, rather than a rule, means you hold less of it during any recovery. Size should be set from the drawdown you can bear before the bad stretch, not during it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In November 2020 the first effective COVID-19 vaccine results were announced, and value staged a sharp rebound that ran through 2021 and 2022. Investors who left near the bottom of the drawdown took the full loss and missed the recovery. That does not prove every drawdown recovers. It shows that a long, deep drawdown is exactly what a real premium can look like from the inside, which is why the decision has to come from a test, not from the drawdown.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://mba.tuck.dartmouth.edu/pages/faculty/ken.french/data_library.html\">Kenneth French Data Library, US HML value factor</a> (peak December 2006, about 58% below it by September 2020, the deepest fall in the series since 1926; +22% in 2021 and +32% in 2022); Pfizer and BioNTech press release, 9 Nov 2020.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"duration-convexity","title":"Duration &amp; Convexity","date":"2022-03-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-03-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 March 2022</div> <h3>The Fed has started hiking, and your bank owns a lot of duration</h3> <p>You run the securities book at a bank whose deposits roughly tripled in 2020 and 2021. Loans could not absorb the cash, so you bought long-dated Treasuries and agency mortgage bonds, much of them labelled held to maturity. Most deposits are above the insurance limit and come from a small, closely connected group of start-ups and funds. Today the Fed raised rates for the first time in this cycle, from near zero, and more hikes are expected. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. The held-to-maturity label keeps any fall in value out of earnings and capital.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The label changes the accounting, not the economics. Duration still says what the bonds are worth after the hikes, and the label only holds if you never have to sell. Your deposits decide that, not you.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy more long bonds. Yields are rising, so new purchases pay more.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Each new long bond adds duration just as rates start climbing. With an illustrative modified duration of 6, a 2.5-point rise in yields costs about 15% of the book on duration alone.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut the book's duration or hedge it now, sized in DV01, and stress-test what happens if a large share of deposits leaves at once.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This treats both sides. The hedge limits how far the assets fall; the stress test asks whether you could be forced to sell them. Duration measures the loss; the liability side decides whether it becomes real.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Hedge the DV01 you measure today, and leave it there.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than nothing, but the mortgage bonds have negative convexity. As rates rise, prepayments slow and their duration lengthens, so a hedge sized on today's DV01 covers less and less of the risk.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> This is Silicon Valley Bank's position. The Fed took its target range from near zero to 4.50–4.75% by February 2023. By the end of 2022 SVB's held-to-maturity securities were about $15 billion below book value, against about $16 billion of shareholders' equity. On 8 March 2023 it said it had sold about $21 billion of securities at a loss of about $1.8 billion and would raise capital. On 9 March depositors withdrew about $42 billion in a single day, and on 10 March regulators closed the bank.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220316a.htm\">Federal Reserve FOMC statement, 16 Mar 2022</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20230201a.htm\">FOMC statement, 1 Feb 2023</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/files/svb-review-20230428.pdf\">Federal Reserve, Review of the Supervision and Regulation of Silicon Valley Bank, 28 Apr 2023</a>; <a href=\"https://dfpi.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/337/2023/03/DFPI-Orders-Silicon-Valley-Bank-03102023.pdf\">California DFPI order taking possession of SVB, 10 Mar 2023</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"emh","title":"The Efficient Market Hypothesis","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research meeting</div> <h3>A strong anomaly, freshly published</h3> <p>A journal has just published a stock-return predictor with an impressive record over decades of data in the original study. A colleague wants to start trading it next week, sized on the paper's historical returns. What do you recommend?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade it at full size. Decades of significant returns cannot be luck.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The in-sample record is the most optimistic number you will ever see. Part of it is the luck of the original search, and once a predictor is published investors trade it. Sizing on the paper's returns oversizes the trade.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Assume much of the edge has gone, test it on data from after the study's sample, with costs, and ask what friction would let it survive.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the evidence and the logic of the lesson together. Returns on published predictors fell by about 58% on average after publication, and an edge needs a reason it is not arbitraged away. If it survives that test, size it on the out-of-sample result.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Trade it only in illiquid, hard-to-arbitrage stocks, where the effect held up better.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That matches McLean and Pontiff: predictor returns were higher where arbitrage is costly. But the same costs that protect the effect also eat into your returns, so it needs a cost-inclusive test before any money goes in.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Ignore it. Efficient markets mean no strategy can ever make money.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That misreads the hypothesis. It forbids a riskless free lunch, not every profit. On average published predictors lost a large share of their returns, not all of them, and a priced risk can still pay.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the research shows.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a dated event. The evidence behind the answer is real: McLean and Pontiff (<em>Journal of Finance</em>, 2016) followed 97 published predictors and found returns about 26% lower in the gap between the end of the study's sample and publication, and about 58% lower after publication. The decline was larger for the predictors with the biggest in-sample returns, and returns held up better where arbitrage is expensive.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"entropy-kl-mutual-information","title":"Entropy, KL Divergence &amp; Mutual Information","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research morning</div> <h3>A feature with \"no relationship\" to tomorrow's volatility</h3> <p>You are screening features to predict next-session realised volatility. A colleague's correlation matrix shows yesterday's <em>signed</em> return has a correlation of 0.02 with next-day volatility, and proposes dropping it. You know large up days and large down days both tend to come before busier sessions. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Drop it. A correlation of 0.02 means there is no relationship.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Correlation only rules out a straight-line link. A link that is symmetric around zero, big moves either way meaning more volatility, gives a correlation near zero for the same reason \\(Y = X^2\\) does. This throws away real information.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Estimate the mutual information between signed return and next-day volatility, then look at the shape, for example a binned average of volatility by return.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Mutual information picks up any dependence, linear or not. If it comes back clearly positive, the binned average shows the U shape, and you can then decide how to use the feature, for example through its absolute value.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Swap in the absolute return straight away and move on.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Probably the right feature in the end, but you have skipped the check. Without measuring the dependence first you are relying on a hunch, and you learn nothing about whether other features were wrongly dropped the same way.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Compute mutual information on the last three months of data and, if it is positive, start trading on it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Mutual information needs a lot of data. On a small sample a naive estimator can report a large value just from overfitting the joint density. And a dependence, even a real one, is not yet an edge after costs.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative case, not a dated episode. It is the lesson's own volatility-feature scenario: when volatility follows the size of a move rather than its sign, the signed return can show near-zero correlation with next-day volatility while its mutual information is clearly positive. The right first step is to measure the dependence with a tool that can see curves, and the right second step is to test whether it is stable and tradeable.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"event-trading-playbook","title":"The Event-Trading Playbook: Payrolls, CPI and Central Banks","date":"2022-11-10","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-11-10\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 10 November 2022</div> <h3>Long dollars into a CPI release, with an ordinary-day stop</h3> <p>It is 8:20 a.m. in New York. You are long US dollars against the euro and the yen, betting that October's CPI will be hot and push the Fed to keep hiking by 75 basis points. Your position is sized for an ordinary day, with stops about one ordinary day's move away. The consensus is about 0.6% for the month and 7.9% for the year. CPI has been the most market-moving release of the year. What do you do in the next ten minutes?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold. The stops limit the damage if you're wrong.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Your stops sit inside the move the options market is pricing for this release. A stop is an order to trade at the next available price, and in the first seconds after a big surprise there may be little liquidity between your level and where price lands. You have sized for a stop that may not fill where you set it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the position. Inflation has been running hot all year, so the number will be high.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is a view on the level, and the level is already in the consensus and the price. Only a hotter-than-7.9% print helps you. Adding doubles your exposure to a coin flip on the surprise, at the widest spreads of the day.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut the position to a size where a move of two or three event standard deviations is affordable, or go flat, and decide again after the number is out.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This sizes for the gap, not the stop. You keep the option to trade the second move once you know the surprise and spreads have normalised. If CPI is hot, you can re-enter with information; if it is soft, a small position limits the damage.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the position but buy options that pay if the dollar falls.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible: the protection caps the loss at its premium. But you are buying it at event vol, the most expensive moment, and you are still carrying a full-size bet on the surprise. Cutting size does the same job more cheaply.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> CPI rose 0.4% in October and 7.7% over the year; core rose 0.3% and 6.3%. All were below consensus. The 2-year Treasury yield fell from 4.62% to 4.32%, the dollar fell broadly, with the yen's biggest one-day gain against it since 2008, and the S&amp;P 500 rose 5.5%. A long-dollar position sized for an ordinary day took a move far larger than an ordinary day in the first minutes. The next month, the Fed slowed to a 50 basis-point hike.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"execution-algorithms","title":"Execution Algorithms: TWAP, VWAP, POV, IS","date":"2010-05-06","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2010-05-06\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 6 May 2010</div> <h3>A $4.1 billion hedge into a falling market</h3> <p>It is the afternoon of 6 May 2010. US markets are already falling on European debt worries. You execute for a large fund complex that has decided to hedge an equity position by selling 75,000 E-mini S&amp;P 500 futures, about \\$4.1 billion. How do you work the order?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Send it as one market order and get it done.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is maximum footprint. A \\$4.1 billion sale in one go walks straight through the book in a nervous market, and you pay the whole impact cost at once. It is the problem every schedule in this lesson exists to avoid.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">POV at 9% of the previous minute's volume, with no price or time limit.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It sounds patient, but it is only as patient as the market's volume. If intermediaries start passing your contracts back and forth, the volume you are following is partly your own selling, and the rule speeds up exactly when depth is vanishing.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Work it patiently over hours, taking price, time and volume into account: a participation cap and a price limit, with a person watching.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">On the urgency-versus-impact chart this trade sits in high impact concern, where a patient, footprint-minimizing schedule belongs. The price limit and cap stop the order from feeding on its own volume. The same trader had sold a similar order this way before.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">A VWAP schedule over the rest of the day, following the usual volume curve.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Patient and footprint-aware, which is the right instinct. But a pre-committed schedule with no price limit still sells into any air pocket, and today's volume is unlikely to look like a normal day's.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> According to the joint SEC/CFTC staff report, the order went to an algorithm set to sell 9% of the previous minute's volume \"without regard to price or time.\" High-frequency traders and other intermediaries bought the first contracts and then traded them among themselves: more than 27,000 contracts in 14 seconds for a net change of about 200. The rising volume made the algorithm sell faster, and the 75,000 contracts went through in about 20 minutes. A similar sale before, worked with a mix of manual and automated execution, had taken more than five hours. E-mini buy-side depth fell to less than 1% of its morning level, the contract fell more than 5% in about four and a half minutes, and an automatic five-second pause let buyers come back.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/news/studies/2010/marketevents-report.pdf\">CFTC and SEC staff report, Findings Regarding the Market Events of May 6, 2010, 30 Sep 2010</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"extreme-value-theory","title":"Extreme Value Theory &amp; Tail Risk","date":"1987-10-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1987-10-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · Friday 16 October 1987</div> <h3>A heavy week, and your risk model says relax</h3> <p>You run a leveraged long book in US stocks. It has been a week of heavy falls, and today the Dow fell about 4.6%. Your risk model is a normal distribution fitted to recent years, when daily index moves had a standard deviation of roughly 1%. On that model, a fall several times larger than today's is so unlikely it is not worth a thought. What do you do with the leverage over the weekend?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the leverage. The model says another big fall is vanishingly unlikely.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A normal curve has far too little mass in the tail, so it calls large losses impossible rather than rare. And extreme days cluster: a heavy week is exactly when the next extreme is most likely, not least.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the book. After a fall this large, a bounce is due.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A rare move is a measurement, not a forecast. It tells you the tail is live, which is a reason to hold less, not more.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut leverage until a loss far beyond anything in your sample is survivable, sizing off a heavy-tailed or stress loss rather than the normal VaR.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what EVT is for: a heavy tail makes a much worse day rare but possible, and sizing has to survive it. Accept that the extrapolated number is an assumption and leave a margin for it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Cut leverage a little, using the worst day in your own sample as the stress loss.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than trusting the normal, but historical VaR this far out just names your worst observed day. The next extreme need not respect it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On Monday 19 October 1987 the S&amp;P 500 fell about 20% and the Dow 22.6%, nearly doubling the Dow's falls of about 13% and 12% on 28 and 29 October 1929. Against daily moves of about 1%, that was on the order of twenty standard deviations: impossible under a normal model. The sessions after swung violently both ways, and the Dow regained 288 points, 57% of the Monday fall, within the next two sessions. The extremes came as a cluster around one event.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1987\">Federal Reserve History, \"Stock Market Crash of 1987\"</a> (Dow −4.6% on 16 Oct, −22.6% on 19 Oct, 288 points regained over the next two sessions); Report of the Presidential Task Force on Market Mechanisms (Brady Report), January 1988.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"factor-based-portfolio-construction","title":"Factor-Based Portfolio Construction","date":"2020-10-30","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-10-30\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · late October 2020</div> <h3>Your value tilt has lost for years. Do you keep it?</h3> <p>You run a multi-factor book with a deliberate value tilt, built exactly the way this lesson builds a target exposure. You have checked the loadings, and the book holds the tilt you solved for. But value has just been through its deepest drawdown on record, which began around 2007 and was at its worst from 2018 to 2020, and clients are asking why you still hold it. The committee meets today. What do you do with the tilt?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Cut the value tilt to zero. Years of losses prove the premium is gone.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The construction worked: you held exactly the exposure you asked for, so you earned exactly the factor's return. A long negative run is what a factor with a positive average can do. Cutting near the low turns a drawdown you sat through into a loss you lock in.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Swap the tilt for growth, because that is what has been working.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This sells the cheap factor after its worst run and buys the expensive side after its best. Nothing in the model says recent factor returns predict the next ones, and you would be paying the crowd that is already selling.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep the tilt, but at a size you could hold through a drawdown this long again, and tell clients that in advance.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The model promises the exposure, not the premium over your horizon. The right lever is size: a tilt small enough that years of underperformance do not force you out. That is the only way to still be holding the factor when it turns.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Halve the tilt and wait for value to show signs of life.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It lowers the pain, and a smaller tilt is easier to defend. But \"wait for signs of life\" means buying back after the rebound, which is exactly when the premium is paid. Better to choose a size you can hold and then hold it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 9 November 2020 Pfizer announced its vaccine trial results, and value had one of its sharpest one-day reversals in years. It went on to outperform through 2021 and 2022. Many investors had cut their value tilts near the bottom and missed the turn, and value managers such as AQR had already seen their assets fall substantially through the drawdown.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://mba.tuck.dartmouth.edu/pages/faculty/ken.french/data_library.html\">Kenneth French Data Library, US HML value factor</a> (+6.7% on 9 Nov 2020, its biggest daily gain since 1933; +22% in 2021, +32% in 2022; peak December 2006, about 58% below it by September 2020); Pfizer and BioNTech press release, 9 Nov 2020; Institutional Investor on AQR's assets, down from a peak of about $226 billion in 2018.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"feature-engineering-leakage-reproducibility","title":"Feature Engineering, Leakage &amp; Reproducibility","date":"2013-01","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2013-01\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · early 2013</div> <h3>A famous threshold, and a trade built on it</h3> <p>Since 2010, Reinhart and Rogoff's finding has been cited everywhere: advanced economies with public debt above 90% of GDP averaged about −0.1% a year growth. Your team wants to build a macro trade around countries crossing that line. The paper is by two highly respected economists in a top journal. What do you do before putting risk on it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use the number as it stands. The authors and the journal are about as credible as it gets.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Credibility is not a substitute for a rebuild. Nobody outside the authors had re-run the number from the raw data, and that is the only check that catches a mistake baked into the procedure.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Ask for the authors' spreadsheet, rerun it, and confirm you get −0.1%.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than nothing, and it does catch drift. But their spreadsheet gave −0.1% every time it was run. Reproducing a result with the same file confirms the file, not the answer.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Rebuild the number yourself from the raw country data, checking which countries and years are in and how each episode is weighted.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the independent rebuild the lesson argues for. It tests the choices as well as the arithmetic: which rows count, which years are dropped, and how a one-year episode weighs against a nineteen-year one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Search the data for the debt level where growth falls the most, and trade that threshold instead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This replaces one unchecked number with a data-mined one. Scanning for the best threshold is the multiple-testing trap, and it still rests on the same unverified dataset.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In 2013 Thomas Herndon, a graduate student at the University of Massachusetts Amherst, could not reproduce the result for a class assignment and obtained the spreadsheet. With Michael Ash and Robert Pollin he found an average that left out five countries, some excluded years, and equal weighting of episodes of very different length. Corrected, average growth above 90% debt was about 2.2%: slower than at lower debt, but no cliff. Reinhart and Rogoff acknowledged the spreadsheet error and defended their other choices.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.100.2.573\">Reinhart and Rogoff, \"Growth in a Time of Debt\", American Economic Review, May 2010</a>; <a href=\"https://peri.umass.edu/publication/item/526-does-high-public-debt-consistently-stifle-economic-growth-a-critique-of-reinhart-and-rogoff\">Herndon, Ash and Pollin, PERI Working Paper 322, April 2013</a>, later in the <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1093/cje/bet075\">Cambridge Journal of Economics</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"fed-corridor-iorb-srf-discount-window","title":"The Fed's Corridor: IORB, the SRF and the Discount Window","date":"2019-09-17","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2019-09-17\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 17 September 2019</div> <h3>Overnight repo has jumped far above the target range</h3> <p>It is the morning of 17 September 2019. The Fed's target range is 2.00–2.25%, but overnight repo rates have jumped far above it. Yesterday, the 16th, quarterly corporate tax payments fell due and a large settlement of new Treasury securities went through, both moving cash into the Treasury's account at the Fed. Reserves had already been shrinking for some time. There is no standing repo facility. The screens say \"funding crisis\". You hold a normal book of risk positions. How do you read it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Cut all risk now. A spike like this means something in the banking system is breaking.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The cause is visible on the calendar: a tax date and a big settlement drained cash on the same day. That is a plumbing squeeze, not a solvency scare. Selling everything on it treats a known drain as a hidden failure.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Ignore it. It is just the mid-month tax date, and it will settle by itself in a day or two.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The diagnosis is mostly right, but the context is not ordinary. With no standing ceiling and reserves already shrinking, nothing caps the rate automatically, so \"it will settle by itself\" is not guaranteed this time.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Read it as a calendar drain landing on a thin system with no ceiling. Treat it as a plumbing problem for the Fed to fix, and watch whether the Fed steps in and whether rates stay high after the tax date.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This names both the cause (tax date plus settlement) and the missing piece (no standing ceiling). The things worth watching are the Fed's response and persistence beyond the calendar, not the headline.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Read the jump in the overnight rate as a sign the Fed is about to tighten policy.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The Fed did not change its target range. The rate moved because cash was briefly in the wrong place, in the Treasury's account rather than the repo market. That is the corridor failing to hold, not a policy signal.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> SOFR printed 5.25% on 17 September against the 2.00–2.25% range, and the effective fed funds rate briefly moved above the top of its range. The New York Fed restarted overnight repo operations that same day, the first in about a decade, and kept offering them in the weeks that followed. In October the Fed began buying Treasury bills to rebuild reserves. The lasting fix came in July 2021, when the Standing Repo Facility made the ceiling permanent.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/what-happened-in-money-markets-in-september-2019-20200227.html\">Federal Reserve, FEDS Notes, \"What Happened in Money Markets in September 2019?\", 27 Feb 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://markets.newyorkfed.org/api/rates/secured/sofr/search.json?startDate=2019-09-13&amp;endDate=2019-09-19\">New York Fed SOFR data</a> (5.25% on 17 Sep 2019); <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20191011a.htm\">Federal Reserve statement on Treasury bill purchases, 11 Oct 2019</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20210728b.htm\">FOMC announcement of the standing repo facilities, 28 Jul 2021</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"fft-option-pricing-carr-madan","title":"FFT Option Pricing (Carr-Madan)","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative overnight run</div> <h3>The Heston calibration will not finish before the open</h3> <p>You recalibrate Heston's five parameters every night to the morning's implied-volatility surface: a few hundred quoted options across several expiries. The optimizer tries thousands of parameter sets, and at each one your pricer integrates the characteristic function strike by strike. Last night's run was still going when the desk needed the fit. What do you change?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Switch the inner pricer to Carr-Madan: one FFT per expiry and parameter set, then check its prices against the direct integration at a handful of strikes after tuning the damping factor and grid spacing.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This removes the bottleneck, one integration per strike, without changing the model. The check matters because a badly chosen damping factor or grid gives fast, wrong prices.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Fit only 20 strikes per expiry so the direct integration finishes in time.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It finishes, but the model is now fitted to a sketch of the smile. The wings you dropped are often exactly where the trades are, and the cost problem returns the moment you add strikes back.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Loosen the optimizer's stopping rule so it tries fewer parameter sets.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It cuts the run time, but it attacks the wrong factor. You may stop before the fit converges, and each repricing is still one integration per strike.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Also move the local-volatility book onto the FFT pricer while you are at it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Carr-Madan needs a closed-form characteristic function to feed in. Many local-volatility models have none, so there is nothing to transform; they stay on Monte Carlo or a PDE solver.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happens.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's own overnight-calibration scenario, not a specific historical event. The documented point is general: since Carr and Madan (1999), FFT-style pricing, and later methods such as COS (2008), is how characteristic-function models are calibrated fast enough to refit routinely.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"fokker-planck-equation","title":"The Fokker-Planck Equation","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative risk meeting</div> <h3>Risk wants a six-month distribution, and the pricer is right there</h3> <p>Your risk team asks for the full distribution of where a currency pair could sit in six months, under the same drift and volatility your option pricer uses. A colleague suggests reusing the pricer's PDE solver, which marches the Black-Scholes equation backward from a payoff at expiry. The deadline is tonight. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Reuse the pricer as it is, with the payoff set to the spot level at six months.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The pricer solves a backward problem: a terminal condition and the plain generator. It returns today's value of one payoff, not a distribution. It will run cleanly and give a stable answer to the wrong question.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Run the forward equation: start from a spike at today's spot and march the Fokker-Planck equation six months ahead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is exactly the forward question: a known start, an unknown spread of outcomes. Same drift, same noise, but the adjoint operator and an initial condition, which is what a distribution needs.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Simulate many paths from today's spot and build a histogram of where they end.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A valid estimate of the same density, and often what risk systems actually do. It is noisier and slower to converge than a direct forward solve, so check that you ran enough paths.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Read the distribution off the pricer's grid at today's date.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The pricer's grid holds option values for each possible spot, not probabilities. Those numbers do not sum to one and are not a density of anything.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The lesson.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a dated event. The check that catches the mistake is the lesson's one-sentence rule: \"given where I start, where might I end up\" is forward; \"given what I want at the end, what is it worth now\" is backward. A backward solver used for a forward question does not crash. It quietly computes the wrong quantity.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"foundation-algebra-functions","title":"Algebra &amp; Functions Refresher","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative account check</div> <h3>How much of your own money does the position need?</h3> <p>You want a position of 10,000 units of an instrument priced at \\$1.20. Your broker allows 20:1 leverage, so each \\$1 of your own money controls \\$20 of position. How much equity do you need in the account?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"good\">\\$600.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The position is worth 10,000 × \\$1.20 = \\$12,000. Leverage lets each \\$1 control \\$20, so divide: \\$12,000 ÷ 20 = \\$600. Check by running it forwards: \\$600 × 20 ÷ \\$1.20 = 10,000 units.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">\\$12,000, to be safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the full value of the position, with no leverage at all. It is a careful choice, but it answers a different question from the one asked.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">\\$240,000.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This multiplies by the leverage instead of dividing. Leverage means you need less of your own money, not more. Undo \"times 20\" with \"divide by 20\".</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">\\$500.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is 10,000 ÷ 20. It forgets the price, so it treats each unit as costing \\$1 instead of \\$1.20.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>How it works out.</strong> This is an illustrative example using the numbers from this lesson's trading scenario, not a real account. The answer is \\$600. The safe habit is the one from the whole lesson: after solving, put your answer back into the original formula and check it gives the number you started with.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"foundation-complex-numbers","title":"Complex Numbers","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative spreadsheet check</div> <h3>The angle that came out on the wrong side</h3> <p>You need the argument of \\(z = -3 - 4i\\). A spreadsheet works out the angle as \\(\\tan^{-1}(b/a)\\). Since \\(-4/-3 = 4/3\\), it reports 53.13°. A colleague wants to use that number. What do you say?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use 53.13°. The formula came straight from the lesson.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The ratio \\(-4/-3\\) is the same as \\(4/3\\), so the formula cannot tell \\(-3 - 4i\\) from \\(3 + 4i\\). 53.13° points up and to the right, but this number sits down and to the left.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Check the signs first. Both parts are negative, so the point is in the third quadrant: 53.13° + 180° = 233.13°. Or use a two-argument arctangent, which handles this itself.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This places the point correctly. Check it: \\(5\\cos 233.13° = -3\\) and \\(5\\sin 233.13° = -4\\), the number you started with.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Sketch it on the plane and read off \"about 230°\".<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The sketch puts it in the right place, which is the most important check. But a drawing only gives a rough angle. Use it as the check and the two-argument arctangent for the number.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Skip the angle and just use the distance, 5.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The distance alone describes a whole circle of points. Without the angle you cannot tell \\(-3 - 4i\\) from \\(3 + 4i\\), or from any other point 5 away.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>How it works out.</strong> This is an illustrative check, not a real event. The correct argument is 233.13° (the same direction as −126.87°). This is the quadrant pitfall from this lesson, and it fails silently: no error appears, just a number on the wrong side of the circle. The habit that catches it is the one used throughout: convert back and see whether you get the number you started with.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"foundation-multivariable-calculus","title":"Partial Derivatives &amp; Multivariable Calculus","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative risk screen</div> <h3>Spot and volatility move together. Where did the money come from?</h3> <p>Your option position shows Delta 0.60, Gamma 0.04, Vega 0.12 per volatility point and Theta −0.03 per day. Overnight, spot rises by 2 and implied volatility rises by 5 points. Your manager asks how much the position made, and why. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use Delta: 0.60 × 2 = +1.20.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Delta is the slope with volatility and time frozen. Here volatility did not stand still, so Delta alone misses part of the move.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Add up each input's own contribution: Delta 0.60 × 2 = +1.20, Gamma ½ × 0.04 × 2² = +0.08, Vega 0.12 × 5 = +0.60, Theta −0.03 for the day. About +1.85 in total.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Each Greek is one partial derivative, so each input's move is paid for by its own slope, and the contributions add. You get the total and a clear answer to \"why\".</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Reprice the option from scratch with the new spot, volatility and date.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That gives an accurate total, and for big moves it is the better number. But it does not say how much came from spot and how much from volatility, which is what was asked. Use it to check the sum.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Average the four Greeks and multiply by the size of the move.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The Greeks measure sensitivity to different inputs, in different units. Averaging them mixes up dials that have nothing to do with each other.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>How it works out.</strong> This is an illustrative screen using the Greek values quoted in this lesson, with units added for the example; it is not a real position. Delta alone says +1.20; the sum of the partials says about +1.85, and a third of that came from volatility. This is the multivariable chain rule at work: when several inputs move, add the contribution through every path.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"foundation-optimization-basics","title":"Optimization Basics: Unconstrained &amp; Constrained","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Monday</div> <h3>The optimiser says: buy more than you have</h3> <p>You run the two-asset portfolio from this lesson. Asset 1 expects 8% with 20% volatility, Asset 2 expects 4% with 10%, and their correlation is 0.3. Your client now wants a 10% target return. You type 10 into the calculator above. It says: put 150% of the money in Asset 1 and sell short 50% of Asset 2. Volatility comes out at 28.9%. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Put it on. The maths is exact, so the answer is optimal.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It is exact for the inputs, not for the world. A 10% target is above the best asset's own 8%, so the only way there is borrowing plus a short sale, with more risk than either asset alone. And the 8% is only an estimate: if it is a little too high, the whole plan rests on a number that is not there.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Show the client that 10% needs leverage, a short and 28.9% risk, and offer a lower target or a no-short rule.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This reads the optimiser honestly. A no-short rule is an inequality fence: it caps Asset 1 at 100%, so the most this pair can deliver is 8% at 20% risk. Making the fence visible lets the client choose the trade-off with open eyes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Cap Asset 1 at 100% and quietly deliver 8%.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safer, and the cap is a sensible rule. But it misses the target without saying so. The client should know that 10% cannot be reached without leverage.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Lower the correlation estimate until the risk looks acceptable.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That bends the input to get the answer you want. The optimiser will obey, and the real risk will not change at all.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What to take from it.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's own two assets, not a historical event. The numbers check out in the calculator above: a 10% target gives \\(w_1 = 1.500\\), a short-position warning and \\(\\sigma_p = 28.90\\%\\). A constrained optimiser always obeys its fences and its inputs. Your job is to check both.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"foundation-trig-logs-exponentials","title":"Trigonometry, Logarithms &amp; Exponentials","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative fund report</div> <h3>\"Up 50%, then down 50%. So we broke even?\"</h3> <p>A small fund made +50% in its first year and −50% in its second. A colleague drafts the report: \"Two years, average return 0%, so investors are back where they started.\" You are asked to check the two-year return. What do you write?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">0%. Add +50% and −50%.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Simple returns don't add. The −50% is a percentage of a bigger amount than the +50% was, so the loss is bigger than the gain.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Down 25%. Chain the years properly: 1.5 × 0.5 = 0.75, or add the log-returns, ln 1.5 + ln 0.5 = −0.288, and convert back, e<sup>−0.288</sup> − 1 = −25%.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Both routes give the same exact answer. \\$100 became \\$150, then \\$75. Log-returns can be added; simple returns have to be multiplied.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Add the simple returns anyway, because over small moves the error is tiny.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That shortcut is close for small daily moves, which is why it survives. With moves of 50% it is badly wrong: it says 0% when the truth is −25%.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>How it works out.</strong> This is an illustrative report, not a real fund. \\$100 grows to \\$150 and then falls to \\$75, a loss of 25% over the two years. The bigger the swings, the further simple-return arithmetic drifts from the truth. Log-returns add exactly, whatever the size of the moves.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"four-markets-four-questions","title":"Four Markets, Four Questions","date":"2020-03","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · mid-March 2020</div> <h3>Stocks are crashing and Treasury yields are rising</h3> <p>It is mid-March 2020. Stocks are falling hard, and on Monday 16 March the VIX closed at a record 82.69. But for several days the 10-year Treasury yield has been rising while stocks fall, and the dollar is surging against almost every currency. You run a balanced book: equities hedged with Treasuries. A colleague says rising yields mean the market expects a fast recovery. What is your read?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Rising yields mean growth optimism. Add to equities.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The other witnesses all say panic: mood is terrible and uncertainty is at a record. Treasuries being sold alongside stocks is not optimism. It says someone is being forced to raise cash from everything they own.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Read the disagreement as a scramble for cash. Expect the Treasury hedge to fail for now, cut gross size, and watch for a liquidity response from the central bank.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The haven not acting like one, plus a dollar bid against everything, is the signature of a liquidity squeeze. Your hedge and your risk are falling together, so the only lever left is size. The fix for this kind of crisis is liquidity, so that is the news to watch for.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy more Treasuries. Yields are higher, so the haven is on sale.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It may pay over a long horizon, but it treats this as ordinary risk-off. While dealers cannot absorb the selling, the haven can keep falling. Defensible for a patient holder, not as a hedge this week.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The VIX is at a record, so the bottom is in. Buy everything.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The VIX answers how big, never which way. A record reading says the coming moves will be huge in either direction. It is not a bottom signal.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The disagreement was the most important reading on the board: a scramble for cash and dollars so severe that even Treasuries were sold to raise it. On 15 March the Federal Reserve had cut rates to near zero, announced large purchases of Treasuries and mortgage securities, and enhanced its dollar swap lines. Within days the swap lines were extended to more central banks, and on 23 March the purchases were made open-ended. The S&amp;P 500's closing low of the crash came on 23 March. The bond market did not predict the bottom. It identified a liquidity problem, which needed a liquidity fix.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200315a.htm\">Federal Reserve FOMC statement, 15 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200315c.htm\">Federal Reserve, coordinated swap-line action, 15 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200319b.htm\">Federal Reserve, swap lines with nine more central banks, 19 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200323b.htm\">Federal Reserve, open-ended purchases and new facilities, 23 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://cdn.cboe.com/api/global/us_indices/daily_prices/VIX_History.csv\">Cboe VIX daily history</a> (16 Mar 2020 close 82.69); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\">S&amp;P 500 daily closes (FRED, SP500)</a> (closing low 2,237.40 on 23 Mar 2020).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"fourier-analysis-frequency-domain","title":"Fourier Analysis &amp; the Frequency Domain","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative afternoon</div> <h3>A monthly cycle in EUR/USD returns?</h3> <p>A colleague computes one raw periodogram of two years of EUR/USD daily returns. Most bars are low and jagged, but one bar, at a period of about a month, stands clearly above the rest. They want to build a trade around the monthly cycle. What do you suggest?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade it. It is the tallest bar in the spectrum.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Each raw periodogram bar is roughly chi-squared with 2 degrees of freedom: very noisy on its own. Among dozens of bars, one will always stand tallest, cycle or not. The tallest bar is not yet evidence.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Split the data into segments, average their periodograms, and see whether the spike survives, keeping in mind that raw return spectra are usually close to flat.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Averaging across segments (Bartlett or Welch style) is the standard way to trade resolution for precision. A real cycle survives it; a noise spike melts. And the prior is against a cycle in raw returns: the classic finding is a near-flat spectrum.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Get two more years of data and recompute the raw periodogram to sharpen the spike.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More data gives you more, finer-spaced bars, not a less noisy bar. The raw periodogram is not a consistent estimator: each ordinate stays just as noisy however long the sample.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Drop it, and drop spectral analysis for FX altogether.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Right about this spike being weak evidence, but the tool is still useful on other FX series. Volume and volatility have real calendar cycles, such as the intraday pattern around the session opens, and a periodogram is well suited to those.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative case, not a dated episode. The lesson's honest finding is that raw FX and equity return periodograms tend to be close to flat, going back to Granger and Morgenstern's spectral studies of stock prices in 1963, and that a single unsmoothed spike is a hint, not a confirmed cycle. Smooth or average first; then, even if a cycle survives, it still has to become a tested rule before it is a signal.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"fractional-brownian-motion","title":"Fractional Brownian Motion &amp; Long Memory","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research week</div> <h3>The volatility residual that will not die</h3> <p>You model a currency pair's realized volatility with GARCH(1,1). The fit looks fine at short lags, but the residual autocorrelation is still small and clearly positive at lags of 60 trading days and beyond, well past what the GARCH persistence can explain. What is your next step?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add more lags: move to a higher-order GARCH or ARMA until the residuals look clean.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More lags only change the rate of an exponential decay; they never make it a power law. Any finite-order model's correlations still add up to a finite total, so this cannot fix true long memory. You would overfit and still miss it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Check for structural breaks or regime shifts first; if the slow decay survives that, fit a long-memory model such as ARFIMA or FIGARCH and validate it out of sample.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Slow decay can be faked by stitched-together regimes, so rule that out first. If it is real, the right fix is a different kind of process, not a bigger one, and any longer-horizon forecast still has to earn trust out of sample.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Fit ARFIMA straight away. The fitted d comes out near 0.35, so H is about 0.85: trust the longer-horizon forecasts.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Probably the right model family, but you skipped two checks: regime breaks can mimic long memory, and a better in-sample fit is not yet evidence that the longer forecasts make money.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Conclude that the currency's returns are predictable over two-month horizons and trade the direction.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The memory is in volatility, not in the direction of returns. Lo's 1991 test found little long memory in returns once ordinary short-term dependence is allowed for.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happens.</strong> This is an illustrative situation based on the lesson's own scenario, not a dated episode. The pattern itself is well documented: absolute returns and realized volatility stay correlated over very long lags (Ding, Granger &amp; Engle, 1993), which is why long-memory volatility models such as FIGARCH were built. Whether trading on them pays is a separate, out-of-sample question.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"fx-fixings-wm-reuters","title":"FX Fixings &amp; the WM/Reuters 4pm Fix","date":"2014-11-12","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2014-11-12\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 12 November 2014</div> <h3>A chat message the day the fines landed</h3> <p>You run the G10 spot desk at a bank. For tomorrow's 4pm fix you hold a client order to buy €400m and another client's order to sell €150m, both at the fix plus an agreed fee. A trader you have known for years at another bank messages you in a chat group: \"Big euro buyer at the fix tomorrow? Me too. Pass yours to me and I'll make sure it goes our way.\" What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Tell him your net and agree to pool the orders so one of you dominates the window.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the conduct regulators fined: sharing client order information between banks and coordinating to move a fix. With a median, pooling orders is exactly what makes a push possible. It is also how the US criminal case against four banks was built.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Say nothing, but buy an extra €250m of your own just before the window, then sell it back during the window.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Trading more than the client's interest just before the window to inflate the price against the client is a listed unacceptable practice. Doing it alone instead of with a friend does not change what it is.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Decline to discuss client business, cross the €150m internally and work the €250m residual through the window under your documented procedure.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the FSB's September 2014 recommendations ask for, and what the FX Global Code later wrote down: no sharing of flows, netting client interest, executing the net amount in a way meant to track the fix, paid by a transparent fee.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Stop accepting fixing orders at all.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You can't misuse what you don't hold, so it is safe. But clients still need the service, and the FSB chose not to ban fix trading for that reason. Handling the orders properly serves them better than refusing them.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> That day the FCA fined Citibank, HSBC, JPMorgan Chase, RBS and UBS a combined £1.11bn, after finding that traders in groups with names like \"the players\" and \"the A-team\" had shared client order information and tried to manipulate the 4pm WM/Reuters and ECB fixes. The CFTC fined the same five banks $1.475bn, the OCC fined Bank of America, Citibank and JPMorgan $950m, and FINMA ordered UBS to pay CHF 134m: about $4.3bn in total. On 20 May 2015 Citicorp, JPMorgan, Barclays and RBS agreed to plead guilty in the US over a euro-dollar chat room whose members called themselves \"The Cartel\", and the Federal Reserve fined six banks more than $1.8bn.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"fx-options-quoting-rr-bf","title":"FX Options Quoting: Delta, Risk Reversals &amp; Butterflies","date":"2016-06-10","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2016-06-10\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 10 June 2016</div> <h3>Sterling insurance has never looked so expensive</h3> <p>You run a US fund holding £50 million of UK assets, currency unhedged. The referendum is on 23 June. GBP/USD is near 1.4390, and the one-month 25-delta risk reversal is about −7.9: sterling puts cost about eight vols more than equivalent calls. A colleague says: \"The puts are absurdly rich. Let's sell some and collect the premium.\" What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell 25-delta GBP puts to harvest the rich skew.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You already own the downside through the £50 million of assets. Selling puts doubles that bet, and the skew is rich precisely because the market sees a real chance of a large, fast fall. You would be paid well for standing in the road.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Hedge some or all of the sterling with forwards, which carry no implied vol, and buy options only for the part where you want to keep the upside.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The risk reversal says option protection on this side is expensive, not that you should go unprotected. A forward hedge costs nothing in vol terms; the price is giving up the rally if Remain wins. Using options only where you need the optionality keeps the skew bill small.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy 25-delta GBP puts on the whole £50 million.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It protects you, and it keeps the upside, but you pay the richest vol on the screen for all of it. Defensible for a fund that cannot give up the upside; expensive for one that can.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Do nothing. A −7.9 risk reversal means the market is too bearish, so sterling should rally.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The risk reversal is a price of insurance, not a forecast, and reading it as a contrarian signal is still a forecast. Staying unhedged into a binary event is a directional bet with the fund's whole sterling position.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Sterling first rallied, to 1.4877 by the close on polling day, as polls leaned towards Remain. Then Leave won. On 24 June GBP/USD closed at 1.3622, down about 8.4%, after trading briefly around 1.32 to 1.33, its weakest since 1985. Forward hedges locked in rates above 1.40; puts paid out; anyone who had sold sterling puts on top of a long sterling book lost twice. Figures from Clark and Amen (2017) and contemporary market reports.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"gamma-exposure-dealer-hedging","title":"Gamma Exposure &amp; Dealer Hedging Flows","date":"2018-02-05","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2018-02-05\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 5 February 2018</div> <h3>You own an inverse-VIX product, and the VIX is soaring</h3> <p>It is the afternoon of Monday 5 February 2018. You hold an inverse-VIX exchange-traded product that paid steadily through the calm of 2017. After Friday's 2% drop, the S&amp;P 500 is falling hard again and the VIX is soaring from Friday's close of 17.31. You know the product rebalances every day near the close: after a rise in VIX futures, its rule makes it buy them back. What do you do before the close?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold. It has always come back, and the daily rebalance is just mechanical noise.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The rebalance is the problem, not noise. A −1× rule must buy about twice the day's move in futures, the same sign as the move and bigger the bigger it is. Every similar product does the same in the same window, so the close is likely to make things worse.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy more. A VIX this high has to fall back, so this is the cheap entry.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Volatility may well mean-revert later, but you have to survive the path first. You would be adding to a position whose own rulebook is short gamma, on the day that rule is forced to run at full size.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut the position before the close, because the product is short gamma by its own rule and that buying is known in advance.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's sign logic applied to a rulebook. Same-sign hedging into one visible window is the amplifying case, and nothing about it is hidden. Getting out before the forced buying lands takes the loss you can see instead of the one you can't size.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep it, but buy some VIX calls as a hedge.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It caps part of the damage, which is better than doing nothing. But you are buying protection after the VIX has already jumped, and you still hold a product that can lose almost everything in one session.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The S&amp;P 500 fell 4.1% and the VIX closed at 37.32, up about 115% in a day, the biggest one-day percentage jump in its history. VIX futures jumped again after the stock market closed, which is when the products' rebalancing purchases were being made. XIV's indicative value fell by more than 90%, enough to trigger a clause letting Credit Suisse redeem the note, and the bank announced the next day that it would. SVXY also lost most of its value, and ProShares later cut its target to −0.5×, effective at the close on 27 February 2018.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bis.org/publ/qtrpdf/r_qt1803t.htm\">BIS Quarterly Review, March 2018, “The equity market turbulence of 5 February: the role of exchange-traded volatility products”</a>; Credit Suisse media release, 6 Feb 2018 (XIV acceleration event); <a href=\"https://www.proshares.com/funds/svxy.html\">ProShares SVXY fund page (objective change, 27 Feb 2018)</a>; <a href=\"https://cdn.cboe.com/api/global/us_indices/daily_prices/VIX_History.csv\">Cboe VIX daily history</a> (17.31 on 2 Feb, 37.32 on 5 Feb 2018); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\">S&amp;P 500 daily closes (FRED, SP500)</a> (−4.1% on 5 Feb 2018).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"garch-volatility","title":"GARCH &amp; Volatility Clustering","date":"2020-03-12","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03-12\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 12 March 2020</div> <h3>The S&amp;P has just fallen 9.5%. How big is tomorrow?</h3> <p>It is the close on 12 March 2020. The S&amp;P 500 fell about 7.6% on Monday and 9.5% today, and the market-wide circuit breaker has halted trading on both days. Three weeks ago the index closed at a record high with the VIX in the mid-teens. You size a position so that its expected daily swing in dollars stays fixed, and you need tomorrow's volatility number. Which do you use?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The volatility you fitted on the calm months before February. That is the market's normal level.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the long-run level, and today is nowhere near it. Clustering says yesterday's storm is the best guide to tomorrow's. Sizing off calm-period volatility leaves you several times too big in the wildest days.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">A GARCH-style forecast updated with today's close, which has jumped with the two huge days, and cut the size to match.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the model is for. Each big day enters squared, so the forecast is now far above February's, and a fixed-risk position has to shrink. It also lets the forecast fade gradually rather than snap back.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the size and go long. After a 9.5% fall, a bounce is due.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">GARCH says nothing about direction. A bounce and a further fall are both big days, and big days are exactly what the model expects next. Betting on the sign with full size leaves you exposed to either.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">A 20-day average of squared returns, which has risen with the last two days.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than the calm number, and it does see the shock. But it gives the two huge days only one twentieth of the weight each, so it under-reacts now, and it will drop off a cliff three weeks later when they leave the window.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The next day the S&amp;P 500 rose 9.3%. On 16 March it fell about 12%, the circuit breaker tripped again, and the VIX closed at 82.69, a record. The breaker was triggered once more on 18 March, and the index rose more than 9% on 24 March. Big days kept coming in both directions, so the up days raised the forecast as much as the down days. Volatility stayed raised for months after the worst had passed.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\">S&amp;P 500 daily closes (FRED, SP500)</a> (−7.6% on 9 Mar, −9.5% on 12 Mar, +9.3% on 13 Mar, −12.0% on 16 Mar, +9.4% on 24 Mar 2020; record close 19 Feb 2020); <a href=\"https://cdn.cboe.com/api/global/us_indices/daily_prices/VIX_History.csv\">Cboe VIX daily history</a> (14.38 on 19 Feb, 82.69 on 16 Mar 2020); Market-Wide Circuit Breaker Working Group, report to the SEC, 2021 (the four halts of 9, 12, 16 and 18 March 2020).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"girsanov-risk-neutral","title":"Girsanov's Theorem &amp; the Risk-Neutral Measure","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative morning</div> <h3>You are bullish EUR/USD and a client wants a call</h3> <p>Your own macro read says EUR/USD will rise over the next six months by more than the interest-rate differential implies. A client asks you to quote a six-month EUR/USD call, and you will hedge whatever you sell. How do you price it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Price it with your own expected drift. You are confident in the view, so the call is worth more.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That swaps the risk-free drift for your real-world drift inside the price. You quote too high against anyone who prices under the risk-neutral measure, and the delta you hedge with is wrong, because it no longer matches what a replicating portfolio needs.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Shade it a little: blend part of your view into the drift, not all of it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Still a mispriced number, only smaller. A desk whose house view leaks into its pricing misprices consistently in the direction of that view, and counterparties who hedge correctly pick it off.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Price and hedge the call at the rate differential, like everyone else, and put the view on as a separate trade: buy EUR/USD spot or forwards.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the split the theorem asks for. The no-arbitrage price comes from the risk-neutral measure and needs only a volatility. Your view is a real bet about the real-world measure, so it belongs in its own explicit position, sized on its own merits.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Decline to quote until you are less bullish.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It avoids the mistake, but it gives up a fair trade for no reason. The price and the view are separate questions, so your view is no reason to stop quoting.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happens.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a dated episode. The lesson's point is general: an option priced with a real-world drift is systematically off in the direction of the view, and the hedge built from it is off too. Price under the risk-neutral measure, and keep the directional bet as a separate, visible position.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"gradient-boosting","title":"Gradient Boosting","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative afternoon</div> <h3>The loss curve that never stops falling</h3> <p>You are fitting a boosted-tree model to a table of daily FX features, predicting next-day returns, with shrinkage 0.1. After a few thousand rounds the training loss is still falling. The validation loss, measured on a random 20% of rows held out from the same years, is falling too. A colleague says the curves prove the model generalises and wants to size it up. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree. Both curves still fall, so add more rounds and trade it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Each round can only lower the training loss, so that curve proves nothing. And a validation set drawn at random from the same years shares regimes and neighbouring days with the training rows: it can fall right along with an overfit model.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Raise the shrinkage to 1 so it converges in far fewer rounds.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A full-strength step lets each tree chase that round's noise harder. It speeds up fitting, not generalising, and it does nothing about the leaky validation split.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-split by time, holding out a later period with a purge gap, use early stopping on that set, and test the result net of costs.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Early stopping only works on a genuine validation set, and for market data genuine means later in time and free of leakage. A model this flexible needs the strictest test, not the most flattering one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Lower the shrinkage to 0.01 and let it run for many more rounds.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Smaller steps usually generalise better, so this is not wrong. But with the same random-row validation set you still cannot tell when to stop or whether anything real was learned.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This afternoon is illustrative, not a historical event. It applies the lesson's own pitfalls: training loss always falls under boosting, a leaked validation split can fall with it, and only early stopping on data held out in time, with costs, says whether the model learned signal or noise.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"graph-theory-centrality","title":"Graph Theory Fundamentals &amp; Centrality Measures","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative risk meeting</div> <h3>You can keep one hedge. Your worry is spillover between two halves of the book</h3> <p>Your book looks like the lesson's toy network: a European cluster (EUR, GBP, CHF), a commodity cluster (AUD, NZD, CAD), and JPY as the only link between them. Risk tells you to drop every hedge but one. The head of desk says the danger is a shock in one cluster spreading into the other. Which node keeps its hedge, and why?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">EUR or AUD, because they have the most connections.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That answers a different question: who has the most direct links. Degree scores EUR and AUD at 3, but the worry here is transmission between the clusters, and degree cannot see that at all.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">JPY, because it has the highest betweenness.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Every one of the 9 cross-cluster shortest routes runs through JPY, so its betweenness of 9 is the highest in the network. The question is about traffic between the halves, and betweenness is the measure built for that question.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Run all three measures and hedge whichever node wins the most of them.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Reporting all three is good practice, but a vote picks EUR or AUD (they win degree and eigenvector). Measures answer different questions; you should pick the one that matches the question, not count wins.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">GBP, because its eigenvector score of 0.74 shows it is safely peripheral.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A low score says GBP is not central, which is the opposite of a reason to hedge it. And no measure points to GBP as the channel between the clusters: its betweenness is zero.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this shows.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's teaching network, not a real episode. The point is the one the worked example makes: the three measures crown different nodes, and the right one depends on the risk you care about. For spillover between clusters, that is betweenness, and the answer is JPY.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"hamiltonian-monte-carlo-nuts","title":"Hamiltonian Monte Carlo &amp; NUTS","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative model fit</div> <h3>NUTS finishes, with a warning about divergences</h3> <p>You fit a hierarchical model that pools a dozen currency pairs' carry returns, with a between-pair spread parameter on top. NUTS runs to the end. The summary shows several divergent transitions, mostly where the between-pair spread is small, the narrow neck of the funnel. The posterior means look sensible. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Ignore the warning. The run finished and the numbers look reasonable.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A divergence means the simulated trajectory became numerically unreliable. Clustered in the funnel's neck, it means the sampler cannot reach that region properly, so the posterior there is biased. \"It ran\" is not \"it worked\".</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the fit as untrusted: lower the step size by targeting a higher acceptance rate, or reparameterize the model so the neck is easier to cross, then check divergences, R̂ and effective sample size again.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Divergences are a direct diagnostic that plain Metropolis and Gibbs do not have. They point at the region of sharp curvature, so the fix aims at that region, and the usual checks confirm whether it worked.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch to a Gibbs sampler, since it never reports divergences.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Gibbs struggles with exactly this funnel, moving one coordinate at a time through a narrow, curved neck. It does not report the problem; that is not the same as not having it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Run the same sampler four times longer.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More draws help effective sample size, but the trajectories through the neck will keep diverging. Longer runs do not fix a geometry problem.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What to take from it.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a historical event. It follows the lesson: divergences come from regions of sharp curvature such as a hierarchical funnel's neck, a too-large step size makes them worse, and divergences, R̂ and effective sample size reduce risk without proving the model is right.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"hedging-with-options","title":"Hedging with Options: Puts, Collars and FX Cover","date":"2020-03-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 March 2020</div> <h3>The index is down 30%, the VIX is at a record, and the committee wants cover</h3> <p>You manage a $10m portfolio that tracks the S&amp;P 500. It is unhedged. At the close on 16 March 2020 the index is 2,386.13, 29.5% below its peak of about four weeks earlier, and the VIX has just closed at a record 82.69. Your risk committee wants protection for the next three months. For the arithmetic, suppose three-month options are priced at a flat 60% volatility, with no interest (an illustration, not a quote). What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy three-month at-the-money puts on the whole portfolio.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">At the illustrative price this costs about 11.9% of the portfolio, $1,192,354 on $10m. The index would need to fall more than that again just for you to break even, and you would have paid close to the highest price on record for the cover.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy three-month puts 10% out of the money on the whole portfolio.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Cheaper, at about 7.0%, and it does set a floor. But the worst case is then 10% plus 7.0%, about 17.0% of a portfolio that has already fallen by about 30%, and you are still paying a panic price for it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Buy the 90% put and sell a 120% call against it, a collar, so that the volatility you sell pays for most of the volatility you buy.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">At 60% the 120 call is worth about 5.4%, so the collar costs only about 1.6% net. The worst case is a further 11.6%, and the price is a cap on gains at +20% for three months. Real call volatilities sit below put volatilities (skew), so the real net cost is higher, which you should check before trading. The idea is the point: when volatility is dear, sell some of it too.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Wait for volatility to fall before buying any protection.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You stay fully exposed to the next leg down while you wait. A week later, on 23 March, the index closed at 2,237.40, another 6.2% lower, and the VIX was still 61.59.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The index closed at 2,237.40 on 23 March, its closing low and 33.9% below the February peak, then recovered: on 16 June it closed at 3,124.74, 31.0% above the 16 March close, and it set a new record close of 3,389.78 on 18 August. On those illustrative prices the plain 90% put would have cost 7.0% and expired worthless over the three months. The collar would have capped the recovery at +20% less its net cost, 18.4%, against the index's +31.0%. Hindsight favours the unhedged, and it proves nothing about the decision: in a different path the cap would have been irrelevant and the floor everything. The choice is graded on prices and mandate at the time. The option prices here are illustrative flat-volatility Black-Scholes values, not market quotes.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\">Cboe Volatility Index (VIX), daily close, via FRED series VIXCLS</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\">S&amp;P 500 index, daily close, via FRED series SP500</a> (S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"herding-narratives-bubbles","title":"Herding, Narratives and Bubbles","date":"1996-12-05","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1996-12-05\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 5 December 1996</div> <h3>The Fed chairman has just said \"irrational exuberance\"</h3> <p>Tonight Alan Greenspan asked in a speech how we would know when \"irrational exuberance\" had unduly escalated asset values. You run a value-minded fund, and you already think technology stocks are priced far ahead of their earnings. Your clients judge you on recent results. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Short the Nasdaq heavily now. Even the Fed chairman agrees with you.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Being right about value says nothing about when. A heavy short has to survive the crowd pushing prices further from value first, through margin calls and client withdrawals. A speech is one piece of public news, and this one moved markets for about a day.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Write down your valuation case and what would have to be true to justify the price. Keep any fade small enough that the move against you can double without forcing you out, and measure how concentrated your own exposure is.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This uses your own information without betting the fund on the timing. Most famous \"right but early\" losses were sizing failures, not analysis failures. Sizing to survive keeps you in the game until the evidence turns.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Stay out of tech entirely and stick to your value book.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It avoids both the short squeeze and the crash, which is sound. But years of lagging a booming index carry their own risk when clients judge you on recent results, and that risk can close a fund before the analysis is proved right.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy the tech leaders on margin. The crowd cannot all be wrong.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This joins a cascade with borrowed money. You add no information of your own, and the leverage that forces buying on the way up forces selling on the way down.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Markets wobbled for a day, and the Nasdaq Composite then nearly quadrupled over the next three-plus years. Julian Robertson's Tiger Management, a value investor that would not buy the technology story, suffered heavy losses and client withdrawals, and at the end of March 2000 Robertson announced he was closing the funds. The Nasdaq had closed at its peak, 5,048.62, on 10 March 2000. By 9 October 2002 it closed at 1,114.11, a fall of about 78%, and it did not close above its 2000 peak again until 2015. The analysis was right; the timing was what cost him.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/boarddocs/speeches/1996/19961205.htm\">Alan Greenspan, “The Challenge of Central Banking in a Democratic Society”, Federal Reserve, 5 Dec 1996</a>; <a href=\"https://hedgefundalpha.com/investor-letters/archives-julian-robertson-closing-letter-investors/\">Julian Robertson, letter to Tiger Management limited partners, 30 Mar 2000</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/NASDAQCOM\">Nasdaq Composite daily closes (FRED, NASDAQCOM)</a> (1,300.12 on 5 Dec 1996; 5,048.62 on 10 Mar 2000; 1,114.11 on 9 Oct 2002; first close above the peak on 23 Apr 2015).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"heston-model","title":"The Heston Model","date":"1987-10-19","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1987-10-19\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · after 19 October 1987</div> <h3>Out-of-the-money puts look too expensive</h3> <p>On 19 October 1987 the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell about 22.6% in one day. In the months after, you notice that index puts struck well below the market trade at a much higher implied volatility than at-the-money options on the same expiry. Your pricing model uses one flat \\(\\sigma\\), and by it those puts are overpriced. What do you conclude?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The puts are mispriced. Sell them and wait for the skew to flatten back.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The flat-σ model cannot produce a skew at all, so its verdict of \"overpriced\" says more about the model than the market. Selling puts here is taking on exactly the crash risk the premium pays for.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the skew as a real feature: falls tend to arrive with volatility spikes, so downside puts are worth more. Price and hedge off the skewed vols, not one flat number.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the Heston reading. Volatility moves, and its shocks are negatively correlated with price, which fattens the left tail. The skew is the market pricing a real asymmetry, not a free lunch to arbitrage away.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the flat-σ model, but use the at-the-money vol and ignore the far strikes.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It avoids the worst trade, but it misprices every strike away from the money and gives the wrong hedge for downside options, which is where the risk now sits.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy the at-the-money options instead, since they look cheap next to the puts.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">\"Cheap compared to the puts\" is still a flat-σ judgement. Nothing says at-the-money vol is too low; the gap between strikes is the structure, not the error.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The skew did not go away. Before October 1987, implied volatility on index options was fairly flat across strikes; afterwards, out-of-the-money puts stayed richer and the skew became a permanent feature of index options. Heston's 1993 model was one answer: stochastic volatility with a negative price-vol correlation produces that skew from its own dynamics.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1987\">Federal Reserve History, “Stock Market Crash of 1987”</a> (the Dow’s 22.6% fall); <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.1994.tb00079.x\">Rubinstein, “Implied Binomial Trees”, Journal of Finance, 1994</a> (index-option smile before and after the crash); <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/6.2.327\">Heston, Review of Financial Studies, 1993</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"hidden-markov-models","title":"Hidden Markov Models &amp; Regime Detection","date":"2022-07-13","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-07-13\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 13 July 2022</div> <h3>Your regime model says the bond hedge should be working</h3> <p>US CPI for June has just printed at 9.1% year on year. The Fed has been raising rates since March, with a 75bp step in June. Stocks and bonds are both down sharply since January. You hold a balanced book of stocks with Treasuries as the hedge, and you use a two-state model fitted on 2000–2021. Its \"stress\" state was learned in years when bonds rallied as stocks fell. What do you do with its reading?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trust the model. If it says stress, the bond leg will cushion the stock leg, as it always has.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The model's stress state has bonds rallying built into its emission distribution. Today's data does not look like either state it knows, so it is choosing the least-wrong box, not describing what is happening.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add a third state and refit with Baum-Welch on the same 2000–2021 data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More states always raise the fit, but the algorithm cannot learn a state the data never showed. On that window there is no \"stocks and bonds fall together\" regime to find.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the label as unreliable for this joint move, and judge the hedge from outside the model: both legs are falling, so the bonds are not protecting you.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the honest response. The model can only pick among states it has seen, and this is not one of them. A judgement call from outside the model is what the situation needs.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Refit the model on a much longer history, reaching back before 2000, when the stock-bond correlation was often positive.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A real fix in principle: a longer history can contain the missing state. But it is a research project, not today's risk decision, and meanwhile the hedge is still not working.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The Fed raised rates by 75bp at each of its next three meetings and by 50bp in December, ending the year at 4.25–4.5%. Over the calendar year the S&amp;P 500 lost about 18% including dividends, and the broad US investment-grade bond index lost about 13%, its worst year on record. Balanced portfolios lost on both legs. A model whose states were learned on 2000–2021 had no state for that year.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cpi_07132022.htm\">BLS, Consumer Price Index news release, 13 Jul 2022</a> (9.1% over 12 months); <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220615a.htm\">FOMC statement, 15 Jun 2022</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20221214a.htm\">FOMC statement, 14 Dec 2022</a>; S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices and Bloomberg index returns for 2022 (S&amp;P 500 total return about −18.1%, US Aggregate about −13.0%).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"hierarchical-bayesian-models","title":"Hierarchical Bayesian Models &amp; Partial Pooling","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative allocation meeting</div> <h3>The newest strategy looks like the best one</h3> <p>You size five sub-strategies in proportion to their expected Sharpe. They are the lesson's five: A has a raw Sharpe of 2.10 on 5 trades, E has 1.90 on 6, C has −0.60 on 8, D has 0.75 on 40, and B has 0.85 on 50. A colleague proposes giving A the largest allocation and cutting C to zero. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree. A has the highest Sharpe and C is losing money.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is no pooling: five trades taken at face value. A small sample is untrustworthy in either direction, so both A's 2.10 and C's −0.60 are mostly noise.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Give all five the same allocation, since the average Sharpe across them is the only reliable number.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is complete pooling. It throws away the real, hard-earned differences that D and B show on 40 and 50 trades.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Size on the partially pooled estimates: about 1.48 for A, 1.42 for E, −0.17 for C, 0.75 for D and 0.84 for B, and let A earn a bigger share as its trades accumulate.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Each estimate is pulled toward the grand mean by exactly as much as its sample is small. A still ranks first, but not by the margin its five trades suggest.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Wait until every strategy has 50 trades before allocating anything.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safe, but it wastes the information you have. The shrinkage weight already discounts small samples by the right amount.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What to take from it.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's worked example, not a historical event. The pooled numbers check out in the calculator above. Two cautions from the lesson still apply: pooling assumes the five strategies genuinely belong in one group, and a shrunk Sharpe is a better point estimate, not proof the edge will hold out of sample.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"hierarchical-risk-parity","title":"Hierarchical Risk Parity","date":"2008-09","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2008-09\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · late September 2008</div> <h3>Five carry trades have started moving as one</h3> <p>You run a basket of five high-yielding currencies funded in the yen and the Swiss franc, weighted by a mean-variance optimizer on a covariance matrix estimated over the calm years before. Lehman Brothers failed on 15 September. Funding has dried up, carry positions are being unwound, and the high-yielders are now falling together while the yen rises. Each time you re-run the optimizer on fresh data, its weights jump around. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Re-run the optimizer on the latest data and trade whatever weights it gives.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Near-duplicate rows make the covariance matrix close to singular, so inverting it turns small estimation noise into huge weight swings. The new weights are least reliable exactly now.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the weights. Five countries and five central banks is real diversification.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">On calm data they looked different. In the unwind, within-bloc correlations are rising toward one: for risk purposes, five carry trades have become one carry trade.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the high-yielders as one bloc, measure that bloc's risk on recent data, and size the whole bloc down to it before worrying about weights inside it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the tree's top split done honestly: allocate to the bloc as a single bet, using a variance that reflects today's market, and stop asking a near-singular matrix to tell the five apart.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch to HRP, using the tree and variances estimated in August.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It removes the fragile inversion, a real improvement. But the tree's variances come from calm data, so the high-yield bloc, the one falling, still looks cheaper in risk terms than it is.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Investors facing losses and margin calls unwound carry positions together. The high-yielders fell as one block: AUD/USD dropped by more than a third between its July high and late October, and the yen rose sharply against almost everything. Within each bloc, correlations rose toward one, so the covariance rows of the high-yielders became close to duplicates. \"Diversified\" carry baskets lost as one bet, just as an inverse-covariance allocator became least reliable.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DEXUSAL\">Federal Reserve H.10 AUD/USD noon rates (FRED, DEXUSAL)</a> (0.9797 on 15 Jul 2008, 0.6073 on 27 Oct 2008, a 38% fall); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DEXJPUS\">USD/JPY noon rates (FRED, DEXJPUS)</a> (107.51 on 12 Sep, 92.64 on 24 Oct 2008); <a href=\"https://www.belfercenter.org/publication/unwinding-carry-trade-has-finally-hit-currencies\">Jeffrey Frankel, “The unwinding of the carry trade has finally hit currencies”, 29 Oct 2008</a>; Lehman Brothers Holdings Chapter 11 filing, 15 Sep 2008.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"hurst-variance-ratio","title":"The Hurst Exponent &amp; Variance Ratio Test","date":"2022-07-13","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-07-13\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 13 July 2022</div> <h3>Inflation at 9.1%, and your Hurst reading just dipped below a half</h3> <p>US CPI for June has just printed at 9.1% year on year. The Fed raised rates by 75bp a month ago. Bond yields have risen for most of the year and the dollar is strong. You run trend rules that trade on horizons of weeks to months, and they are short bonds and long the dollar. Suppose a Hurst reading on the last 30 daily bars has just slipped from 0.58 to 0.47 (a hypothetical reading for this drill). What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Close the trend book. H is below 0.5, so the market has turned mean-reverting.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">One reading from 30 bars is squarely in the small-sample zone, where R/S is noisy and biased, and can flip across 0.5 on estimation noise alone. It is weak evidence of a regime change, and the rules trade a longer horizon than the window measures.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep running the rules at their normal size, and treat the short reading as noise until longer-horizon evidence says otherwise.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The gate question is about persistence at the horizon the rules trade. A short-window wobble around 0.5 does not answer it, and neither statistic forecasts direction anyway. Sticking to the process avoids trading on noise.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Double the size. Inflation at 9.1% means the trend has to continue.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Neither H nor the variance ratio says which way the next move goes, and a strong macro story is not a measurement. Doubling up on conviction is how a trend book gets caught by the reversal it cannot see coming.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Trim a little, and wait for a variance ratio on a longer window, with its standard error, before deciding more.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Asking for a proper test with a standard error is the right instinct. But trimming on one noisy reading still lets noise move your size, which costs you when the persistence is real.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The Fed raised rates by 75bp three more times, on 27 July, 21 September and 2 November. Yields kept rising for most of the year, and by late September the dollar had reached multi-year highs (the Fed's broad trade-weighted index peaked on 26 September). Trend followers, short bonds and long the dollar, had a very strong year. The 30-bar Hurst reading in the setup was hypothetical; the real lesson is that one short-window reading crossing 0.5 is a noisy hint, not a regime change.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cpi_07132022.htm\">BLS, Consumer Price Index news release, 13 Jul 2022</a> (9.1% over 12 months); FOMC statements of <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220727a.htm\">27 Jul</a>, <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220921a.htm\">21 Sep</a> and <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20221102a.htm\">2 Nov 2022</a> (75bp each); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DTWEXBGS\">Federal Reserve nominal broad dollar index (FRED, DTWEXBGS)</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"interest-rate-parity","title":"Interest Rate Parity — Covered vs. Uncovered","date":"2024-07-31","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2024-07-31\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 31 July 2024</div> <h3>The Bank of Japan has just hiked</h3> <p>You are long AUD/JPY as a carry trade: $10,000,000 of Australian dollars funded in yen, with the RBA cash rate at 4.35% and Japanese rates near zero. The roughly 4.2-point gap has been paying about $420,000 a year, and AUD/JPY has already come off its 11 July high. Today the Bank of Japan raised its policy rate, against a market that had priced Japan as a permanent zero-rate funding source. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold. The rate gap is still about 4 points, so the carry still pays.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The differential barely moved. What matters is the market's tolerance for being short the funding currency, and a hike that hits years of yen-funded positioning is exactly what makes everyone want to buy back yen at once.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut the position, or hedge it, now. The funding currency is starting to rise, and that is the crash you are paid to carry.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Carry is short a fat left tail. Practitioners de-risk when volatility rises or when the funding currency starts appreciating sharply, because one bad month can take years of carry. Giving up some carry is cheap next to the tail.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add. Uncovered parity fails, so the carry is close to free money, and a lower price is a better entry.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A failure of uncovered parity is not an arbitrage. It is a risk-bearing bet, and the risk it bears is exactly a sudden yen rally like this one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the position, but place a stop a few percent lower.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It caps the loss in a slow move, so it is defensible. In a fast, one-way unwind a stop fills at whatever price is there, and it is not a hedge.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> A soft US payrolls report days later revived US recession fears at the same time. Yen-funded carry was unwound across the board, and the yen rose sharply against nearly everything. AUD/JPY fell about 17% from its 11 July high to its 5 August low, roughly three and a half weeks, much of it in the sessions after the hike. On $10,000,000 that is about $1,700,000: around four years of that $420,000 carry, gone in a few weeks.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/mopo/mpmdeci/mpr_2024/k240731a.pdf\">Bank of Japan, change in the guideline for money market operations, 31 Jul 2024</a> (call rate to around 0.25%); <a href=\"https://www.rba.gov.au/statistics/cash-rate/\">Reserve Bank of Australia, cash rate target</a> (4.35% through the period); <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_08022024.htm\">US Bureau of Labor Statistics, Employment Situation for July 2024, 2 Aug 2024</a>; <a href=\"https://www.bis.org/publ/bisbull90.htm\">BIS Bulletin No 90, \"The market turbulence and carry trade unwind of August 2024\", 27 Aug 2024</a>. AUD/JPY high and low (about 109.35 and 90.12): Continuum Economics and FXStreet market notes, July and August 2024.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"investor-mood-forced-selling","title":"Mood, the Quote and Forced Selling","date":"2021-03-26","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2021-03-26\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 26 March 2021</div> <h3>A client has missed its margin calls, and other banks hold the same stocks</h3> <p>You run risk at a prime broker. A family office, Archegos Capital Management, has built very large, concentrated positions in a handful of stocks, including ViacomCBS and several US-listed Chinese companies, mostly through total return swaps with you and several other banks. This week ViacomCBS fell after announcing a share sale, and Archegos cannot meet its margin calls. You hold the stock as the hedge on your swaps. So do the other banks. What do you do this morning?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold the stock and wait for prices to recover. Selling now only locks in the loss.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You are a forced seller whether you admit it or not: the client cannot pay, and the collateral is all you have. Waiting while other holders of the same stocks sell means you walk a thinner book later.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Agree with the other banks to sell slowly together, so nobody crashes the price.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">In principle that limits the damage for everyone. In practice it relies on every other forced seller holding back. If one breaks ranks, the ones still waiting are the slowest sellers, and the slowest seller walks the book the furthest.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Sell the collateral promptly in large blocks, accepting a worse price now, because several forced sellers hold the same thing and this is a race.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's point about shared holders. When the selling is forced and the holdings overlap, the book is only so deep, and the first sellers get the best of it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On Friday 26 March, banks began selling large blocks of the underlying stocks: price-insensitive sales driven by collateral, not by any view on the companies. Banks that sold early limited their losses. Credit Suisse later reported a loss of about $5.5 billion, and Nomura close to $3 billion.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/news/press-release/2022-70\">SEC press release 2022-70, charges against Archegos and its founder, 27 Apr 2022</a>; CNBC, \"The Archegos blowup and its ripple effect across markets\", 29 Mar 2021; Credit Suisse Special Committee report on Archegos (Paul, Weiss), 29 Jul 2021; Reuters, Nomura and Credit Suisse loss warnings, 29 Mar 2021.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"ito-lemma","title":"Itô's Lemma","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative forecast</div> <h3>An 8% expected return at 40% volatility</h3> <p>A colleague models a stock as geometric Brownian motion with an expected return \\(\\mu = 8\\%\\) a year and volatility \\(\\sigma = 40\\%\\). Their slide says \"the typical investor compounds at 8% a year\". You have to sign off on the slide. What do you tell them?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sign it off. The expected return is 8%, so that is the typical compound growth.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">8% is the drift of the price, an average across paths. The typical path compounds at the drift of \\(\\ln S\\), which is lower.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Typical compound growth is \\(\\mu - \\sigma^2/2\\), which is 8% − 8% = 0%: the median investor ends roughly where they started, and about half of paths end lower.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Itô's Lemma applied to \\(\\ln S\\) gives drift \\(\\mu - \\sigma^2/2\\). With \\(\\sigma = 40\\%\\), the correction is 0.08 and wipes out the whole 8%. Under this model the median future price equals today's price.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Knock a little off for volatility drag, say 1%, and call it 7%.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Right direction, wrong size. The drag is not a small haircut to guess at: it is exactly \\(\\sigma^2/2\\), and at 40% volatility that is 8 points.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Leave it. The \\(\\sigma^2/2\\) term only matters for pricing options.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It is the same term as volatility drag in compounding and in Kelly sizing. It matters any time you take the log of something random.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The arithmetic.</strong> This is an illustrative case, not a historical one. Under geometric Brownian motion with \\(\\mu = 8\\%\\) and \\(\\sigma = 40\\%\\), the log price drifts at 0.08 minus half of 0.40 squared, which is 0. The mean outcome still grows at 8%, carried by a few very large winners, while the median path goes nowhere. Same model, two different \"typical\" numbers, and Itô's correction is the gap between them.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"kalman-filter","title":"The Kalman Filter &amp; Adaptive Relationships","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Monday</div> <h3>One reading way off your hedge ratio</h3> <p>You track a hedge ratio with the lesson's filter: \\(Q = 0.0009\\), \\(R = 0.0100\\), started at 1.20. After the two worked-example days your estimate is 1.311, with uncertainty about 0.0047. This morning's reading comes in at 1.60, far above anything you have seen. What do you do with the hedge?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Move the hedge straight to 1.60. The latest data is the best data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is a gain of 1, trusting one noisy reading completely. With \\(R = 0.0100\\) a single reading has a standard deviation of 0.10, so one print is weak evidence on its own.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Run the update as specified and use its estimate, about 1.415, then watch the next few readings.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The prediction's uncertainty is \\(0.0047 + 0.0009 = 0.0056\\), so the gain is \\(0.0056 / 0.0156 \\approx 0.36\\), and the estimate moves about a third of the way: \\(1.311 + 0.36 \\times 0.289 \\approx 1.415\\). If the drift is real, the next readings will keep pulling it up.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Raise \\(Q\\) until the filter would have caught this jump, then re-run the backtest.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is tuning the noise settings on the result you want. It relocates the window-length overfit into \\(Q\\) and \\(R\\), and a large \\(Q\\) makes the filter chase every noisy print from now on.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Ignore the reading as an outlier and keep 1.311.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Cautious, and right if it was a bad print. But if the relationship really has moved, you have thrown away the only new information you have. The filter's partial step already guards against a fluke.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happens.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a historical episode. The point is the arithmetic: with the lesson's settings the filter moves about a third of the way, to roughly 1.415. One wild reading cannot drag it all the way, and several readings in a row can move it steadily. That balance is set by \\(Q\\) and \\(R\\), chosen in advance, not by the outcome.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"kalman-smoother-ekf-ukf","title":"The Kalman Smoother &amp; Extended/Unscented Kalman Filters","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative backtest review</div> <h3>A hedge-ratio strategy that looks too good</h3> <p>A colleague shows you a pairs strategy that trades a spread built on a Kalman-tracked hedge ratio. The backtest is very smooth, with few losing weeks. Reading the code, you see the hedge ratio for each day comes from an RTS smoother run once over the full five-year sample. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Approve it. The smoother gives the most accurate hedge ratio, so the backtest is the most accurate too.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It is the most accurate estimate of the past because it uses data from after each day. No live system could have known those hedge ratios on the day. The backtest is using the future without saying so.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Rerun it with the forward-only filtered hedge ratio, the number a live system would have had each day, and judge the strategy on that.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The filtered estimate uses only data through each day. That is the honest version of the backtest, and the gap between the two runs measures how much of the result came from hindsight.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch the smoother for an unscented filter to make it more accurate.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That answers a different question. The UKF handles curved relationships; the problem here is lookahead. A linear hedge ratio needs the plain filter, run forward only.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the smoothed version, but halve the reported returns to be safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It admits the problem but guesses at its size. The forward-only rerun measures it, and costs little more.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What to take from it.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a historical event. It is the lesson's main pitfall: a smoothed state estimate uses observations from after the point being estimated, the same trap as Viterbi-decoded regime labels. Smoothed paths belong in research notes and post-mortems; live signals and honest backtests use the filtered estimate.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"kelly-criterion","title":"The Kelly Criterion &amp; Position Sizing","date":"1998-08-21","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1998-08-21\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 21 August 1998</div> <h3>Russia has defaulted, and your volatility has tripled</h3> <p>You run a book of convergence trades: small, reliable spreads that should narrow over time. You sized it close to full Kelly, using volatility measured over the calm years before. Four days ago Russia defaulted on its domestic debt. Your spreads have widened against you, and the volatility you now measure is roughly three times your old estimate. What do you do with the size?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold the size. The spreads are mispriced and must converge eventually.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Kelly scales with one over the variance. Tripling volatility cuts the right bet ninefold, so the book you sized at full Kelly is now about nine times Kelly, deep in the zone where growth is negative. \"Eventually\" only helps if you survive the path, and margin calls arrive first.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the trades. Wider spreads mean a bigger edge, so Kelly says bet more.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The edge may be bigger, but the variance grew by a factor of nine. Kelly is edge divided by variance, and the variance moved far more than the edge. Adding here is the classic way a positive-expectancy book compounds towards zero.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-estimate volatility from recent data and cut the book towards the new Kelly size, taking the loss now.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the formula says. With the new variance, the right size is a small fraction of what you hold. Cutting locks in a painful loss, but it keeps you alive to keep compounding, which is the whole point of Kelly.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Halve the book and wait for calmer markets.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A real step in the right direction, but half of nine times Kelly is still about four and a half times Kelly: growth is still negative. Halving feels decisive; the arithmetic says it is not enough.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Long-Term Capital Management held its positions and looked for fresh capital. It lost $1.8 billion in August alone, which left its capital at $2.3 billion against $4.8 billion at the start of the year, and the losses continued into September. On 23 September 1998, fourteen banks and brokers convened by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York put in about $3.6 billion to take the fund over and unwind it. Many of the trades did converge later, too late for a book that could not survive the path.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.cftc.gov/sites/default/files/tm/tmhedgefundreport.htm\">President's Working Group on Financial Markets, Hedge Funds, Leverage, and the Lessons of Long-Term Capital Management, April 1999</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/boarddocs/testimony/1998/19981001.htm\">Alan Greenspan, testimony to the House Banking Committee, 1 October 1998</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"limit-order-books","title":"Limit Order Books &amp; Queue Dynamics","date":"2014-10-15","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2014-10-15\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 15 October 2014</div> <h3>A client hedge in Treasuries, in the middle of the flash rally</h3> <p>It is about 9:35 am Eastern. Weak US retail sales at 8:30 had already pushed yields lower, and now the 10-year yield is falling fast, with no headline you can find. Volume is very heavy. You have to buy a large block of 10-year notes for a client hedge, and the size showing at the best offer is small. How do you execute?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Send the whole block as one market order. Volume is huge, so the market can obviously absorb it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Volume is not depth. That morning little size rested at any one price, so a big market order would take the touch, find little behind it, and walk level after level, paying the worst prices of a move that had no news behind it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Work the order: rest limit orders near the touch, buy in slices as depth refills, and do not treat the displayed size as guaranteed.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You stop paying to walk a thin ladder at its most stretched moment. With no news driving the move, patience costs little, and checking real depth before each slice keeps each fill near the top of the book.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Split the block into a few smaller market orders a minute apart.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than one block, because the book gets a moment to refill between clips. But each clip is still a market order into thin depth, and the size you saw may be cancelled before your order arrives.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Read the heavy volume as strong buying on news you have not seen, and buy more than the hedge needs.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The move had no clear news behind it. Heavy volume from fast traders posting, hitting and replacing small quotes is activity, not information or depth.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Between 9:33 and 9:45 am the 10-year yield fell about 16 basis points and rose straight back, inside a 37-basis-point range for the day. The joint staff report by the Treasury, the Fed, the New York Fed, the SEC and the CFTC found no clear news in that window. It found heavy volume, thin resting size at the best prices, bank dealers pulling back their resting orders, and automated firms trading heavily while showing little size. Anyone who bought at market at the low in yields paid the top of a move that reversed within minutes.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://treasurydirect.gov/files/laws-and-regulations/gsa/joint-staff-report-treasury-10-15-2014.pdf\">Joint Staff Report: The U.S. Treasury Market on October 15, 2014 (US Treasury, Federal Reserve Board, New York Fed, SEC, CFTC), July 2015</a>; <a href=\"https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/news/aboutthefed/2015/oa150713.html\">New York Fed press release, 13 July 2015</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"limits-integrals-annuity-factors","title":"Limits, Integrals &amp; Annuity Factors","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative trading day</div> <h3>You have just sold $10 million of 10-year notes</h3> <p>You make markets in 10-year Treasury notes and have just sold $10,000,000 face of a note priced near par, with a modified duration of 8.50 years. That is a DV01 of $8,500 per basis point. The 10-year future you hedge with has a DV01 of about $85 per contract. You want to go home flat on rates. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell 100 futures, so both positions match.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Selling futures adds a second short. A 1bp fall in yield would now cost about $17,000 instead of nothing. A short bond is hedged with a position that gains when yields fall, which is a long future.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Buy 100 futures: $8,500 ÷ $85 per contract.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The hedge ratio is one derivative divided by another. If yields rise 1bp the short note gains $8,500 and the futures lose $8,500. If yields fall, the reverse. Net roughly flat either way.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy 50 futures now and the rest only if yields start to fall.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Half a hedge leaves about $4,250 per basis point open. That can be a deliberate view, but it is not going home flat, which is what you said you wanted.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy 85 futures, one for each dollar of the contract's DV01.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That confuses the divisor with the answer. The number of contracts is your DV01 divided by theirs, not the contract's DV01 itself. At 85 contracts you would still have $1,275 per basis point open.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson shows.</strong> This is an illustrative situation using the lesson's own numbers, not a dated event, and the $85 futures DV01 is itself an illustrative figure: real desks compute it from the cheapest-to-deliver bond. 100 contracts neutralise the first-order exposure. Because duration is a local slope, the hedge is only exact for small moves: after a large move both DV01s change, and the curvature that duration ignores is the subject of convexity.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"linear-algebra-matrices","title":"Linear Algebra &amp; Matrices","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Monday</div> <h3>Is a third dollar pair a third bet?</h3> <p>You hold equal long positions in EUR/USD and GBP/USD, with the lesson's covariance matrix: variance 3 each, covariance 1, a correlation of about a third. That book is about 1.8 effective bets. You want to add a third dollar pair, AUD/USD, with the same variance and the same covariance of 1 with each of the others. A colleague says: \"Three pairs, three bets. Size each one as if it were independent.\" What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree. A correlation of a third is low, so the pairs are close enough to independent.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Low pairwise correlation still adds up. All three share one direction, \"the dollar moves\", and that shared direction now carries 5 of the 9 units of variance, more than any single pair.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Work out the eigenvalues first: 5 for the shared direction and 2, 2 for the rest, so about 81 ÷ 33 ≈ 2.45 effective bets. Size the shared dollar exposure as one big bet.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's tool used on a 3×3 matrix. The third pair adds about 0.65 of a bet, not a whole one, and about 56% of the book's variance is a single move. Sizing for that is honest risk.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Skip the third pair: two pairs are already diversified enough.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Cautious, but it rests on a guess. The third pair does add some diversification, about 0.65 of a bet. The point is to measure it, then decide.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add it at double size, since diversification makes the book safer.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Adding a pair that mostly loads on the same dollar direction makes the shared bet bigger, not safer.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What to take from it.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's own matrix, not a historical event. With three pairs, the matrix has 3 on the diagonal and 1 everywhere else. Its eigenvalues are \\(3+1+1 = 5\\) along \\((1,1,1)\\) and \\(3-1 = 2\\) twice. Effective bets \\(= 9^2/(5^2+2^2+2^2) = 81/33 \\approx 2.45\\), up from 1.8 with two pairs. Count bets with eigenvalues, not with position tickets.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"linear-quadratic-programming","title":"Linear &amp; Quadratic Programming","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative evening</div> <h3>The nightly rebalance says \"optimal\", with yesterday's cap</h3> <p>You run the overnight rebalance for a systematic multi-asset fund. Every night a QP sets new weights under fixed rules: no instrument above 15%, no asset class net short, and position caps from the prime broker that move with volatility. After a volatile day the prime broker has cut one instrument's cap to 10%. The solver ran with the old 15% cap, reports \"optimal\" and puts 15% in that instrument. What do you do before the orders go out?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Send the orders. The solver converged and reported an optimal solution.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The answer is optimal for the wrong rules. A stale cap never throws an error; it quietly changes what \"optimal\" means. The book would breach the prime broker's limit at the open.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Clip that weight to 10% and rescale everything else to sum to 100%.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Clip-and-rescale is not a QP solve. Rescaling can push other weights back over their own caps, and the result is no longer the minimum-risk portfolio under the real rules. It looks fixed and is not.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Update the cap to 10% and re-run the QP, then add a nightly check of every constraint input against the mandate and the prime broker's current limits.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Re-solving with the right walls gives the true optimum under tonight's rules; the solver works out which other constraints now bind. The check stops the same silent failure next time.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep yesterday's weights until operations confirm the new caps.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safe from a breach only if yesterday's weights already respect the new 10% cap, and it skips a rebalance the fund wanted. Defensible as a stop-gap, not as the fix.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happens.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's own rebalance scenario, not a specific historical event. Its lesson is general: a QP solver is only as trustworthy as the constraint set it is given, and which constraints bind changes from night to night, so there is no shortcut around solving again.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"loss-aversion-prospect-theory","title":"Why a Loss Hurts Twice as Much","date":"1995-01-17","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1995-01-17\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 17 January 1995</div> <h3>The earthquake has hit, and the hidden losses just got bigger</h3> <p>You run a futures operation in Singapore, and you also control the back office that records your trades. For a while you have parked losing trades in an error account, and the hidden loss has grown. You are short Nikkei straddles and long Nikkei 225 futures. This morning the Kobe earthquake struck and the Nikkei is falling sharply. Both legs are losing together. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy more futures. Supporting the market is the fastest way back to even.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the convex loss side of the curve choosing for you. A bigger bet offers a chance of getting back to even, and measured from \"even\" that chance feels better than a certain loss. It is a larger position placed at the moment your judgement is worst.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Report the full loss now, and close or hedge the positions, taking the certain loss.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Move the reference point to today. From here, the question is whether you would open these positions, at this size, right now. You would not. A certain, visible loss today is smaller than what a hidden, growing one becomes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Stop adding, keep the positions hidden, and wait for the Nikkei to recover.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Not adding is better than adding. But hiding the loss keeps the reference point at \"even\", the margin calls keep coming, and you are still betting the bank on a rebound you cannot control.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell more options to raise premium and cover the margin calls.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It turns a hidden loss into a bigger hidden bet. Collecting premium today adds more risk to the same falling market, all to avoid booking a loss.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Nick Leeson bought more Nikkei futures, as if to hold the market up. The exchange's margin calls were met with funds wired from Barings in London, which still believed the business was hedged. In late February 1995 Leeson left Singapore. By 27 February the hidden losses had reached about £827 million, more than the bank's capital, and closing out the positions took the total to about £927 million. Barings, founded in 1762, was placed into administration on 26 February 1995 and sold to ING for £1. The Bank of England's Board of Banking Supervision reported that July and put the failure on the absence of basic controls, starting with one person running both trading and settlement.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/report-into-the-collapse-of-barings-bank\">Report of the Board of Banking Supervision inquiry into the circumstances of the collapse of Barings, 18 July 1995</a>; <a href=\"https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/bulletin/1995/nov/1.html\">Reserve Bank of Australia Bulletin, Implications of the Barings Collapse for Bank Supervisors, November 1995</a>; <a href=\"https://ing.com/news/2025/03/ings-1-deal-30-years-of-uk-growth.html\">ING, \"ING's £1 deal\", 5 March 2025</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"market-impact-almgren-chriss","title":"Market Impact Models &amp; Almgren-Chriss","date":"2021-03-26","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2021-03-26\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 26 March 2021</div> <h3>You hold the same blocks as every other prime broker</h3> <p>You run equity financing at a prime broker. Your client, Archegos, built huge positions in a handful of stocks through total return swaps, and you hold the shares as your hedge. ViacomCBS sold new shares, its stock fell sharply, and Archegos cannot meet its margin calls. Last night Archegos asked its prime brokers to agree a standstill while it wound the positions down, and they did not agree. Several other banks hold large blocks of the same stocks. It is Friday morning. How do you sell?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Follow a patient, low-urgency schedule over the coming weeks to keep your impact cost down.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the right answer for a lone seller facing random volatility. Here the risk of waiting is not random: every block another bank sells pushes down the price of what you still hold. The patient schedule sells into everyone else's impact.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Sell early and in size, in large blocks, accepting a steep discount now.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">With rival forced sellers, the first to sell gets the best prices still on offer. You pay a known, large impact cost to avoid a larger, predictable loss from selling after the others.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Run a strongly front-loaded schedule through the day, finishing by the close.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The right instinct: high urgency, done fast. But spread over a whole day, much of it can still land after rivals' blocks have hit the price. Better than a slow schedule, worse than moving first.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold off and keep pushing for an orderly joint wind-down with the other banks.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Talks had already failed, and nothing stops a rival from selling while you negotiate. Waiting leaves your whole position exposed to their selling.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On Friday 26 March some banks, Goldman Sachs among them, sold billions of dollars of stock in large blocks at steep discounts, before the market had absorbed what was happening. Goldman, Deutsche Bank and Wells Fargo later reported immaterial losses. Credit Suisse sold just over $3 billion that day, partly through algorithms kept to 2 to 3% of daily volume, and joined a managed liquidation with UBS and Nomura that ran into April. The slower banks sold into prices already pushed down by everyone else's blocks: Credit Suisse lost about $5.5 billion and Nomura about $2.9 billion.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: Credit Suisse Group Special Committee, Report on Archegos Capital Management, 29 July 2021 (<a href=\"https://www.paulweiss.com/insights/client-news/credit-suisse-publishes-independent-review-of-archegos-losses\">Paul, Weiss summary</a>); Bloomberg, \"Nomura Sticks to Global Goals After $2.9 Billion Archegos Hit\", 27 April 2021.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"markowitz-black-litterman","title":"Markowitz &amp; Black-Litterman","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · around 1990, a global bond and currency book</div> <h3>The optimizer's answer nobody will sign</h3> <p>You manage a global bond and currency portfolio. You feed your expected-return forecasts for each market into a mean-variance optimizer. It comes back with huge long and short positions in a few markets, and when you revise one forecast by a fraction of a point the weights jump again. The investment committee meets tomorrow. What do you take to it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The optimizer's output as it stands. The maths is right, so the weights are right.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The maths is right for exact inputs, and your forecasts are not exact. The optimizer piles into whichever markets your errors happen to flatter: error maximization. Those corners are artifacts of noise, not conviction.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the forecasts but add tight position limits so no weight can go extreme.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Limits stop the worst corners, which is why desks used them. But the answer then sits on whatever limits you chose, and it still jumps around when forecasts move. It hides the problem rather than fixing the input.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Start from the returns implied by the market's own holdings, and move away from them only where you hold a specific view, by as much as your confidence justifies.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is Black and Litterman's fix. The prior is sensible and stable, each view is a precision-weighted nudge, and a modest view gives a modest tilt rather than a corner.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Spend another month refining every market's forecast to two decimal places, then rerun.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Extra decimals are not extra accuracy. The optimizer's sensitivity to expected returns is the problem; more precise-looking guesses make it no less sensitive.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> This is the documented pattern rather than one dated day. Managers who fed real forecasts into an optimizer kept getting extreme, unstable weights; Michaud named it error maximization in 1989. Fischer Black and Robert Litterman, working with Goldman Sachs clients who managed global bond and currency portfolios, published their fix as a Goldman paper in 1990 and in the <em>Financial Analysts Journal</em> in 1992. Later, DeMiguel, Garlappi and Uppal (2009) found none of fourteen optimization-based models consistently beat equal weights out of sample.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2469/faj.v45.n1.31\">Michaud, \"The Markowitz Optimization Enigma: Is 'Optimized' Optimal?\", Financial Analysts Journal, 1989</a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2469/faj.v48.n5.28\">Black and Litterman, \"Global Portfolio Optimization\", Financial Analysts Journal, 1992</a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhm075\">DeMiguel, Garlappi and Uppal, \"Optimal Versus Naive Diversification\", Review of Financial Studies, 2009</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"mcmc-metropolis-gibbs","title":"MCMC: Metropolis-Hastings &amp; Gibbs Sampling","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research afternoon</div> <h3>One chain looks perfect. Four chains disagree.</h3> <p>You are fitting a three-parameter stochastic-volatility model by Metropolis-Hastings. Your first chain's trace plot looks like a hairy caterpillar, and its posterior means look sensible. Before using them, you run three more chains from deliberately scattered starting points. Three of the four settle in one band; the fourth settles in a clearly different band of the vol-of-vol parameter. \\(\\hat{R}\\) for that parameter is well above 1. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use the first chain. Its own trace plot was clean.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A single chain can look perfectly well behaved while sitting in the wrong mode. Nothing inside its own history can reveal that. This is exactly the mistake multiple chains exist to catch.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Pool all four chains and report the average.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Averaging two regions the chains never moved between does not give you the posterior. It gives you a blend of two stuck walks, weighted by how many chains happened to start near each one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the fit as unconverged: find out why the chains disagree (a second mode, a poor step size), fix the sampler, and use the posterior only once \\(\\hat{R}\\) is close to 1.00 across all chains.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">\\(\\hat{R}\\) well above 1 is a direct, quantitative flag that the chains disagree about where the posterior is. The honest answer is \"not yet\", followed by work on the sampler, not on the report.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Drop the odd chain and recompute \\(\\hat{R}\\) on the other three.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">\\(\\hat{R}\\) will now look fine, because you deleted the evidence. The fourth chain may have found part of the posterior the others never reached.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What to take from it.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a historical event. It follows the lesson's own pitfalls: one chain can converge to the wrong mode with nothing in its history to show it, and \\(\\hat{R}\\) needs several independently started chains for exactly that reason. Even \\(\\hat{R}\\) near 1 is evidence, not proof: all chains can share the same blind spot.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"mdl-information-criteria","title":"Minimum Description Length &amp; Information Criteria","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative model review</div> <h3>One lag or five for a mean-reversion spread?</h3> <p>You have 100 days of a mean-reversion spread and two autoregressive models. The 5-lag model fits better in sample. Its four extra coefficients would cost about 13.3 bits to state at the precision 100 days support, and they cut the bits needed for the leftovers by about 8.66. BIC prefers the 1-lag model by about 7.8 points. Which model do you take forward, and what do you say about it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The 5-lag model. It fits the data better, and fit is what matters.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">In-sample fit always improves when you add parameters. The extra lags cost more bits to write down than they save, which is the signature of describing this sample's quirks rather than the spread's behaviour.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">The 1-lag model, as the shortest total description among the two you compared, then check it out of sample.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what both MDL and BIC say, and it states the claim at its true size: the best compression of this data among the candidates tried. The out-of-sample check is the next test, not something the code length already proves.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">The 1-lag model, described in the write-up as the true model of the spread.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Right model, wrong claim. A shorter code is a relative verdict among the models you compared. It does not certify that the winner is correct, or that it carries a trading edge.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch to AIC, which penalises parameters less, and keep the 5-lag model if AIC prefers it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Choosing the criterion after seeing which answer you like is a hidden form of overfitting. AIC also targets a different question, expected out-of-sample KL divergence, so the exact coding argument here does not carry over to it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own lag-selection scenario, not a dated episode. Describing the four extra lag coefficients costs more bits than the story they tell about the spread is worth, so the 1-lag model is the shorter message, the same verdict BIC gives. It wins among the candidates compared; whether it holds up is a question for out-of-sample data.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"mdp-bellman-equation","title":"MDPs, Value Functions &amp; the Bellman Equation","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative losing short</div> <h3>Your short costs you every step. Hold or close?</h3> <p>You are in the lesson's three-state market with a discount of 0.9. You are short. Each step you hold, the position costs 0.5. Closing costs 0.1 and puts you back flat, and from flat the best you can do (open again and take your chances) is worth 7.32. Holding short for good is worth 6.49 at best. What do you do, and why?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold. Closing locks in a loss, and the position might turn.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">In this model holding costs 0.5 every step with no chance of turning, and its value is −0.5 + 0.9 × 6.49, about 5.34. Refusing to realize a loss is exactly what the equation prices as the worse choice.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Close, because −0.1 + 0.9 × 7.32, about 6.49, beats holding's 5.34.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This compares both actions on their full continuation value: the reward now plus the discounted value of where you land. Closing pays a small cost to get back to a state worth more.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Close, because losing 0.1 now beats losing 0.5 now.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Right answer, short-sighted reason. That is the zero-discount view. It works here, but it ignores where each action leaves you, and in other cases the one-step view picks the wrong move.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold, because flat is worth 7.32 and you will get there eventually anyway.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You only get to flat by closing. Holding keeps you short and paying 0.5 a step. The value of flat counts only for the action that actually takes you there.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this shows.</strong> This is an illustrative situation using the lesson's toy MDP, not a real episode. Close is the Bellman answer: 6.49 against 5.34. The point is the method, not the numbers. Judge each action by the reward now plus the discounted value of where it leaves you.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"merton-jump-diffusion","title":"The Merton Jump-Diffusion Model","date":"2015-01-14","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-01-14\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 14 January 2015</div> <h3>A floor that has held for three years</h3> <p>You are long EUR/CHF on leverage, with a stop-loss a few pips under the Swiss National Bank's 1.20 floor. The floor has held since September 2011, and the SNB has said it will enforce it with the utmost determination. Realised volatility is the lowest you have ever seen on the pair, so your risk model says the position is small. How do you size it going into tomorrow?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the size. The stop caps the loss at a few pips.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A stop is an order, not a guaranteed price. It assumes the path is continuous and will trade through your level. In a jump there is no price in between, so the stop fills wherever liquidity comes back.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add size. Volatility is so low that a volatility-targeted book says you can run more.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The calm is the floor holding σ down. Nearly all the risk sits in the jump terms, λ and μ_J, which a volatility estimate from the floor years cannot see. Scaling up on low σ scales you into the jump.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Size the position for a gap far through the floor, as if the stop will not fill, and only hold what you could lose that way.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">On a floored rate the loss to plan for is the jump, and everyone can name its direction. Sizing for the gap, not the stop distance, is the only version of this trade that survives the day the floor goes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Halve the size and keep the stop.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Halving does halve the damage, so it is defensible. But the size is still set by the stop distance, which assumes a continuous path. Half of a loss many times your plan is still many times your plan.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On the morning of 15 January 2015 the SNB discontinued the floor without warning and cut its deposit rate to −0.75%. Within minutes EUR/CHF fell from 1.20 to below 0.90, with very little trading on the way down, before ending the day near parity. Stops just under 1.20 were filled far below. Many retail clients lost more than their account balance; FXCM needed an emergency $300 million loan from Leucadia, and Alpari UK went into special administration on 19 January.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/publications/communication/press-releases/2015/pre_20150115\">Swiss National Bank press release, 15 January 2015</a>; <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/the-snb/organisation/history/geld-und-waehrungspolitische-chronik\">SNB chronicle of monetary events (6 September 2011, 18 December 2014, 15 January 2015)</a>; Bloomberg, \"Leverage Cripples a Currency Broker\", 22 January 2015; Finance Magnates, 16 January 2015 (FXCM and Leucadia).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"merton-portfolio-problem","title":"Merton's Continuous-Time Portfolio Problem","date":"1998-08-17","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1998-08-17\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 17 August 1998</div> <h3>Russia has defaulted, and you are running 25 to 1</h3> <p>You run a book of small convergence trades at roughly 25 to 1: well over $100 billion of positions on under $5 billion of capital. You keep the leverage at a constant fraction of wealth. Today Russia devalued and defaulted, investors are rushing into the most liquid bonds, and your spreads are starting to widen together. Much of the market knows roughly what you hold. At this leverage, every 1% fall in the positions means selling about a quarter of the book to stay at your fraction. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold the book. The trades are right and the spreads must converge.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The trades may well be right, but at 25 to 1 a 1% fall cuts wealth by about a quarter. You do not get to choose when to de-lever: your lenders will, by demanding collateral, at the worst prices.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the trades. Wider spreads mean a bigger edge.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A bigger edge does not help if the variance has jumped too, and adding raises the fraction further above 1. Every extra unit of leverage makes the next forced sale bigger, in a market that already knows your positions.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Bring the leverage down now, in sizes the market can absorb, and take the loss while you still choose the timing.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Above a fraction of 1, the constant-fraction rule sells into losses, and the sale scales with the fraction minus 1. Cutting the fraction early shrinks every future forced sale. It costs money today, but it keeps you in charge of the trades instead of your lenders.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Follow the rule to the letter: after each 1% fall, sell a quarter of the book at whatever bid is there.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It is what the model prescribes, but the model assumes you trade instantly and for free at the quoted price. When the market knows what you hold, a sale that big moves prices against you and deepens the next mark.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Long-Term Capital Management, where Merton himself was a partner, could not rebalance at quoted prices. Its spreads widened together, lenders demanded more collateral, and because the market knew roughly what it held, every sale moved prices against it. By late September it had lost about $4.6 billion. On 23 September 1998 the New York Fed brought together fourteen banks and brokers, who put in about $3.6 billion to take over the portfolio and wind it down. Many of the trades converged later, after the fund could no longer hold them.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.cftc.gov/sites/default/files/tm/tmhedgefundreport.htm\">President's Working Group on Financial Markets, Hedge Funds, Leverage, and the Lessons of Long-Term Capital Management, April 1999</a> (capital of $4.8 billion at the start of 1998, more than $125 billion of assets on 31 August, fourteen firms in the consortium of 23 September); <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/boarddocs/testimony/1998/19981001.htm\">Alan Greenspan, testimony to the House Banking Committee, 1 October 1998</a>. The $4.6 billion loss is the commonly reported total and is not stated in either document.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"model-selection-aic-bic","title":"Model Selection: AIC &amp; BIC","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative lag-length choice</div> <h3>Five lags fit better. Do you trade them?</h3> <p>You model a demeaned FX cross spread that you believe reverts to zero. On the same 100 days you fit a one-lag model (2 parameters, deviance 100) and a five-lag model (5 parameters, deviance 94). AIC scores both at 104. BIC scores them 109.2 and 117.0. A colleague wants to trade the five-lag model because it \"explains the data better\". What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade the five-lag model. Its in-sample fit is better.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It had to be better. A model that nests another can never fit worse in sample. The extra lags bought 6 points of fit, no more than AIC's price for them and well under BIC's.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Take the one-lag model, make sure both were fitted on the same trimmed window, then test it out of sample before risking capital.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Neither criterion finds that lags 2 to 5 earn their keep. Aligning the sample removes the unequal-n trap, since the five-lag model uses its first five days as inputs. The out-of-sample test is the real verdict.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Call it a tie, since AIC cannot separate them, and pick either one.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A fair reading of AIC alone. But BIC's 7.8-point gap is a clear preference, and in a tie the simpler model is the safer bet. It also skips the out-of-sample check.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep adding lags until the fit stops improving, then trade that.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The fit never stops improving: each extra lag can only match or beat the last model in sample. This rule always ends at the biggest model you are willing to build.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the numbers say.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's worked example, not a historical event. The four extra lags were most likely fitting noise in that 100-day window. The one-lag model is the one more likely to keep the same coefficients on data it has not seen, and it still has to pass a genuine out-of-sample test before it earns any trust.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"multiple-testing","title":"Multiple Testing &amp; the Look-Elsewhere Effect","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research review</div> <h3>Four winners out of seventy slices</h3> <p>You tested an FX idea across 70 slices: 7 pairs × 5 regimes × 2 holding periods. Four slices came back below \\(p = 0.05\\), with p-values of 0.003, 0.012, 0.031 and 0.044. A colleague wants to start trading the four winners. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade all four. Each one passed the standard 5% test.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Seventy tests of pure noise produce 3.5 false positives on average, and at least one with 97.2% probability. Four winners is about what nothing at all looks like.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Correct for the 70 looks first. None of the four survives Bonferroni (0.000714) or Benjamini-Hochberg at 10% (the best needs 0.00143), so treat it as no finding and look for fresh out-of-sample data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the arithmetic of the lesson. The staircase thresholds for ranks 1 to 4 are 0.00143, 0.00286, 0.00429 and 0.00571, and every p-value misses its step. Only data the search never touched can show a real effect.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Drop three and paper-trade only the best slice, the one at p = 0.003.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Paper trading is out-of-sample evidence, which is the right instinct. But being first of 70 is no evidence on its own: the best of many noise draws is expected to look good, and 0.003 does not clear the corrected bar.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add more slices, by session and day of week, until one winner is clearly significant.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Every new slice is another lottery ticket. Searching until something clears the bar guarantees a winner and makes the correction even harsher, because \\(m\\) keeps growing.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the evidence says.</strong> This is an illustrative situation with made-up p-values, not a dated event. The same error at the scale of a profession is real: Harvey, Liu and Zhu (<em>Review of Financial Studies</em>, 2016) counted 316 published return factors tested on largely the same data, and argued a new factor should clear a t-statistic of about 3.0 rather than 2, because the failed tests were never counted.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"multivariate-correlated-sdes","title":"Multivariate &amp; Correlated SDEs","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative risk run</div> <h3>The Cholesky step fails on Monday morning</h3> <p>Your VaR engine simulates EUR/USD, GBP/USD and EUR/GBP together from one correlated set of shocks. This morning the correlation matrix was assembled from two data vendors with different closing times, and the Cholesky step fails: the matrix is not positive semi-definite. The risk report is due in an hour. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Simulate the three pairs independently for today.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That gets each pair's own risk right and every joint risk wrong. EUR/USD and EUR/GBP move together on euro news while GBP/USD pulls the other way, so the portfolio VaR would no longer describe the actual book.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Set the offending correlations to zero until the decomposition runs.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Zeroing entries by hand is arbitrary: it can still leave the matrix invalid, and when it works it throws away exactly the co-movement the simulation exists to capture.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Repair the matrix to the nearest valid correlation matrix, check its eigenvalues are all non-negative, run the simulation, and fix the closing-time mismatch at the source.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Nearest-correlation repair (Higham, 2002) is the standard fix and changes the matrix as little as possible. Fixing the asynchronous data stops the same failure tomorrow.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Drop EUR/GBP from the matrix and build it from the EUR/USD and GBP/USD paths.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Neat for this triangle, since EUR/GBP is close to EUR/USD divided by GBP/USD. But the broken input is still there, and the next book without such a tidy cross will fail the same way.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This drill is illustrative, not a dated episode. The problem is real and common: correlation matrices pieced together from different sources often fail to be positive semi-definite, and Higham's 2002 paper \"Computing the nearest correlation matrix: a problem from finance\" came straight from it. Repair before simulating is now a standard step.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"nelson-siegel-svensson","title":"Curve Fitting: Nelson-Siegel &amp; Svensson","date":"2022-12-14","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-12-14\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 14 December 2022</div> <h3>Your factor model says the curve must re-steepen</h3> <p>You forecast the US Treasury curve by fitting Nelson-Siegel each day and forecasting the three betas with simple time-series models, all estimated on the 2010s. Today the Fed raised rates again, to 4.25–4.50%, after four 75bp increases in a row. The 2-year yield is about 4.2%, up from about 0.7% at the start of the year; the 10-year is about 3.5%. Since early July the 2-year has stayed above the 10-year, so your fitted slope \\(\\beta_1\\) is positive. Your model, which learned a \\(\\beta_1\\) that was almost always negative, forecasts a fast return to a normal upward curve. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trust the forecast and put on a large steepener. The slope has to revert to its long-run average.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The \"long-run average\" is the 2010s, when the short end sat near zero for years. The model's persistence and mean are estimates from a regime that has just ended, so its confidence is borrowed from the wrong decade.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat it as a regime change: keep fitting the curve daily to describe it, but stop trusting forecasts estimated only on the old regime, and size any curve trade small.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Nelson-Siegel still describes each day's curve well. What broke is the assumption that the factors behave as they did in the 2010s. Separating the description, which still works, from the forecast, which does not, is the honest response.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Add more terms until the fit hits every bond, then trade any bond that sits off the curve.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A second hump may genuinely be needed soon, so Svensson is reasonable. But piling on terms to chase every wiggle loses the stability that was the point, and a bond's residual against a fitted curve is fitting noise, not an edge.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Drop the model entirely and connect the bonds with a bootstrap instead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The fitting was never the problem. A bootstrap gives you an exact but kink-prone curve, and it says nothing more about where the curve goes next.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The curve did not snap back. The inversion deepened, and at its deepest, in 2023, the 10-year yield was roughly a full percentage point below the 2-year. Treasury bills yielded more than 2-year notes, which yielded more than 10-year bonds, yet by late 2023 the 30-year yielded more than the 10-year again: a hump and a dip that needs Svensson's second term. For holders of long bonds, 2022 was the worst calendar year on record for the Bloomberg US Treasury index, down about 12.5%.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20221214a.htm\">Federal Reserve press release, 14 Dec 2022</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10Y2Y\">FRED, 10-year minus 2-year Treasury spread (T10Y2Y)</a>, with DGS2, DGS10, DGS30 and DGS3MO for the levels; Fortune (Bloomberg data), \"The U.S. Treasury market just suffered its worst-ever annual loss in 2022\", 31 Dec 2022.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"neural-sdes-spdes","title":"Neural SDEs &amp; Neural SPDEs","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative vendor pitch</div> <h3>\"Our neural SDE fits the surface far better than Heston\"</h3> <p>A vendor shows you a neural SDE calibrated to two years of your market's option prices. Its in-sample fit to the volatility surface is far tighter than your Heston calibration, and it produces beautiful simulated paths. They want you to price and hedge with it from next month. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Adopt it. A tighter fit means a better model.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">In-sample fit is what flexibility always buys. A model with many more knobs will fit two years of data more closely whether or not its dynamics generalise. That is a statement about the past sample, not about next month.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Ask for out-of-sample tests: fit on one period, then price and hedge on later data it never saw, including a stressed regime, with realistic costs, compared against Heston.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the same discipline you would apply to a simple moving-average rule, applied more urgently because there are far more parameters to overfit. If it still wins out of sample, the flexibility is earning its keep.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Use it only to generate extra market scenarios for stress tests, not for pricing.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A reasonable, lower-stakes use, and one of the few active practitioner uses today. It still needs checking that the scenarios are realistic, but a bad scenario costs less than a bad hedge.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Reject it outright, because neural networks cannot be trusted in finance.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">\"Neural\" carries no evidence either way. Rejecting it on the label skips the test just as surely as adopting it on the label. The question is out-of-sample evidence.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The lesson.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a dated event. Neural SDEs are active research, with deep hedging (2019) and neural-SDE calibration (from around 2020) the main routes into finance and no desk standard yet. A close fit says nothing until it holds on data the model never saw.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"newton-raphson-gradient-descent","title":"Newton-Raphson &amp; Gradient Descent","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative calibration morning</div> <h3>Three strikes come back at 400% volatility</h3> <p>Your volatility-surface job runs Newton-Raphson against Black-Scholes for every strike on a listed options chain. Near the money every strike converges in two or three steps. But three very deep out-of-the-money strikes come back with implied vols around 400%, and one hit the iteration cap without converging. The surface is due out to the desk in ten minutes. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Publish as is. The solver ran, so those are the market's numbers.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Far out of the money, vega is tiny, so each step divides the pricing error by almost nothing and jumps wildly. A 400% vol there is a solver artifact, and it will bend the smile for every model that reads the surface near those strikes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Raise the iteration cap until every strike converges.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More steps do not fix a step that is the wrong size. With a near-flat slope the method can bounce or converge, fast and confidently, to an answer you did not want.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-solve those strikes with a capped step inside a sane bracket, falling back to bisection or Brent's method, and flag any vol outside a plausible band for review.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the standard guard. Newton's speed where it is safe, a slower method with a guarantee where it is not, and a human look at anything implausible before it reaches the surface.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Drop the three strikes and fit the surface without them.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than publishing garbage, and the wings are thin anyway. But it hides the problem: you lose information at exactly the strikes where tail risk lives, and nobody learns the solver needs guarding.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point of the drill.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a dated event; it is the failure the lesson's trading scenario describes. Practitioners guard Newton-Raphson exactly this way: a sensible starting guess, a cap on the step, a fallback bracketing method, and a plausibility band. Manaster and Koehler (1982) gave a starting value for Black-Scholes that guarantees convergence, which is part of why implied-vol solvers became routine.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"nlp-trading-signals-sentiment","title":"NLP for Trading Signals: Sentiment, Embeddings &amp; FinBERT","date":"2011","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2011\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 2011</div> <h3>A fund wants to trade Twitter mood</h3> <p>A widely reported paper says one mood series built from about 9.8 million tweets, \"Calm\", called the Dow's daily direction with 87.6% accuracy. The test window was a few weeks of trading days in December 2008, after seven sentiment series were tried at several lags. A new London fund will trade the signal and asks you to allocate. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Allocate. 87.6% is far above chance, and the paper is everywhere.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Seven series, several lags and a test window of weeks give many chances to find something by luck. Press coverage and citations measure attention, not out-of-sample evidence.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Ask for the signal's record on data the researchers never saw, with the number of series and lags tried counted against it, before committing anything.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the step the lesson keeps returning to. Turning text into a score always works; whether the score adds anything has to be shown out of sample, with the search that found it taken into account.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Allocate a small pilot and watch the live results.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A small live test limits the damage and does produce genuinely out-of-sample evidence. But it pays to run a test that a fresh historical check could have done first, and a few months of daily direction calls is itself a noisy sample.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Build your own version with FinBERT and more mood series, and keep whichever works best.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A better text model does not fix the problem. Adding more series and keeping the winner widens the search that produced the original result, making a lucky fit more likely, not less.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The London hedge fund launched in 2011 to trade Twitter sentiment closed within about a year. In 2017 Lachanski and Pav tried to reproduce the original result and could not recover its predictive power. The text-to-number step worked as advertised. The claim that the number predicted the Dow did not survive.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jocs.2010.12.007\">Bollen, Mao and Zeng, \"Twitter mood predicts the stock market\", <em>Journal of Computational Science</em> 2(1), 2011</a> (<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.3003\">arXiv preprint</a>: 9,853,498 tweets, test period 1 to 19 December 2008); <a href=\"https://econjwatch.org/articles/shy-of-the-character-limit-twitter-mood-predicts-the-stock-market-revisited\">Lachanski and Pav, \"Shy of the Character Limit\", <em>Econ Journal Watch</em> 14(3), September 2017</a>; <a href=\"https://hedgeco.net/news/05/2012/twitter-hedge-fund-shuts-down-after-a-month.html\">HedgeCo News on the Derwent Capital Markets fund closing, 29 May 2012</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"numerical-integration","title":"Numerical Integration","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative pricing request</div> <h3>A basket option on ten underlyings, priced by tomorrow</h3> <p>A client wants a price on a basket call: the payoff depends on the weighted average of ten correlated stocks at expiry. That makes the price a ten-dimensional integral of the payoff against the joint distribution of the ten stocks. A colleague who has just learned Simpson's rule suggests laying a grid over all ten dimensions. You need a price you can defend by tomorrow morning. How do you compute it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Simpson's rule on a grid with 10 points per axis. It has the best error rate in the lesson.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Ten points per axis is \\(10^{10}\\), ten billion payoff evaluations, and ten points per axis is still a coarse grid. Simpson's better rate per point is irrelevant once the number of points grows exponentially with the dimension.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Monte Carlo: draw a million joint scenarios from the calibrated model, average the discounted payoff, and report the standard error alongside the price.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The cost is a million evaluations whatever the dimension, and the standard error tells you how much of the number is noise. If it is too wide, quadrupling the draws halves it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Quasi-Monte Carlo with a Sobol sequence instead of random draws.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A real, widely used choice that keeps the dimension-free cost and often converges faster for smooth payoffs. The catch is that you lose the clean probabilistic error bar, so check it against a plain Monte Carlo run.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Monte Carlo with 1,000 draws, quoting the price to four decimal places.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Right method, wrong confidence. The error shrinks like one over the square root of the draws, so 1,000 draws are about 32 times noisier than a million. Quoting the point estimate without its standard error overstates the precision.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happens.</strong> This is an illustrative request, not a specific historical trade. The general lesson is well documented: high-dimensional pricing problems such as basket and path-dependent options are priced by simulation, while one- or two-dimensional pricing integrals, such as the Heston Fourier integral, are done with quadrature.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"open-interest-walls-max-pain","title":"Open Interest Walls, Max Pain &amp; Squeeze Trading","date":"2021-01-27","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2021-01-27\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 27 January 2021</div> <h3>The chain says one thing, the tape says another</h3> <p>It is the evening of 27 January 2021. GameStop has gone from under \\$20 at the start of the month to hundreds of dollars. Its short interest is reported above 100% of the free float, Melvin Capital has needed about \\$2.75 billion from Citadel and Point72, and retail buyers keep piling into short-dated, out-of-the-money calls. A max-pain calculation for Friday's expiry sits far below the price, and there are big call walls above it. How do you use the chain for tomorrow?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Short the shares and wait for the price to drift down to max pain into Friday.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Max pain only describes where writers would lose least, and tests of it as a magnet have not supported the pull. Here the chain is also rebuilt higher every day, and with short interest above the float you would be joining the most crowded side of the trade.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Write calls at the next big strike up, because a call wall is resistance.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A call wall leans against a rally only when dealers are long those calls. Here customers bought them, so the writers who hedge are short them and must buy shares as price climbs toward the strike. Each strike was being overrun and rebuilt higher within days.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the chain as a snapshot that may be stale by the afternoon: stand aside, or keep any position small with risk you can define in advance.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's own warning at its most extreme. Walls and max pain describe where positions sat this morning. When open interest is rebuilt daily and trading conditions can change overnight, neither one gives you a level you can lean on.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy the shares to ride the squeeze, with a stop below.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The wall-break momentum read is a real one, and the call walls are a plausible accelerant. But the SEC staff later found no evidence that dealer hedging drove the rise, and in a market this violent a stop can fill far from where you set it. Defensible only at a small size.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 28 January GameStop hit an intraday high of \\$483. That day Robinhood and several other brokers restricted customers to selling the stock, not buying it; Robinhood said its clearing-house deposit requirement had jumped. The price fell sharply from its high. The SEC staff report in October 2021 attributed the rise mainly to a broad wave of buying, with short covering only a small part, and found no evidence of a gamma squeeze: market makers were, on its data, buying calls rather than writing them. Nobody was enforcing any max-pain level.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/files/staff-report-equity-options-market-struction-conditions-early-2021.pdf\">SEC staff report on equity and options market structure conditions in early 2021, 14 Oct 2021</a> (intraday high of $483.00 on 28 January; short interest above 100% of float; short covering a small fraction of buying; no evidence of a gamma squeeze); Benzinga, \"GameStop Short Seller Melvin Gets $2.75B Funding From Citadel, Point72\", 25 Jan 2021; CNBC, \"Robinhood says restrictions on GameStop due to tenfold increase in deposit requirements\", 30 Jan 2021.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"options-contract-mechanics","title":"Options Contract Mechanics: What You Actually Trade","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative expiry Friday</div> <h3>Your short call is ten cents in the money, with minutes left</h3> <p>This is a hypothetical scenario, not a real episode. It is the third Friday of the month, 3:45 p.m. New York time, 15 minutes before the close. You are short 5 contracts of the XYZ 105 call on a US-listed stock, sold weeks ago for a profit if it expires worthless. XYZ is trading at 105.10. The call is quoted 0.10 bid, 0.15 ask. You intended to let it expire. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Do nothing. It is only ten cents in the money, so it will probably expire and you keep the premium.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">An option that is $0.01 or more in the money is exercised automatically by the OCC unless the holder says otherwise, and holders can decide after the close with after-hours prices in front of them. You would not know whether you were assigned until after the market has closed. Being assigned leaves you short 500 shares into the weekend. You also cannot hedge for both outcomes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Buy the five calls back at the ask, about $75 plus fees, before the close.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It costs 0.15 × 100 × 5 = $75 and removes the assignment and the weekend gap risk completely. You give up a small amount of the premium you collected, but you end trading with a known position. It is the pin-risk rule of thumb: do not hold a short contract that is near the strike when trading ends.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy 500 shares of XYZ now, so that if you are assigned the shares are covered.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This does protect against assignment, but if the call is not exercised you own 500 shares you did not want, over a weekend, with the same gap risk in the other direction. It swaps one uncertainty for another and costs more in commissions than the buy-back.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell more calls at the same strike, because the premium is higher than at the open and you think the stock will fall back.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That doubles the exposure at the worst time. A contract with minutes left and the stock at the strike has the largest risk of a binary outcome relative to what you collect. You would be adding to a position that cannot be managed once the market closes.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What would happen.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, so there is no recorded result. The mechanics are real and documented: the OCC's automatic exercise of options in the money by $0.01 or more, the holder's later deadline (about 5:30 p.m. New York time, often earlier at brokers), and the delivery of shares after assignment. The point of the drill is that on expiry day the decision you can still control is the one before the close.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"options-four-positions","title":"The Four Option Positions","date":"2015-01-14","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-01-14\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 14 January 2015</div> <h3>Selling franc upside under a central-bank floor</h3> <p>Since September 2011 the Swiss National Bank has held a minimum of 1.20 francs per euro and promised to buy foreign currency in unlimited amounts to defend it. EUR/CHF has sat just above that line for more than three years. A colleague wants to sell options that pay out if the franc rises sharply. \"The central bank has promised it can't happen,\" he says, \"so all we risk is the premium.\" How do you size the trade?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Size it by the premium. The worst realistic case is giving the premium back.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The premium is the seller's best case, not the worst. A short option that pays out on a franc rise has no cap on the loss. A small premium only means the market thinks the bad case is unlikely, not that it is small.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell more. A central-bank promise makes this close to free money.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Selling more stacks up obligations against one event. If the promise breaks, every contract pays out at once, and the loss is many times the premium you collected.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Sell a smaller size, with a stop-loss just below the floor.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than sizing by premium, and a stop works when prices trade smoothly. But a stop assumes the market trades through your level. If the price gaps, it skips the level and the order fills far away, if at all.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Size by the loss at a stress price well beyond the floor, assuming no fills on the way there, and only sell what you could survive at that price.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is how sellers size: by the loss at a chosen shock, never by the premium. It treats the floor as a policy that can change, and it assumes a gap rather than a smooth slide.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The next morning, 15 January 2015, the SNB abandoned the floor without warning and cut its deposit rate to −0.75%. Within minutes the franc surged. EUR/CHF traded well below parity at the extreme before settling near it, and prices skipped the levels in between, so stop-loss orders could not be filled where they were set. At retail FX brokers, client losses exceeded account balances. Alpari's UK arm entered insolvency, and FXCM needed a rescue loan of about $300 million from Leucadia.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/mmr/reference/pre_20150115/source/pre_20150115.en.pdf\">Swiss National Bank press release, 15 Jan 2015</a> (floor of 1.20 discontinued; sight-deposit rate to −0.75%); <a href=\"https://www.bis.org/review/r150116a.htm\">Thomas Jordan's remarks on the decision, BIS central bankers' speeches, 15 Jan 2015</a>; Alpari (UK) insolvency announcement, 16 Jan 2015 (reported by Reuters); <a href=\"https://www.financemagnates.com/forex/brokers/breaking-leucadia-hands-fxcm-300-mln-life-line/\">Finance Magnates, \"Leucadia hands FXCM $300 mln life-line\", 16 Jan 2015</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"options-spreads-and-structures","title":"Option Spreads &amp; Structures","date":"1995-01-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1995-01-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 January 1995, the day before Kobe</div> <h3>You want to be paid for the Nikkei sitting still</h3> <p>You trade Nikkei 225 index options and believe the market will not move much over the next few weeks. You want to collect premium for that view. Rather than a plain bought option, you are choosing a structure. Which do you use?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell an at-the-money straddle: the most premium for the view.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It collects the most, and nothing stops the loss in either direction. If the market is hit by a shock, the loss is a multiple of the premium with no ceiling. This is the structure that turned an ordinary range view into a catastrophe.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell a wider strangle and rely on a stop-loss order.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A wider strangle is safer on a normal day, but the loss is still uncapped. A stop order does not protect you from a gap: it is a trigger to trade, and if the market jumps through your level, you are filled at the next available price, far beyond it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Sell an iron butterfly or iron condor: the same short premium, with a bought option on each side as a wing.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You collect less, but the worst case is fixed in advance at the wing width less the credit. A shock costs you that amount and no more. You pay for the protection out of the premium; that is the price of surviving the surprise.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Skip the trade: your view of \"quiet\" is not strong enough to be paid for.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible and cheap. If you cannot say what you sold to collect the premium, and what the worst case is in points and in money, standing aside is a position. It earns nothing, but it loses nothing.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> A Barings trader had built up a large book of short straddles on Nikkei index options during 1994 and into 1995 (the dated scenario above is hypothetical, set on the eve of the quake). The Kobe earthquake struck early on 17 January 1995 and the market fell; the options book swung from a gain of about US$178 million at end-1994 to a loss of about US$108 million by end-February. The trader added to other positions hoping for a recovery. Unauthorised trading losses reached about £827 million (US$1.4 billion) by 27 February, and administrators were appointed to the Barings Group parent company on 26 February 1995. The losses were made far larger by hidden positions and failures of control, so this drill is about the option structure only. A wing on each side of that one option position would have capped its loss; it would not have fixed the controls.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: Board of Banking Supervision, <em>Report of the Board of Banking Supervision Inquiry into the Circumstances of the Collapse of Barings</em>, HMSO, July 1995; Nick Leeson, <em>Rogue Trader</em>, Little, Brown, 1996. Option-book values and the build-up from January 1994: S. Brown and O. Steenbeek, \"Doubling: Nick Leeson's trading strategy\", <em>Pacific-Basin Finance Journal</em> 9 (2001), citing the Singapore and Bank of England reports; £827 million at 27 February 1995 and administration on 26 February: Reserve Bank of Australia <em>Bulletin</em>, November 1995. The US$ conversion is as commonly reported; the exact position size is not stated here.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"options-trade-selection","title":"From View to Trade: Choosing the Expiry and Strike","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative day before earnings</div> <h3>You expect good results and the weekly straddle costs $8</h3> <p>This is a made-up situation with invented numbers, to practise the arithmetic. A stock is at $100 and reports after tomorrow's close. You expect good results and a rise of about 4%. The seven-day at-the-money straddle costs $8.00, so the market is pricing an average move of about 8% either way (about 72% annualised volatility). The seven-day call costs $3.99. Your past studies of this stock's earnings moves suggest they average 4 to 5%. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy the seven-day at-the-money call. Direction is all that matters.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Breakeven is about $104, exactly where you expect the stock to go, so there is no edge before costs. And if the stock does rise 4% while volatility falls to 30% after the report, the call is worth only about $4.34, a gain of $0.35 on $3.99 before the spread. You can be right on direction and make almost nothing.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Compare your expected move with the straddle first. The market is charging $8 for a move you expect to be about half that, so pass, or use a smaller defined-risk structure.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The straddle is the average absolute move the market is pricing. You expect roughly half of it. Unless you can show the move will exceed the implied one, or that the direction is unusually predictable, you are overpaying for the volatility. \"No trade\" is a position.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy a three-month call instead, to avoid the volatility crush.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Longer options carry less event premium, so there is less to crush, and a late reaction is survivable. But you still pay for the same direction view, with more vega and more cash at risk, and a one-day event is a poor reason to own three months of time.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell the straddle: your history says the stock moves 4 to 5% and the market prices 8%.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Averages favour the seller here, but the loss when it goes wrong is a multiple of the premium collected and on the call side has no limit. One gap of 20 or 30% costs many quiet events' profits. The short-premium lesson later in this series covers exactly this trade, and sizing for it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this shows.</strong> This is an illustrative scenario with invented numbers, not a real episode, so there is no outcome to report and no source to cite. The arithmetic is real: a $100 stock with a $8.00 seven-day straddle implies about 72% annualised volatility. After a report, with volatility at 30% and six days left, the same call would be worth about $4.34 after a 4% rise and about $8.07 after an 8% rise (the size the straddle was pricing). Only a move at least as big as the quoted one pays well, which is why the comparison comes before the trade.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"options-trading-styles","title":"Five Ways to Trade Options, and How Each One Fails","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative earnings week</div> <h3>Everyone expects a big move. Which side do you take?</h3> <p>This is a hypothetical situation, not a real company, with round numbers chosen to keep the arithmetic clear. A stock is at $100 and reports results tomorrow after the close. The nearest at-the-money straddle costs $6.40, so the market is pricing a move of roughly 6% either way. You also expect a big move, perhaps 6%. You have no view on direction, and costs are $0.10 per option leg. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy the straddle. A big move is coming.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The price already contains your forecast. If the move is 6% as priced, the straddle returns about what it cost before costs, and the 20 cents of costs on two legs makes the average result negative. You need the move to be larger than 6.4% plus costs, and you have no reason to think it will be.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell the straddle. The price is high, so collect it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A price that matches a fair forecast is not high. If the move is 6% you roughly break even before costs. A 15% move would lose about $8.60 against the $6.40 collected, with no cap on how large it can be, and you have no reason to think the move will be smaller than priced.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Pass, unless you can say why you expect a move bigger or smaller than 6.4%.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Edge is your forecast against the price. Where the two agree there is none, and costs make the average trade a loss on either side. A trade is worth considering only when you have a reason for a different number, and then you size it for the case where you are wrong.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy a small straddle for the fun of it, sized so that losing the premium is trivial.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible as a bet you treat as entertainment, with the loss capped at an amount you do not mind. It is not an edge: the expected result is minus the costs. The research on retail option traders found entertainment was a bigger motive than hedging, and it found large average losses.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>This is a hypothetical, so there is no outcome to report.</strong> The arithmetic is the point. If the typical move really is the priced 6.4%, a straddle buyer's average result is zero before costs and −$0.20 after them, and the seller's is also −$0.20. Both sides lose the toll to the market maker. Someone can only do better than that with a forecast that differs from the price and is right.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"ou-mean-reversion","title":"The Ornstein-Uhlenbeck Process &amp; Mean Reversion","date":"2007-08-09","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2007-08-09\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 9 August 2007</div> <h3>Your reversion book has gone the wrong way for three days</h3> <p>You run a leveraged, market-neutral equity book built on short-horizon mean reversion: buy recent losers, sell recent winners, and wait for the pull back. Since Tuesday your positions have moved steadily against you, with no obvious news in the stocks themselves. The gaps to fair value are now the widest your model has ever seen, and you are close to your risk limit. What do you do with the book?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to it. The gaps are the widest ever, so the expected snap back is the biggest ever.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The half-life describes the expected path, not this path. The push is a steady stream of selling, not random noise, and adding leverage near your limit means one more bad day forces you out completely.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut leverage to a size you could hold even if the gaps widen for several more days, and keep the rest of the book.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You stay in the trade but take the decision away from your risk limit. The aim is to survive the path, because a book that is forced out cannot collect the reversion when it comes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Liquidate everything now, before it gets any worse.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It caps the damage and is defensible risk control. But it sells into the same one-way flow that is causing the losses, locks them in near the extremes, and leaves nothing to benefit if the reversion arrives.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Change nothing. The fitted half-life says the gap will close within days.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A constant \\(\\theta\\) is a model assumption. The pull back is supplied by traders with capital, and when those traders are being forced out, the pull weakens exactly when the gap is widest. Doing nothing leaves the decision to your risk limit.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In Khandani and Lo's reconstruction, a simple strategy of buying the previous day's losers and selling its winners lost heavily on 7, 8 and 9 August 2007, then rebounded sharply on Friday 10 August. Their explanation, offered as a hypothesis, was that at least one large fund unwound a quant equity book quickly, and similar funds hit their limits and sold too. Funds forced to cut on the Wednesday and Thursday sold near the bottom and missed the rebound.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://web.mit.edu/~alo/www/Papers/august07.html\">Khandani and Lo, \"What Happened to the Quants in August 2007?\", <em>Journal of Investment Management</em> 5 (2007)</a>, with the <a href=\"https://resources.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/research/conference/2007/liquidity/Khandani_Lo.pdf\">conference version at the New York Fed</a> (contrarian strategy −1.16%, −2.83% and −2.86% on 7, 8 and 9 August, then a sharp rebound on 10 August); <a href=\"https://www.nber.org/papers/w14465\">Khandani and Lo, NBER Working Paper 14465, 2008</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"overconfidence-overtrading","title":"Overconfidence and the Cost of Trading Too Much","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative winning streak</div> <h3>Three wins in a row. Time to speed up?</h3> <p>You trade a currency pair off the daily chart. It moves about 80 pips on a typical day and costs 1.2 pips a round trip, so costs are 1.5% of the daily range. Your last three trades won and you feel in the zone. A friend says to move to the 5-minute chart, where you would target 8-pip moves, \"to get more opportunities\". What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Move to the 5-minute chart and trade more. You are hot.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The same 1.2 pips is now 15% of each 8-pip move, ten times the share. Three wins is a tiny sample, and crediting it to skill is exactly the self-attribution that turns a streak into turnover.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Stay on the timeframe your plan was written for, keep your round-trip budget, and log a range for each trade so the streak can be scored.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This puts a rule between the feeling and the order ticket. Cost stays at 1.5% of the move, and after enough logged trades you will know whether your confidence is calibrated or just recent.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Stay on the daily chart but double the size while you are on a streak.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It avoids the cost trap, which is better than speeding up. But sizing up on confidence that has never been scored adds risk without adding edge. Betting as if your edge were bigger than it is can turn a winning edge into a losing one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Take every signal on both charts, so you never miss a move.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This adds the worst of both: more round trips, and many of them at a cost of 15% of the move. Turnover rises with no evidence that the extra trades beat their own fee.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What tends to happen.</strong> This streak is illustrative, not a real trader. The pattern it describes is documented: investors who switched to online trading in the 1990s had been beating the market before they switched, then traded more actively and fell behind the market (Barber and Odean, 2002). In Taiwan's complete trading records, fewer than 1% of day traders could be shown to profit reliably, year after year, net of fees.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"particle-filter","title":"The Particle Filter (Sequential Monte Carlo)","date":"2015-01-15","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-01-15\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 15 January 2015</div> <h3>The franc floor goes, in one print</h3> <p>You track the hidden \"fair level\" of EUR/CHF with a state-space filter. For more than three years the Swiss National Bank has held a minimum rate of 1.20 francs per euro, and the pair has barely moved. This morning the SNB scraps the floor. Within minutes EUR/CHF trades below 1.00. Your filter has to make sense of a print more than 15% away from its estimate. Which filter do you want running?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">A linear Kalman filter with the small process noise that fitted the last three calm years.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">With one small Gaussian shock size, the filter treats the print as mostly sensor noise and moves its estimate a little each bar. It underreacts to a genuine jump for a long time, while reporting high confidence.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">A particle filter whose process noise is a mixture: mostly small drifts, with a small chance of a large jump.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Some particles take the rare jump on every step. When the print arrives, those few explain it, the weight collapses onto them, effective sample size plunges, and resampling refills the crowd around the new level. The estimate jumps with the market.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">A Kalman filter with its process noise raised permanently to the size of today's move.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That handles today, then overreacts to ordinary noise on every quiet day afterwards. One bell curve cannot be both small and large; that is the lesson's point.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the calm-period Kalman filter, but reset it by hand to the new price.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It works once, if someone is watching. It is a manual override, not a model, and it gives no help the next time a jump arrives unannounced.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 15 January 2015 the Swiss National Bank discontinued the minimum exchange rate of 1.20 francs per euro, which it had set in September 2011, and cut its deposit rate to −0.75%. EUR/CHF fell from about 1.20 to below parity within minutes. A process that sat almost still for years moved in a single discontinuous step: the \"mostly small, occasionally huge\" shock shape that a single Gaussian cannot hold and a particle filter can.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/mmr/reference/pre_20150115/source/pre_20150115.en.pdf\">Swiss National Bank press release, 15 Jan 2015</a>; <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/mmr/reference/pre_20110906/source/pre_20110906.en.pdf\">Swiss National Bank press release setting the 1.20 minimum, 6 Sep 2011</a>; JPMorgan Chase Institute, \"Timing central bank announcements\" (EUR/CHF from 1.201 to 0.895 in about 24 minutes).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"payoff-shape-vs-greek-signs","title":"Same Greeks, Different Risk","date":"2008-10-27","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2008-10-27\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 27 October 2008</div> <h3>Short Volkswagen calls on the Monday after Porsche's disclosure</h3> <p>You run a book that has sold call options on Volkswagen ordinary shares, collecting premium. Yesterday, Sunday 26 October, Porsche disclosed that it controls about 74% of the ordinary shares, about 43% outright and most of the rest through cash-settled options. The state of Lower Saxony holds about another 20%. That leaves only a few percent freely tradable, and many funds are short the stock. Your risk report shows short gamma, long theta and short vega, the same columns as your short puts elsewhere. What do you do with the short calls?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold them. The Greek columns look the same as the short puts, which have never caused trouble.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Same signs, different roads. A short put's loss stops at the strike minus the premium. A short call has no cap, and with almost no free shares and many forced buyers, the road ahead is the cliff, not the wall.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell more calls and collect extra premium while the move is hot.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Any extra premium is the market pricing a big move. Selling more adds unbounded loss at the moment the squeeze has a mechanism behind it. The premium is your best case, never your risk.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the calls but delta-hedge, buying shares as the price rises.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Hedging keeps you flat for small moves. But it means buying into a squeeze where the supply of shares is tiny, and any gap between hedges is an uncapped loss. You stay exposed to exactly the tail the Greeks do not show.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Close the short calls or cap them now by buying further-out calls, and size whatever is left by a stressed loss you choose, accepting the cost today.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's rule: size shorts by stressed loss, not by Greek columns, and cap the side that has no ceiling. Paying up now is the price of turning a cliff into a wall.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The short sellers needed to buy shares back, and there were far too few to go round. Over the next two trading days VW's share price rose roughly fivefold, trading above €1,000 intraday on 28 October and making Volkswagen, for a brief moment, the most valuable listed company in the world by market capitalisation. The squeeze eased only after Porsche said it would release some of its position to provide liquidity. Anyone short the upside had no level at which the loss was capped.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.porsche-se.com/en/media/press-releases/detail/porsche-heads-for-domination-agreement-1\">Porsche SE press release \"Porsche heads for domination agreement\", 26 Oct 2008</a> (42.6% in shares plus 31.5% in cash-settled options); Porsche SE press release on settling hedges covering up to 5% of VW ordinary shares, 29 Oct 2008; Bloomberg, \"Volkswagen Overtakes Exxon as Most Valuable Company\", 28 Oct 2008; Risk.net on the BaFin investigation (high of €1,005.01 on 28 October, from €210.52 the previous Friday; Lower Saxony 20.1%).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"pca-factors","title":"Principal Component Analysis &amp; Factor Regimes","date":"2020-03-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 March 2020</div> <h3>Six currency pairs, and they are all moving as one</h3> <p>Last night the Federal Reserve cut rates to zero and widened its dollar swap lines. Firms and funds are scrambling for dollars and selling whatever is liquid, even Treasuries and gold. The dollar is rising against almost every currency at once. You hold six FX positions, sized as six separate bets because a PCA fitted on the calm months before showed several distinct factors. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the sizes. The PCA says you hold several independent bets.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That PCA describes a market that has just disappeared. It was fitted on calm months, and a calm-period spectrum overstates how many independent bets you hold in the tail, which is now.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add two more pairs to spread the risk further.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">In a one-factor market the new pairs load on the same dollar factor. You add size to the one bet you already have too much of.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-run the PCA on the last few weeks, treat the book as one dollar bet, and cut the gross size to what you would hold in a single position.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the lesson's warning means in practice. Count bets by factors, not by positions. With one factor dominating, the honest size is the size of one trade.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Hold for now. The Fed acted last night, so the dollar squeeze should ease.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It did ease eventually, but you cannot know when. Holding a book sized for several bets while it behaves as one is a bet on the timing of policy, not risk management.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> That day the VIX closed at a record 82.69. The Fed widened its dollar swap lines again on 19 March and, on 23 March, announced open-ended asset purchases and new credit facilities. The S&amp;P 500 hit its low that day, about 34% below its 19 February peak. The dollar squeeze eased after that, and with it the one-factor market.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200315a.htm\">Federal Reserve FOMC statement, 15 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200319b.htm\">Federal Reserve press release on dollar swap lines with nine more central banks, 19 Mar 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200323b.htm\">Federal Reserve press release on extensive new measures, 23 Mar 2020</a>; Cboe VIX daily history (record close on 16 Mar 2020); S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices daily closes for the S&amp;P 500 (record on 19 Feb, low on 23 Mar 2020).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"point-in-time-data-survivorship-bias","title":"Point-in-Time Data &amp; Survivorship Bias","date":"2024-08-21","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2024-08-21\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 21 August 2024</div> <h3>The payroll history under your backtest just changed</h3> <p>You are testing a strategy that trades the US dollar on the monthly payrolls report. Your backtest reads payroll figures from a table that keeps one current value per month. Today the Bureau of Labor Statistics publishes its preliminary annual benchmark revision: job growth in the twelve months to March 2024 was likely overstated by about 818,000. Your data vendor will fold the revision into the history. What do you do with the backtest?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Re-run it on the updated table. Better data makes a better backtest.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better data about the economy, worse data about what traders saw. The strategy traded the first-print number on release day. The revised figure did not exist then, so feeding it in is lookahead that looks like ordinary history.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Drop the twelve revised months and keep everything else.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It removes this one leak, but payroll figures are revised routinely, month after month and again each year. The rest of the history has the same problem, just less visibly.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Rebuild the history from first-release vintages, each tagged with the date it was published, and test on those.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what a live system could have known on each day. Public archives that keep every vintage, such as the St. Louis Fed's ALFRED, exist for exactly this reason. Store the revisions as new dated records, never as overwrites.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Freeze the table as it stands today so nothing changes again.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Freezing keeps every revision that was already folded in place before today. You still cannot tell which numbers were first prints. What you need is the publication date on every value, not a frozen copy.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 21 August 2024 the Bureau of Labor Statistics published a preliminary benchmark revision cutting payroll growth for the year to March 2024 by about 818,000 jobs. Any backtest run afterwards on a current-value table would quietly trade on weaker numbers that nobody had seen when those reports came out. The prices moved on the first prints, months earlier. Only a vintage-aware history can tell the two apart. The final benchmark, published in February 2025, came in smaller, at about −589,000 for March 2024, which is itself a reminder that the history keeps moving.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/ces/notices/2024/2024-preliminary-benchmark-revision.htm\">Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2024 preliminary benchmark revision notice, 21 Aug 2024</a> (−818,000, −0.5% of total nonfarm employment, for March 2024); <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/ces/publications/benchmark/ces-benchmark-revision-2024.pdf\">BLS, CES benchmark revision 2024</a> (final revision); <a href=\"https://alfred.stlouisfed.org/\">ALFRED, Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis</a>, for data vintages.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"policy-gradients-actor-critic","title":"Policy Gradients &amp; Actor-Critic Methods","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative backtest review</div> <h3>Your sizing agent looks brilliant. What do you check first?</h3> <p>A colleague trained an actor-critic agent to size a futures position. The reward is raw daily P&amp;L. The environment is a historical simulator that fills every order at the mid price with no costs. In the backtest the agent's Sharpe ratio is far above anything your team has run. The agent tends to sit near maximum size and rarely cuts. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Deploy at small size. The optimizer clearly found something real.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The optimizer found the best policy for the problem it was given. That problem had no risk penalty and free fills, so it has no reason to avoid tail risk or trading costs. Small size does not fix a wrong objective.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Fix the problem before trusting the answer: add costs and impact to the simulator, put a drawdown or tail-risk penalty in the reward, then retrain and test walk-forward.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's central warning. Reinforcement learning solves the decision problem you specified. If the reward lacks a risk penalty and the simulator lacks realism, the agent exploits both, and the backtest measures those gaps rather than an edge.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch to PPO so updates are more stable, then retrain.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">PPO does constrain how far each update moves the policy, which helps with instability. But it optimizes the same flawed reward in the same unrealistic simulator just as faithfully, only more smoothly.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add more training episodes so the critic's baseline becomes more accurate.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A better critic reduces the noise in the gradient. It does nothing about what the gradient points toward. More training on the wrong objective gives a more confident version of the wrong policy.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this shows.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a real episode. Its lesson is the one the page states plainly: a policy gradient is an optimizer, not an edge. Published uses that worked, such as Moody and Saffell's Sharpe-style reward net of costs and deep hedging's costs-included risk objective, put the costs and the risk inside the problem first.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"primer-correlation-regression","title":"Correlation &amp; Linear Regression","date":"2015-01-14","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-01-14\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 14 January 2015</div> <h3>A near-perfect hedge ratio, built on a policy</h3> <p>Since September 2011 the Swiss National Bank has held EUR/CHF at a floor of 1.20. Because the franc is tied to the euro, USD/CHF and EUR/USD have moved almost exactly opposite each other for three years. You are long EUR/USD and hedge it with USD/CHF, using a regression slope fitted over that window. The fit is superb: the correlation is close to minus one. What does the regression tell you about tomorrow?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">With a correlation this close to minus one over three years, the hedge is near-perfect and will stay that way.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A correlation describes the window it was measured in. This one exists because of a policy, the floor, not because of anything permanent in the two currencies. The number cannot tell you whether the cause will last.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">The lockstep comes from the floor, not from nature. Treat the slope as a description of the past window and size the trade as if the hedge could fail.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's trap box in action: a high correlation proves only that two things moved together over the sample, not that the relationship survives into the next window. Ask what is causing the correlation, and what happens if it goes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Re-fit the slope over a longer window that includes the years before the floor.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Mixing regimes gives a different, less flattering slope, which is a useful warning. But it is still a summary of the past. It does not tell you what happens on the day the floor goes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Increase the position. The hedge removes almost all the risk.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A hedge is only as good as the relationship behind it. Leaning harder on a relationship that a single policy decision could end is exactly backwards.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On Thursday 15 January 2015 the Swiss National Bank scrapped the 1.20 floor without warning. Within minutes the franc surged against both the euro and the dollar, and EUR/CHF traded far below 1.10 that day. The lockstep the regression had measured for three years broke in a morning. A slope fitted over a window describes that window; it is not a law.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/mmr/reference/pre_20150115/source/pre_20150115.en.pdf\">Swiss National Bank press release, 15 Jan 2015</a>; <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/mmr/reference/pre_20110906/source/pre_20110906.en.pdf\">Swiss National Bank press release setting the 1.20 minimum, 6 Sep 2011</a>; JPMorgan Chase Institute, \"Timing central bank announcements\" (EUR/CHF from 1.201 to 0.895 in about 24 minutes).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"primer-probability","title":"Probability &amp; Random Variables","date":"2007-08-13","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2007-08-13\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 13 August 2007</div> <h3>Several \"impossible\" days in a row</h3> <p>You run a quantitative equity strategy. For several days running, your daily loss has been many standard deviations, on a risk model that treats each day as an independent draw from a bell curve. On that model, even one such day should essentially never happen. It is Monday morning. A colleague says: \"A streak like this is so unlikely it can't go on. Today has to be calm.\" What do you conclude?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree: after a run like that, a calm day is due.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">If days really were independent, the past would say nothing about today, so nothing is \"due\". And they are not independent: big days cluster. This mixes up both ideas at once.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">It is just extreme bad luck on independent days. Keep full size.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">On independent normal days, several such moves in a row is so unlikely that the far better explanation is that the model's assumptions are wrong. Keeping full size bets on the broken model.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Big days come in bunches, so the days are not independent. Expect more turbulence and cut size until volatility settles.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is volatility clustering, the exact way the lesson says real returns break independence. A wild day makes another wild day more likely, so the safe reading of a streak is \"this may continue\", not \"this must stop\".</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Re-estimate σ from the last few days and keep the same model.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A higher σ is a step in the right direction. But it keeps the assumption that each day is a fresh, independent draw, which is the part that just failed.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In early August 2007, many quantitative equity funds lost heavily over several consecutive days, an episode later called the \"quant quake\". Goldman Sachs's chief financial officer, David Viniar, told the Financial Times they were seeing \"25-standard deviation moves, several days in a row.\" On independent bell-curve days that is essentially impossible. A run of them is the signature of what this lesson warns about: dependence between days and fatter tails than the normal allows.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: Financial Times, 13 Aug 2007, interview with Goldman Sachs CFO David Viniar (the wording here is taken from secondary reports, not the article itself); <a href=\"https://www.bis.org/review/r090219d.pdf\">Andrew Haldane, \"Why banks failed the stress test\", Bank of England, Feb 2009 (BIS copy)</a>; <a href=\"https://web.mit.edu/~alo/www/Papers/august07.html\">Khandani and Lo, \"What Happened to the Quants in August 2007?\", <em>Journal of Investment Management</em> 5 (2007)</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"primer-returns-compounding","title":"Returns, Log-Returns &amp; Compounding","date":"2018-02-05","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2018-02-05\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 5 February 2018</div> <h3>A product that compounds every day, after a very good year</h3> <p>It is Monday 5 February 2018, before the US open. You hold an inverse-volatility note: each day it returns minus one times a VIX-futures index, then resets. Through the calm of 2017 it compounded strongly, and its average daily return looks excellent. You are deciding how big a position to keep. How should you size it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Size it on the average daily return. It has been excellent, so a big position is justified.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The arithmetic average says nothing about what one terrible day does to compounded wealth. Your result is a product of daily gross returns, and a single day near −100% wipes out the whole product, whatever the average was.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Size it so that losing almost all of it in one day is survivable.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson in one rule. The note compounds daily, so one day near −100% leaves almost nothing to compound from, and no run of good days can rebuild it. Size for the day the product goes to zero, not for the average day.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the size, but plan to cut if the VIX rises sharply over several days.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A sensible instinct, but it assumes the damage arrives slowly. A daily-reset product can lose almost everything in one session, before any multi-day plan kicks in.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to it. Volatility drag is a slow, long-run cost, and you only plan to hold for a few weeks.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Drag is the slow part of the risk. The fast part is a single jump day far outside the small-move zone, where the drag formula stops applying and the exact product, with its −100% floor, takes over.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> That afternoon the VIX index more than doubled in a single session, its largest one-day percentage rise on record. Credit Suisse's XIV note, built to return the inverse of a VIX-futures index each day, lost more than 90% of its value in one evening. That triggered a clause letting the issuer redeem it, and it was wound up. A similar ProShares fund survived but later cut its target from −1× to −0.5× the daily index.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://credit-suisse.com/articles/media-releases/2018/02/en/credit-suisse-announces-event-acceleration-xiv-etn.html\">Credit Suisse, \"Credit Suisse AG Announces Event Acceleration of its XIV ETNs\", 6 Feb 2018</a>; <a href=\"https://www.etf.com/sections/news/inverse-vix-etn-shuts-down\">ETF.com, \"Inverse VIX ETN Shuts Down\", Feb 2018</a> (VIX up 115.6% in a day; XIV down about 96%); ProShares notice of the SVXY objective change from −1× to −0.5×, effective at the close on 27 Feb 2018.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"primer-stats-normal","title":"Descriptive Statistics &amp; the Normal Distribution","date":"1987-10-19","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1987-10-19\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 19 October 1987</div> <h3>A day your bell curve says cannot happen</h3> <p>It is the close on Monday 19 October 1987. The Dow Jones Industrial Average has just fallen 22.6% in a single day. Suppose your risk model measures the index's daily σ at about 1% and treats returns as normal. On that ruler today is a move of more than 20σ, and the bell says a move beyond even 3σ should come about once in 370 days. What do you conclude?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">A 20σ day is impossible under a normal curve, so the price data must be wrong. Ignore it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The data is right; the curve is the problem. When reality and a model disagree this violently, it is the model's assumption, normal tails, that has failed.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">It was a once-in-the-age-of-the-universe event, so it will not happen again. Keep sizing as before.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That rarity is only what the normal curve claims. Real returns have fatter tails, so extreme days come far more often than the bell allows. Sizing as if they cannot happen is how books get wiped out.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">The normal curve understates the tails. Keep z-scores as a ruler, but size and stress-test for moves far beyond 3σ.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's own advice. The bell is a good common scale for \"how unusual is this move\", and a poor promise about how often extremes come. Plan for fat tails on purpose.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Recompute σ over a window that includes the crash, and carry on with the normal model.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A fresh σ is sensible, since volatility clearly jumped. But a bigger σ inside a normal curve still has thin tails. It fixes the scale, not the shape.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The Dow's 22.6% fall on 19 October 1987 is still its largest one-day percentage drop. Mandelbrot had shown in 1963 that price changes have far fatter tails than the normal curve allows, and after 1987 that point was impossible to ignore. The bell survived as the baseline for z-scores and value-at-risk; fat-tail corrections start from it. The 1% σ here is an assumption for the drill, not a measured figure.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: Report of the Presidential Task Force on Market Mechanisms (the Brady Report), January 1988 (the Dow fell 508 points, or 22.6%, on 19 October 1987); Benoit Mandelbrot, \"The Variation of Certain Speculative Prices\", <em>Journal of Business</em> 36(4), 1963.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"process-vs-outcome-decision-journals","title":"Judge the Decision, Not the Result","date":"2015-02-01","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-02-01\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 1 February 2015</div> <h3>The pass at the one-yard line</h3> <p>Super Bowl XLIX. Seattle trail New England 28–24 with under half a minute left, second down, the ball on New England's one-yard line. Most people expect a run. Seattle's coach calls a pass. It is intercepted at the goal line, and New England win. By the next morning the call is being described as one of the worst in Super Bowl history. You are asked to grade the decision. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Call it a terrible decision. It lost the Super Bowl, and the result proves it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is resulting: grading the decision by its outcome. Had the pass been caught, the same call made with the same information would be called clever. The result is one draw, not a verdict.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Grade it on what was known before the snap: how rare interceptions are from there, the clock, and how many chances each call left. Grade the result separately.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This splits the two axes the lesson's grid separates. The decision is judged on the reasoning available at the time, and the outcome is noted as an outcome. If the reasoning was sound, the interception lands in \"bad luck\".</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Refuse to judge it at all. It was all luck either way.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than resulting, but process over outcome is not \"nothing can be judged\". The decision can be graded on its reasoning, and over many such calls results do test whether the reasoning is good.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Say you would have run the ball, as anyone could see.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is hindsight bias talking. Knowing the ending makes it feel predictable, and you do not notice the shift. Unless you wrote it down before the snap, you cannot know what you would have called.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Russell Wilson's pass was intercepted by Malcolm Butler and New England won. The call was widely panned the next day. Annie Duke opens <em>Thinking in Bets</em> with this play. Her argument is not that the call was certainly right, but that the verdict was pure resulting: interceptions from that position are rare, an incomplete pass stops the clock, and a reasonable case exists that passing gave Seattle more total chances to score.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: NFL.com game report and play-by-play for Super Bowl XLIX, 1 Feb 2015 (Seattle trailed 28–24, second-and-goal from the New England 1 with about 26 seconds left; Wilson's pass was intercepted by Malcolm Butler); NPR, \"Watch the interception that won the Super Bowl\", 2 Feb 2015; Annie Duke, <em>Thinking in Bets</em> (Portfolio, 2018), chapter 1.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"purchasing-power-parity","title":"Purchasing Power Parity","date":"2022-01-03","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-01-03\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · early January 2022</div> <h3>The yen looks cheap on every PPP measure</h3> <p>You trade on a horizon of a few months. USD/JPY is around 115. The yen looks cheap on every PPP measure, US inflation is running well above Japan's, so relative PPP says the yen should gain. The Fed has not yet started hiking; the Bank of Japan holds its short rate at −0.1% and caps 10-year yields. An analyst note calls the yen \"20% undervalued\". What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy yen with leverage sized for a three-month trade. Cheap plus the inflation gap is a strong case.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">With a half-life of three to five years, a 20% gap is still about 19% after three months, and it can widen first. PPP gives a direction for the long run and no timing at all.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy yen, and add each time it gets cheaper, since the gap has to close.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Nothing makes a cheap currency stop getting cheaper. Adding into a widening gap turns a slow anchor into a fast drawdown.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Take no position on PPP alone. On a few-month horizon, weigh what moves currencies that fast, here the rate gap that is about to open, and keep PPP as a long-run anchor.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">PPP's usefulness is horizon-conditional. Over months, capital chasing an interest-rate gap is far larger and faster than goods arbitrage, and a Fed about to hike against a Bank of Japan pinned below zero points the other way.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy a small amount of yen as a multi-year position you can hold through a much cheaper yen.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible if your horizon really is years and the size survives a large move against you. It is no longer a trade on your few-month book.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The Fed raised its policy rate from near zero in March 2022 to 5.25–5.50% by July 2023, while the Bank of Japan kept its short rate at −0.1% until March 2024. USD/JPY rose to near 152 that October and passed 160 in 2024, the weakest yen against the dollar since the mid-1980s. Japan's Ministry of Finance bought yen in autumn 2022, its first yen-buying intervention since 1998, and again in 2024; that slowed the fall without reversing it. Anyone who bought the yen in 2022 because it was \"20% cheap\" sat through a much cheaper yen first.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220316a.htm\">Federal Reserve press release, 16 Mar 2022</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20230726a.htm\">Federal Reserve press release, 26 Jul 2023</a>; <a href=\"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/mopo/mpmdeci/mpr_2024/k240319a.pdf\">Bank of Japan, Changes in the Monetary Policy Framework, 19 Mar 2024</a>; <a href=\"https://www.mof.go.jp/english/policy/international_policy/reference/feio/foreign_exchange_intervention_operations.csv\">Japan Ministry of Finance, foreign exchange intervention data</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DEXJPUS\">FRED, DEXJPUS</a>; Reuters, 21 Oct 2022 (USD/JPY high of 151.94)</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"q-learning-temporal-difference","title":"Q-Learning &amp; Temporal-Difference Methods","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative execution desk</div> <h3>Your learned table says \"be aggressive here\". Do you trust it?</h3> <p>You trained a tabular Q-learning agent on a year of logged order-book data to place limit orders. The state is time left and shares left, plus the spread. In one state, the table rates an aggressive limit price far above every other action. You check the logs: that state-action pair was tried only three times, while its neighbors were tried thousands of times. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trust it. The table is the greedy policy, so the highest value is the right move.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">With three samples the estimate is mostly noise, and the update always takes the maximum over noisy guesses, which runs high. A rarely visited action that tops the table is exactly where the overestimation bias bites hardest.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat it as unproven: gather more experience in that state, check it with a less biased estimate such as Double Q-learning, and only then let it drive orders.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Convergence needs every state-action pair visited enough, and the max in the target is upward biased. More samples and a de-biased estimate address both problems before real orders depend on it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Never take that action again, since it is untested.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safe, but it stops learning. With no exploration the table can never find out whether that action really is better. Exploration is part of the convergence guarantee, not an optional extra.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Raise the learning rate so the table learns faster from the samples it has.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A bigger step size makes each noisy sample move the guess further. It speeds up learning but makes three data points even louder, the opposite of what this estimate needs.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this shows.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a real episode. The lesson's real execution result, Nevmyvaka, Feng and Kearns (2006), learned order placement from historical order-book data. The traps here are the lesson's own: too little exploration and the upward bias of the max.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"qe-qt-balance-sheet","title":"QE and QT: What the Fed's Balance Sheet Actually Does","date":"2013-05-22","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2013-05-22\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 22 May 2013</div> <h3>A hint that the buying will slow</h3> <p>You hold long-dated US Treasuries and some emerging-market bonds. The Fed is in the middle of QE3, buying every month. Today Chairman Bernanke tells Congress that the Fed could begin to slow the pace of its purchases within the next few meetings. Nothing has been sold and nothing has stopped. A colleague says: \"The Fed is still buying, so nothing has changed for yields.\" What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold everything. Purchases are unchanged, so yields cannot rise until the Fed actually buys less.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This ignores the signalling channel. A long yield includes the expected path of policy rates, and a hint that purchases will slow moves that expectation today, before any bond changes hands.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy more long bonds while the Fed is still a buyer.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You would be adding duration just as the market starts repricing the policy path. The Fed's flow has not changed, but the expected end of it has.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Trim a little now and wait for the actual taper decision before doing more.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A step in the right direction, but it treats the announcement as noise until the purchases change. Markets reprice on the hint, so most of the move can happen before the decision.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut duration and emerging-market exposure now. The hint alone moves the expected-path part of long yields, and the term premium the Fed was compressing can come back.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This reads the right channels. Signalling works on announcements, not on purchases, and assets that rallied on QE are the ones most exposed when it is expected to slow.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The 10-year yield climbed from around 1.6% in early May to near 3% by September, and emerging-market currencies and bonds sold off hard. The actual taper did not begin until January 2014. Nothing had been sold; the move came from markets repricing the expected path of policy on the hint that purchases would slow.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/testimony/bernanke20130522a.htm\">Bernanke, testimony to the Joint Economic Committee, 22 May 2013</a>; Associated Press, 22 May 2013 (\"in the next few meetings\", from the hearing's questions); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DGS10\">FRED, DGS10</a> (1.66% on 1 May 2013, 2.98% on 5 Sep 2013); <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20131218a.htm\">Federal Reserve press release, 18 Dec 2013</a></p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"quantile-regression","title":"Quantile Regression","date":"2008-09-15","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2008-09-15\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 15 September 2008</div> <h3>The central forecast barely moved. Do you trust it?</h3> <p>Lehman Brothers has filed for bankruptcy this morning. Credit spreads are widening, volatility is rising and funding markets are seizing up: every input to a financial conditions index is tightening at once. Your economists' central forecast for next year's US growth has been cut, but only in a small step, and it still shows modest growth. You size a macro book on next year's growth. What do you size it on?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The central forecast. It has only moved a little, so the outlook has only changed a little.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A central forecast is a conditional mean. Tight financial conditions mostly change how bad things can get, not the typical outcome, so the mean is the number least likely to show the danger.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">The lower tail of the growth outlook given today's conditions: ask how far the bad-case quantile has fallen, and cut risk to that, even if the median looks steady.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the conditional-quantile question. The same predictor can move the 5th percentile far more than the median, and only a tail-targeted fit shows it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">A fixed downside buffer taken from the long-run spread of past growth outcomes.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than the mean alone, but it is a static adjustment: the same buffer every year, blind to the fact that today's conditions have just made the bad tail much fatter.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Wait for the economists to cut the central forecast properly before changing anything.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Central forecasts tend to be cut in steps as data arrive. By the time the mean has caught up, the bad outcome may already be under way.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Central forecasts were cut, but in steps, and the recession that followed was far deeper than the central path had suggested. US output fell at an annualised rate of about 8.5% in the fourth quarter of 2008, the worst quarter since 1958. Almost a decade later, \"Vulnerable Growth\" (Adrian, Boyarchenko and Giannone, 2019) fitted quantile regressions of future US growth on financial conditions and found exactly this pattern: as conditions tightened, the lower quantiles fell sharply while the median barely moved. The IMF built its Growth-at-Risk framework on the idea. Later out-of-sample work, such as Plagborg-Møller, Reichlin, Ricco and Hasenzagl (2020), found financial variables add little to forecasts of growth risk beyond the very near term.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/A191RL1Q225SBEA\">FRED, real GDP growth (A191RL1Q225SBEA)</a>; Reuters, 15 Sep 2008 (Lehman Brothers' bankruptcy filing); Adrian, Boyarchenko and Giannone, \"Vulnerable Growth\", <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20161923\">American Economic Review 109(4), 2019</a>; IMF, Global Financial Stability Report, October 2017; <a href=\"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/when-is-growth-at-risk\">Plagborg-Møller et al., \"When Is Growth at Risk?\", Brookings Papers on Economic Activity, 2020</a></p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"random-matrix-theory","title":"Random Matrix Theory &amp; the Marchenko-Pastur Law","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Monday</div> <h3>A colleague wants to hedge the \"euro factor\"</h3> <p>Your risk report runs a principal component analysis on 30 FX pairs over the last 250 days. The first eigenvalue is 8.2, the dollar factor. The second is 1.6, and its loadings look like a euro block. A colleague proposes adding a separate hedge for this \"euro factor\" to the book. What do you say?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree. It is the second-biggest factor in the basket, so it must matter.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">\"Second-biggest\" is not the test. At \\(q = 0.12\\) the noise band runs up to about 1.81, so 1.6 is exactly what 30 unrelated series over 250 days could throw up by chance. Hedging it is trading noise.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Check it against the noise band first. At 1.6 it sits inside 0.43 to 1.81, so treat it as unproven, and only revisit it with more independent history.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the rule. Inside the band does not mean the factor is fake; it means this sample cannot tell it from noise. More data shrinks \\(q\\) and narrows the band, which is the honest way to find out.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Rebuild the matrix from overlapping weekly returns to get more observations, then test again.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Overlapping windows add rows, not independent information. Plugging the inflated count into \\(q\\) makes the band look narrower than the data supports, so a noise factor can look significant.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Reject the hedge and drop the second factor from every report for good.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Not hedging is right for now, but \"inside the band\" is a statement about this sample's power, not proof of no structure. A real but modest factor can hide there at small \\(T\\).</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happens.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's worked numbers, not a dated episode. With \\(N = 30\\) and \\(T = 250\\), only \\(\\lambda_1 = 8.2\\) clears the band. The same check on large US stock matrices in 1999 found that almost every eigenvalue sat inside the band, with only the market mode and a few sector modes clearly outside it.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"recurrent-networks-lstm-gru","title":"Recurrent Networks for Sequential Data: LSTM &amp; GRU","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Friday</div> <h3>The LSTM beats everything in the backtest</h3> <p>You built a small LSTM that reads the last 20 daily EUR/USD returns, one bar at a time, and forecasts tomorrow's direction. On your test period it beats the feedforward network you tried first. A colleague wants to scale it up: more layers, more units, a longer window. What do you do next?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Scale it up. The architecture clearly works, and bigger models learn more.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Financial series are short and noisy, and an LSTM already has many more parameters than a feedforward model on the same window. Making it bigger mostly buys more capacity to fit noise.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-check how the 20-bar windows were built for leakage, then run a walk-forward test against simple baselines, such as an ARIMA model or boosted trees on the same lags, net of costs.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Order mattering in principle is not the same as this model winning in practice. Point-in-time windows and an honest walk-forward comparison against cheaper models are what turn \"it beat one network\" into evidence.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch to a transformer, the newer architecture.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Attention earns its keep on long sequences with plenty of data. On a 20-bar window of noisy returns it adds appetite for data, not evidence of an edge, and the testing problem is unchanged.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Swap in a GRU to cut the parameter count.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Fewer parameters is a reasonable hedge against overfitting on a small dataset. But it is another model choice, not a test, and still needs the same honest comparison.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This day is illustrative, not a historical event. It applies the lesson's own pitfalls: a flexible sequence model on a short, noisy history overfits easily, and window construction can leak. In the published record, Fischer and Krauss (2018) found an LSTM ranking S&amp;P 500 stocks beat a random forest and logistic regression, yet its returns faded sharply after about 2010.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"regime-switching-state-space","title":"Regime-Switching State Space Models","date":"2008-12-01","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2008-12-01\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 1 December 2008</div> <h3>The committee confirms what the model said months ago</h3> <p>Today the NBER announces that the US recession began in December 2007, nearly a year ago. Jeremy Piger's real-time run of a dynamic-factor Markov-switching model of four monthly indicators, the kind he and Chauvet tested, first put the recession probability above 50% in its 1 November 2008 report. You want a rule for next time: turn defensive when the model's recession probability crosses 50%. To backtest that rule over past cycles, which probability history do you use?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The smoothed recession probabilities published on FRED, computed on today's revised data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Smoothed probabilities use data from after each month, and revised data were not available at the time either. The chart lines up with the recessions more neatly than any real-time reading did, so the backtest would flatter the rule.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Filtered probabilities, recomputed month by month from the data as first released.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is what a live model could actually have said each month. It is how Chauvet and Piger judged real-time performance, and it is the only honest test of a trading rule.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The NBER's own recession dates.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The dates are fixed long after the fact: this recession's start was announced about a year late, and its June 2009 end only in September 2010. Using them in a backtest is lookahead of a year or more.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">The smoothed probabilities, but with the signal delayed by a month to allow for lag.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It admits the problem but guesses at its size. Smoothing and revisions do not create a fixed one-month lag, so the real-time history is still the right test.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The NBER announced on 1 December 2008 that the recession had begun in December 2007; its end, in June 2009, was announced in September 2010. Run live, Piger's filtered probability crossed 50% on 1 November 2008, then fell back to 16% a month later when October's data showed industrial production and income growing again. The authors' rule, three months above 80%, would have declared the recession on 1 January 2009, a month after the NBER, and its end in January 2010, eight months before. The smoothed chart of the same model on today's data looks far cleaner than that. In 2020 the model's recession probability reached near certainty with the data available in May, and the NBER announced the February peak on 8 June 2020. A regime call describes the backdrop; whether it pays as a trade is a separate test.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.nber.org/news/business-cycle-dating-committee-announcement-december-1-2008\">NBER announcement, 1 Dec 2008</a>; <a href=\"https://www.nber.org/news/business-cycle-dating-committee-announcement-september-20-2010\">NBER announcement, 20 Sep 2010</a>; <a href=\"https://www.nber.org/news/business-cycle-dating-committee-announcement-june-8-2020\">NBER announcement, 8 Jun 2020</a>; Chauvet and Piger, Journal of Business &amp; Economic Statistics 26(1), 2008; <a href=\"https://www.nber.org/papers/w16162\">Hamilton, \"Calling Recessions in Real Time\", NBER Working Paper 16162, 2010</a> (Piger's real-time reports, 2008–10); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/RECPROUSM156N\">FRED, RECPROUSM156N</a> and its ALFRED vintage of 1 May 2020</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"regression-diagnostics","title":"Regression Diagnostics: Durbin-Watson, Breusch-Pagan &amp; Jarque-Bera","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research review</div> <h3>A colleague brings you a p-value of 0.01</h3> <p>A colleague regressed next-day FX returns on a yield-spread signal over about 500 trading days. The slope has a p-value of 0.01 and they want to start building a strategy on it. You run the residual checks: Durbin-Watson is 0.6, Breusch-Pagan is unremarkable, and Jarque-Bera shows mild skew. What do you tell them?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Go ahead. Two of the three checks pass, and p = 0.01 clears the bar comfortably.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">One failure is enough. A Durbin-Watson of 0.6 means \\(\\hat\\rho \\approx 0.7\\) and only about 88 independent days, so the p-value was computed as if there were 500. The comfortable margin is the part that is fake.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Drop it. The diagnostics failed, so the relationship is spurious.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A diagnostic failure says the standard error is wrong, not that the slope is meaningless. Throwing the coefficient away is the opposite mistake to trusting the p-value.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Recompute the inference with Newey-West standard errors, report that p-value instead, and if anything survives, test it out of sample before building anything.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This fixes the specific failure without discarding the estimate, and it keeps the two questions apart: is the in-sample claim honestly computed, and does the relationship hold on data the model has not seen.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Note the low Durbin-Watson, quote the p-value with a caveat, and move on.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Honest, but a caveat does not tell anyone how much the evidence shrinks. The corrected number is a few lines of work and decides whether there is anything here at all.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the numbers say.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own scenario, not a historical episode. With \\(\\hat\\rho \\approx 0.7\\), 500 days carry the evidence of about 88, and once the standard error is corrected the p-value is nowhere near 0.01. In a simulation of exactly this setup with no real link between signal and returns, the classical test called the slope significant at 5% in about two samples out of five; Newey-West cut that to about one in nine.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"regularized-regression-lasso-ridge","title":"Regularized Regression: LASSO &amp; Ridge","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative month-end refit</div> <h3>LASSO changed its mind about the yield curve</h3> <p>You run the lesson's FX model: fifteen standardized macro predictors for a currency pair's monthly return, with \\(\\lambda\\) chosen by cross-validation. Last month LASSO kept six predictors, including the 5-year spread, and zeroed the 2-year and 10-year spreads. This month's refit, on one extra month of data, keeps the 10-year spread instead and zeroes the 5-year. A colleague wants to write up \"the 10-year spread is the real driver\". What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree. LASSO selected it, so the 10-year spread is the true driver.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The tenor spreads move almost in lockstep, and among correlated predictors LASSO's pick is close to a coin flip. One fit's selected list is not a validated claim about which predictor really matters.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Check how often each spread is selected across bootstrap resamples, and compare with an Elastic Net fit that keeps the correlated group together.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's own remedy. Selection stability tells you whether the pick means anything, and Elastic Net stops the arbitrary all-or-nothing choice among a correlated group.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Lower \\(\\lambda\\) until both spreads stay in, using in-sample \\(R^2\\) to decide.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">In-sample fit always prefers \\(\\lambda = 0\\), so this quietly undoes the regularization. \\(\\lambda\\) has to come from held-out folds.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch to Ridge so all fifteen predictors stay in, shrunk.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible if you believe most of the fifteen carry a little signal: Ridge spreads weight across the correlated spreads instead of picking one. But you lose the short, interpretable model, and you still have not tested which view fits the data better.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's fifteen-predictor example, not a historical event. A flip like this is exactly what the lesson warns about: when predictors come in correlated groups, LASSO's choice within a group is unstable, so treat its selection as informative, not definitive, and test stability before you tell a story about it.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"rehypothecation-collateral-chains","title":"Rehypothecation &amp; Collateral Chains","date":"2011-10","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2011-10\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · October 2011</div> <h3>Your futures broker is in the headlines</h3> <p>You keep a futures account, with spare cash well beyond what your open positions need, at MF Global, one of the largest US futures brokers. The euro-area debt crisis is deepening, and the firm holds a large position, reported at about $6.3 billion, in short-dated bonds of Italy, Spain and other euro-area governments. In late October it reports a quarterly loss and is downgraded by the rating agencies. Other customers are starting to move their accounts. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Stay put. US rules require customer funds to be segregated, so your money is safe whatever happens to the firm.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Segregation is a legal right drawn in contracts and ledgers, not a property of the cash. A firm in a run needs cash today, and client money is the nearest liquid pool. The rule tells you what should happen, not what will.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Move the account, or at least the spare cash, to another broker now, and keep only what open positions need.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It cuts your exposure to one firm's failure to the minimum you need to trade. Spreading collateral across brokers is exactly the practice the industry adopted after Lehman.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Stay, but ask the firm to confirm in writing that your assets are fully segregated.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A sensible check, and it costs nothing. But a confirmation is another claim on paper; it does not move your cash out of reach if the firm's books turn out to be wrong.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Shift the account to the firm's London affiliate, away from the US headlines.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That swaps US protections for UK client-money rules and a separate UK administration if the group fails. Moving within the same group is not moving away from it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Counterparties demanded more margin on the bond financing while customers pulled their accounts, and cash left from both ends. MF Global filed for bankruptcy on 31 October 2011. The trustee found a shortfall of roughly $1.6 billion in the customer accounts that were meant to be segregated; the regulator's later case was that in its last week the firm had used customer money to support its own operations and those of its affiliates. Customers who traded through the London affiliate were caught in a separate UK administration. US customers were eventually repaid essentially in full, but only after years of proceedings.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.cftc.gov/PressRoom/PressReleases/pr6776-13\">CFTC press release 6776-13, 18 Nov 2013</a>; <a href=\"https://www.cftc.gov/PressRoom/PressReleases/pr6904-14\">CFTC press release 6904-14, 3 Apr 2014</a>; SIPA trustee James Giddens, investigation report, Feb 2012 (the $1.6 billion shortfall estimate); Reuters, 31 Oct 2011 (bankruptcy filing and the $6.3 billion euro-bond position)</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"research-pipeline-hypothesis-to-backtest","title":"The Research Pipeline: Hypothesis to Honest Backtest","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research afternoon</div> <h3>The out-of-sample number comes back just under the bar</h3> <p>You followed the pipeline. The mechanism was written down, the zero-parameter baseline recorded, three trials logged, and the bar fixed in advance: an out-of-sample Sharpe above 0.30 on at least 30 trades. The test comes back at 0.27 on 31 trades. A colleague says 0.27 is respectable for currencies, and suggests adding a volatility filter and rerunning on the same holdout. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Call it supported. 0.27 is close enough, and decent for this asset class.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The bar was 0.30 and it was written down first. Deciding after the fact that a lower number counts is moving the goalposts to where the ball landed. That is the one thing pre-registration exists to prevent.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add the volatility filter and rerun on the same holdout until it clears 0.30.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The holdout has now been used to choose the rule, so it is no longer out of sample. This is the red loop in the pipeline diagram: a quiet return to Stage 3 using the test data as a second round of tuning.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Bank it as not supported, exactly as written. If the filter has its own mechanism, log it as trial 4 and test it on data the holdout never touched.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The verdict stays a visible null. A new idea is a new hypothesis, with its trial counted and its own untouched test data, so nothing leaks from this result into the next one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Bank it as not supported, then drop the project without writing it up.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The verdict is honest, but an unrecorded null is a hidden trial. Anyone who later tests a similar idea will undercount how many variations have been tried, and their multiple-testing correction will be too generous.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This drill is illustrative, not a real episode. The discipline is real, though: when large NHLBI clinical trials had to declare their main outcome in advance from 2000, the share reporting a benefit fell from 17 of 30 (57%) to 2 of 25 (8%). Writing the bar down first is what lets a null stay a null.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"risk-parity-erc","title":"Risk Parity &amp; Equal Risk Contribution","date":"2020-03-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 March 2020</div> <h3>Your \"balanced\" book is suddenly far over its risk target</h3> <p>You run a risk-parity fund: equities, bonds and commodities sized to equal risk contributions, with leverage on the large bond sleeve. Your weights come from a covariance matrix estimated on a calm year. Today the VIX closed at a record 82.69. For several days long Treasury yields have been rising while stocks fall, so the bond sleeve is losing at the same time as equities. Measured on recent data, the portfolio's volatility is far above target, and most of the excess comes from the bond sleeve. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold the weights. On the model's covariance matrix the risk contributions are still equal.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">They are equal only on stale data. The split is exact for the matrix you feed it, and the matrix has changed in days. Your real risk is far above what the mandate allows.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the bond sleeve. Treasuries are the safe haven and must rally.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The diversifier has stopped diversifying for now. Adding leverage to the sleeve whose risk contribution just ballooned doubles down on the assumption that has already failed.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-estimate risk on recent data and bring volatility back toward target in planned steps, starting with the levered bond sleeve, accepting the cost.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The rule exists to keep risk at its target, and the current risk says cut. Doing it in planned steps, knowing that every fund running similar rules is selling the same assets, limits how much your own selling costs you.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Cut everything back to target at today's close, in one go.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Right direction, wrong execution. Selling everything at once, alongside every other fund on the same rule, means paying the worst prices: the de-levering is itself a market event.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The S&amp;P 500 fell about a third from its February peak to its low on 23 March. Some market strategists at the time described risk-parity and vol-targeting funds as forced sellers of equities and bonds into the fall; others argued their role was overstated. The selling was mechanical and came after the losses rather than before, and it left the funds with less exposure when markets rebounded from late March, after the Federal Reserve announced open-ended purchases on 23 March. Cutting cost money in hindsight; holding a book far over its risk target with no plan would have risked much worse had the fall continued.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\">FRED, VIXCLS</a> (closing high 82.69, 16 Mar 2020); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\">FRED, SP500</a> (3,386 on 19 Feb 2020, 2,237 on 23 Mar 2020); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DGS10\">FRED, DGS10</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200323b.htm\">Federal Reserve press release, 23 Mar 2020</a>; Bloomberg, March 2020, and Neuberger Berman, \"Risk Parity and the Fallacy of the Single Cause\" (the two sides on forced selling)</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"sabr-model","title":"The SABR Model","date":"2014-06-05","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2014-06-05\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 5 June 2014</div> <h3>The ECB takes its deposit rate below zero</h3> <p>The European Central Bank has just cut its deposit facility rate to −0.10%, below zero. Short-dated euro forward rates are close to zero and could go below it. Your euro swaption smiles are fitted with SABR at a backbone of \\(\\beta = 0.5\\). What do you do about the model?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. Rates have never stayed below zero for long, so the forwards will stay positive.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">If a forward goes negative, \\(F^{\\beta}\\) for a non-integer \\(\\beta\\) is not even defined. The model does not just misprice: it stops working on exactly the strikes the market now cares about.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Floor every forward at a tiny positive number so the formula keeps running.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The code runs, but every option near or below zero is now priced as if negative rates could not happen. That is a silent mispricing of the tail the market is actively trading.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Move to a model that allows negative forwards: a shifted SABR, or the normal \\(\\beta = 0\\) convention, and refit.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A shift adds a constant so the shifted forward stays positive, and \\(\\beta = 0\\) treats volatility as an absolute size of move that works at any rate level. Either keeps SABR's fast, closed-form fit while respecting what rates can now do.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep SABR but free up \\(\\beta\\) and refit it every day alongside \\(\\rho\\).<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It feels flexible, but \\(\\beta\\) and \\(\\rho\\) are close to redundant on skew, so a free fit is poorly identified, and it still does nothing about a negative forward.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The ECB cut its deposit rate to −0.10% on 5 June 2014, effective 11 June, and euro rates did go negative in the mid-2010s. Desks moved to shifted SABR, and in parts of the rates market the normal \\(\\beta = 0\\) convention became standard, because a fractional power of a negative forward has no meaning. Earlier the same year, Hagan and co-authors published \"Arbitrage-Free SABR\" to fix the formula's other weak spot, the far wings.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/pr/date/2014/html/pr140605.en.html\">ECB press release, 5 Jun 2014</a>; Hagan, Kumar, Lesniewski and Woodward, \"Arbitrage-Free SABR\", Wilmott, January 2014</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"sde-existence-uniqueness","title":"Existence &amp; Uniqueness of SDE Solutions","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative model review</div> <h3>A new local-volatility model that \"looks like GBM\"</h3> <p>A colleague proposes an FX local-volatility model with diffusion coefficient \\(\\sigma(x) = \\sigma_0 x^{1.5}\\), to fit the smile harder at high spot levels. Its Monte Carlo ran overnight without errors and the prices look sensible. It is locally Lipschitz away from zero, but it grows faster than a straight line as spot rises. You sign off model validation. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Approve it. The simulation ran cleanly and it is GBM with a slightly bigger exponent.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A fixed-step scheme can step straight over a blow-up and still print plausible paths. Well-posedness is a property of the continuous-time equation, not of one run, and this coefficient breaks the growth condition the standard theorem needs.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Hold approval until someone shows the process cannot explode in finite time for these parameters, or cites a result that does.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the gate the lesson describes. Failing linear growth means the textbook guarantee does not cover the model, so a separate non-explosion argument is real due diligence, not a formality.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Reject it outright. Any model that fails a condition of the theorem is broken.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Failing a sufficient condition does not prove failure. The CIR square root fails Lipschitz and is still a standard, well-posed model under a more careful theorem. The right response is a more careful check.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Cap the exponent so the coefficient turns linear above some spot level, then approve.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Capping does restore linear growth, so the standard theorem applies. But it is a different model from the one that was fitted, and the smile at high spot, the reason for the change, now needs refitting and rechecking.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This drill is illustrative, based on the lesson's own scenario, not a real episode. The real-world version of the check is routine: model validation asks whether a new model is well-posed, stays positive and has finite moments before it is used, and square-root models such as CIR and Heston are exactly where the answer depends on the fitted parameters.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"sde-numerical-schemes","title":"Numerical Schemes: Euler-Maruyama &amp; Milstein","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative pricing desk</div> <h3>Reusing the vanilla grid for a barrier</h3> <p>Your desk prices vanilla FX calls by Monte Carlo with Euler-Maruyama on a daily grid, and the prices have converged nicely. A client now asks for a down-and-in call on the same pair, which only comes alive if spot touches a lower barrier before expiry. A colleague suggests running the barrier through the same engine and the same daily grid. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use the same grid. It already gives converged vanilla prices.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The vanilla only needs the terminal distribution, where Euler-Maruyama is weak order 1.0. The barrier needs the path, where it is only strong order 0.5. A coarse path can step over a touch, so the knock-in probability is mispriced.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the grid and run ten times more paths to be safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More paths cut Monte Carlo noise, not discretization error. Every path still checks the barrier only once a day, so the bias from missed touches stays exactly where it was.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Run a convergence study for the barrier: price it on finer and finer grids until the price stops moving, and use that step size.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the standard check the lesson describes. It measures how much the path-sensitive price depends on the step, instead of borrowing a step size chosen for a different, endpoint-only payoff.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch to Milstein on the same daily grid.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Milstein improves pathwise accuracy to strong order 1.0, so it helps. But it still only sees the path once a day, so a convergence check on the step size is still needed before you trust the price.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This drill is illustrative, built on the lesson's own barrier scenario, not a dated episode. The rule it rests on is the lesson's: the same scheme and the same model need a different step size depending on whether the payoff cares about the path or only the endpoint.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"second-order-greeks","title":"Second-Order Greeks: Vanna, Charm, Vomma &amp; More","date":"2015-01-15","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-01-15\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 15 January 2015</div> <h3>A risk report that says the tail is a rounding error</h3> <p>It is the morning of 15 January 2015. EUR/CHF sits just above the Swiss National Bank's 1.20 floor, as it has since September 2011, and implied volatility on EUR/CHF options is very low. Your book is short out-of-the-money EUR/CHF puts, hedged flat on delta and vega, and the risk report shows Vanna and Vomma close to zero. A colleague wants to double the size: the bank has promised to defend the floor. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Double it. Flat on delta and vega, second-order terms near zero, and a central bank standing behind the floor.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The report's small terms are small only for small moves. A floor break moves price and vol hugely at the same time, so the vanna and vomma cross terms become the P&amp;L, and then the price gaps past every strike where no hedge can be adjusted.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Size the short-put book for a gap through every strike, or buy far-out puts to cap the loss, whatever the risk report says.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This sizes for the event the position is short, not for the small-move expansion. A cap or a size you can survive does not depend on Vanna, Vomma or any re-hedge working in a market with no prices.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the size but put a stop-loss order under the market.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A stop is better than nothing, but it assumes there will be a price near your stop. In a gap the order fills wherever the next trade happens, which can be far from where you placed it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Double it, and hedge Vanna and Vomma precisely with other options.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Neutralizing the second-order terms helps against moderate joint moves in price and vol. It does nothing once the move is so large that the whole expansion stops describing the book.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> That morning the SNB removed the floor without warning and cut its deposit rate to −0.75%. EUR/CHF traded as low as about 0.85 within minutes (the exact low varied by data feed), a fall of close to 30%, before recovering part of the way the same day, and many orders were filled far from where they had been placed. Implied volatility jumped from very low levels. FXCM needed a rescue loan of about $300 million from Leucadia, and Alpari's UK arm became insolvent.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/mmr/reference/pre_20110906/source/pre_20110906.en.pdf\">Swiss National Bank press release, 6 Sep 2011</a> (the 1.20 minimum rate); <a href=\"https://www.snb.ch/en/mmr/reference/pre_20150115/source/pre_20150115.en.pdf\">Swiss National Bank press release, 15 Jan 2015</a> (floor discontinued, rate cut to −0.75%); Reuters, 15 Jan 2015; <a href=\"https://www.financemagnates.com/forex/brokers/breaking-leucadia-hands-fxcm-300-mln-life-line/\">Finance Magnates, 16 Jan 2015</a> (FXCM and Leucadia); Reuters, 16 Jan 2015 (Alpari UK insolvency)</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"sharpe-family-deflated","title":"Sharpe, Sortino, Calmar &amp; the Deflated Sharpe Ratio","date":"1998","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1998\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · early 1998</div> <h3>The smoothest record you have ever seen</h3> <p>You allocate to hedge funds. Long-Term Capital Management, run by John Meriwether with Myron Scholes and Robert Merton among its partners, returned roughly 40% a year after fees in 1995 and 1996, with low reported volatility. Its Sharpe, Sortino and Calmar ratios all look excellent. Its trades are convergence bets on small spreads between nearly identical bonds, run at a balance sheet of the order of 25 times capital. How do you read those ratios?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Allocate more. Three excellent ratios, all agreeing, is as strong as evidence gets.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">All three were measured on the same calm sample. A short-liquidity book looks smooth until a flight to quality, and 1995 to 1997 contained none. Agreement between ratios is not independent evidence.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the ratios as untested: ask what a flight to safety would do to the spreads at 25 times leverage, and size for that.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Every ratio assumes the sample is a fair draw of the risks to come. This one had no storm in it. Asking what the payoff does in the missing storm is the only way to see the risk the ratios cannot.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Deflate the Sharpe ratio for how many strategies the fund might have tried, then decide.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Sensible habit, wrong problem. The Deflated Sharpe Ratio corrects for the search behind a number, not for tails the sample has not drawn yet. It would still have looked good.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Check the Calmar ratio. If the worst drawdown is small, the tail risk must be small.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Calmar uses the worst drawdown that happened in the sample. A sample with no stress shows a small worst drawdown. It measures the storms you have had, not the ones you have not.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In August 1998 Russia defaulted on its domestic debt. Investors ran for the safest, most liquid bonds, and the spreads LTCM was betting would narrow widened instead, across many of its trades at once. At that leverage the losses ran into billions within weeks. In September 1998 the Federal Reserve Bank of New York brought the fund's creditor banks together, and they put in about $3.6 billion to take over the portfolio and wind it down. Greenspan told Congress the fund had lost half its capital by the end of August, and its 1998 losses are commonly put at about $4.6 billion.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.cftc.gov/sites/default/files/tm/tmhedgefundreport.htm\">President's Working Group on Financial Markets, Hedge Funds, Leverage, and the Lessons of Long-Term Capital Management, April 1999</a> (returns, leverage of more than 25 to 1, the $3.6 billion recapitalisation of 23 Sep 1998, the Russian debt moratorium of 17 Aug 1998); <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/boarddocs/testimony/1998/19981001.htm\">Greenspan, testimony to the House Banking Committee, 1 Oct 1998</a></p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"short-premium-risk-and-sizing","title":"Short Premium: Risk, Margin &amp; Sizing","date":"2015-01-14","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-01-14\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · Wednesday 14 January 2015, evening</div> <h3>A floor has held for three years, and it pays to sell what is under it</h3> <p>EUR/CHF has traded at or above 1.20 since the Swiss National Bank set that minimum in September 2011, and the bank has defended it by buying foreign currency. You run a small FX account. You sell one-month EUR/CHF puts struck below 1.20, which pay little but almost always expire worthless, and you also hold a leveraged long EUR/CHF position. Your broker lets you use most of the account as margin. Tonight a colleague says the floor cannot be broken because the bank has promised to defend it. What do you do with the size?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold the size. The central bank has promised to defend the floor, so the downside is limited.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A promise is an exposure to a decision, not a cap on the payoff. The floor was a policy, and policies can end. The puts and the long position are both short that decision, and a model with a floor in it will never price a world where the floor is gone.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell more puts. They pay so little that you need more of them to make the income worthwhile.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Low premium on a large notional is leverage on the one scenario you cannot price. The worst loss grows with every contract, and the premium barely moves. Chasing a premium target by adding size is how a quiet position becomes the largest risk in the account.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Put the floor-removed scenario into the grid: a fall of 15% to 30% in minutes, no fills in between. Cut the notional until that loss fits a small budget, and treat the long position as having no stop.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the sizing rule applied to a can't-happen row. Divide the budget by the loss in the worst cell and round down. You give up income on the quiet days and keep the account on the day the market skips the stop.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the size but place stop-loss orders just below 1.20 on the long position.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A stop is an order, not a guarantee. Once triggered it is filled at the next available price, and in a gap that can be far below the stop. It helps in an orderly decline, but it does not answer the sizing question for a jump, and it does nothing for the short puts.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On Thursday 15 January 2015 the SNB discontinued the minimum rate \"with immediate effect\", stopped its foreign-currency purchases and cut the rate on sight deposits to &minus;0.75%. At one point on the day the franc was up about 30% against the euro, and EUR/CHF was later down about 13% to 14%, near 1.04. Alpari UK, a London retail broker, entered insolvency, Global Brokers NZ said it could not resume business, and FXCM, which said client losses had created negative balances of about $225 million, took a $300 million financing from Leucadia National on 16 January. This drill is a simplified position built on documented events, not anyone's actual account.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bis.org/review/r150116a.htm\">Thomas Jordan, \"The rationale for discontinuing the minimum exchange rate and lowering interest rates\", SNB press conference, 15 January 2015 (BIS central bankers' speeches)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.foxnews.com/world/swiss-francs-unprecedented-surge-causes-2-brokerage-firms-to-become-insolvent.amp\">Fox News report, \"Swiss franc's 'unprecedented' surge causes 2 brokerage firms to become insolvent\", January 2015</a>; <a href=\"https://www.financemagnates.com/forex/brokers/breaking-leucadia-hands-fxcm-300-mln-life-line/\">Finance Magnates, Leucadia financing of FXCM, 16 January 2015</a>; <a href=\"https://mondovisione.com/media-and-resources/news/fxcm-comments-on-swiss-franc-movement-2015116/\">FXCM, \"FXCM Comments on Swiss Franc Movement\", 15 January 2015</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"short-rate-models-vasicek-cir-hull-white","title":"Short-Rate Models: Vasicek, CIR &amp; Hull-White","date":"2014-06-05","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2014-06-05\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 5 June 2014</div> <h3>The ECB has just taken its deposit rate below zero</h3> <p>You run a euro rates book. Your caps and swaptions are marked with a lognormal model, and your short-rate scenarios come from a CIR model. Today the European Central Bank cuts its deposit rate to −0.10%, the first major central bank to go below zero. Overnight rates are about to follow. A client asks you to price a floor struck at −0.25%. What do you do with your models?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep both models. Negative rates are a short blip and will snap back above zero.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Neither model can produce a rate below zero, so the −0.25% strike has no sensible price, and the lognormal volatility numbers blow up near zero. You are betting the regime away instead of modelling it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep CIR but refit it with a long-run level close to zero.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A lower target does not move the wall. The square-root noise still shrinks to nothing at zero, so CIR still says a negative rate is impossible, and a smaller \\(b\\) only makes the Feller check harder to pass.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Move to models that allow negative rates: quote volatility in normal, basis-point terms and use a Gaussian model such as Hull-White fitted to today's curve, while remembering it puts no floor anywhere.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This matches the regime you are in and prices the negative strike. The caveat matters too: a Gaussian model gives real probability to −3%, which the data never showed.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Patch the lognormal model with a fixed shift, so it allows rates down to minus the shift.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible, and the market did exactly this. But the shift is a number someone picks, not one estimated from data, and if rates fall past it the model breaks again.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The ECB took its deposit rate in steps to −0.50% by September 2019. Switzerland and Sweden went negative in 2015 and the Bank of Japan in 2016. The market moved to normal-volatility quoting and to shifted models. Rates found a soft floor, with the deepest major policy rates around −0.75%, and stayed there for years. Then the ECB raised its deposit rate to zero in July 2022 and to 4.00% by September 2023.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/pr/date/2014/html/pr140605_3.en.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ECB press release, 5 June 2014</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/stats/policy_and_exchange_rates/key_ecb_interest_rates/html/index.en.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ECB key interest rates, history</a>; Swiss National Bank press release, 15 January 2015 (sight deposit rate −0.75%).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"stationarity-acf-pacf","title":"Stationarity, ACF &amp; PACF","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative morning</div> <h3>A beautiful regression between two price levels</h3> <p>You regress one currency pair's daily price level on another's over five years. The fit looks superb: an R² of 0.8 and a t-statistic of 12. You plan to trade the gap between them, the regression residual, as a mean-reverting spread. Before sizing it, you plot the residual's ACF. It is still well outside the noise band at lag 25. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade it. A t-statistic of 12 cannot be luck.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Granger and Newbold showed that regressions between two independent random walks routinely look this significant. On non-stationary levels the usual significance numbers mean very little, so the fit on its own is not evidence.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the slow-fading ACF as a unit-root warning: run an ADF test on the residual, and trade the spread only if it passes.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A correlogram still elevated 20 to 30 lags out is the cheap first-pass sign of a unit root. The formal test is the next step, and a spread is only tradeable if it is stationary.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use a longer window, say 60 days, for the z-score so it captures the slow decay.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A longer window does not create a home level. If the residual has a unit root there is no fixed mean for any window to estimate, and the bands drift along with the spread.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Difference both series and study the ACF and PACF of the daily changes instead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Differencing is the standard fix for reading the plots, so this is sound statistics. But it throws away the level relationship you wanted to trade. Testing the residual answers the question you actually have.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a dated event. The history in this lesson gives the answer: Granger and Newbold (1974) showed by simulation that regressions between independent random walks routinely look this significant, Dickey and Fuller (1979) supplied the unit-root test, and Engle and Granger (1987) made it the rule for spreads: a spread is tradeable only if it passes a stationarity test. A residual whose ACF has not faded by lag 25 is unlikely to pass.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"stochastic-pdes","title":"Stochastic PDEs","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative curve trade</div> <h3>Your risk report says the steepener is almost riskless</h3> <p>You put on a 2s10s steepener: long the 2-year, short the 10-year, sized so a parallel move in rates nets to roughly zero. Your risk system prices everything with a one-factor Hull-White model. The report shows almost no risk on the position. What do you make of it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trust it. The model is calibrated to today's curve, so its risk numbers are right.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Fitting today's curve says nothing about how the curve moves. A one-factor model moves every tenor with the same shock, so it cannot see the 2-year and 10-year moving differently, which is the only thing the trade is about.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Raise the volatility in the one-factor model until the risk looks bigger.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A bigger single number bolted onto the same model still moves every point in lockstep. The report scales up, but the steepening risk is still invisible.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Measure the trade against separate level, slope and curvature factors, imperfectly correlated with each other.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Shape risk needs several factors that can move the short and long ends differently. This is the practical, finite-dimensional step towards the whole-curve model, and it shows the exposure the trade was built to take.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Build a full SPDE model of the whole forward curve before trading again.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It would capture the shape risk, but full SPDE machinery is rare outside specialist curve desks. A small multi-factor model gets you most of the way for a fraction of the work.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The lesson.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a dated event. A one-factor short-rate model is the curve with every tenor but one thrown away. That is fine for books exposed mainly to the level of rates, and blind to trades whose payoff depends on the curve's shape.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"stress-testing-scenario-analysis","title":"Stress Testing, Scenario Analysis &amp; Reverse Stress Tests","date":"2022-09-26","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-09-26\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 26 September 2022</div> <h3>The gilt move is heading past what your buffer was built for</h3> <p>You oversee the hedge of a UK defined-benefit pension scheme. It runs through a leveraged LDI fund that holds gilts, gilt repo and swaps, with a collateral buffer sized to cover a yield rise of a chosen size, taken from the history of gilt moves. On Friday the government's mini-budget announced large unfunded tax cuts. Long gilt yields are jumping and collateral calls are arriving. At this pace the rise will soon pass anything your buffer was set for. Every other LDI fund holds a similar book. What do you do first?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Wait. A move this extreme has to reverse.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A move past history is exactly what a buffer sized to history cannot handle. Calls you cannot meet do not wait for a reversal; the fund gets closed out at the worst level.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Meet each call as it comes by selling gilts.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It keeps the hedge alive for now, but you and every similar fund are selling the same gilts into a market with few buyers. That pushes yields up again and triggers the next round of calls.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Run the reverse test now: what further yield rise uses up what is left of the buffer, counting everyone else forced to sell at once? Then raise collateral from other assets and cut leverage to survive that answer, not the historical one.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The forward test is about to fail. Solving backward for the shock that breaks you, including the forced-selling loop that steepens the loss, tells you how close the edge really is and how much cash and de-leveraging you need.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Re-run the historical replay with the worst week on record and report that the book survives it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The record is being beaten as you watch. Replaying it again only measures a storm you already know is too small.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The collateral calls came in. To meet them, funds and schemes sold gilts into a market with few buyers, which pushed yields up again and set off the next round of calls. The Bank of England said afterwards that the move went beyond what LDI funds had been prepared for. On 28 September it stepped in with temporary purchases of long-dated gilts, to protect financial stability rather than as monetary policy, and ended them on 14 October. Within weeks the Chancellor had been replaced and the Prime Minister had resigned. UK authorities then set a higher minimum level of resilience for LDI funds.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/news/2022/september/bank-of-england-announces-gilt-market-operation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bank of England press release, 28 September 2022</a>; <a href=\"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/financial-policy-summary-and-record/2023/bank-staff-paper-ldi-minimum-resilience\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bank of England staff paper, LDI minimum resilience, March 2023</a>; Reuters, 14 October 2022 (Chancellor replaced) and 20 October 2022 (Prime Minister resigns).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"swap-curves-ois","title":"Swap Curves &amp; OIS Discounting","date":"2008-09-15","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2008-09-15\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 15 September 2008</div> <h3>Lehman has failed, and LIBOR no longer looks risk-free</h3> <p>You run a large book of collateralised interest rate swaps: cash is posted daily against every mark. Like the rest of the market, you value them on one LIBOR curve that both discounts the cash flows and forecasts the floating legs, which pay LIBOR. The LIBOR-OIS spread, normally a few basis points, has been widening since August 2007 and is now jumping again. Today Lehman Brothers filed for bankruptcy. How should you value the book?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep one LIBOR curve for everything. It is the market standard.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">LIBOR now carries a large and growing premium for bank credit risk. Your trades are collateralised, so they carry almost none of it. Discounting at LIBOR bakes a credit premium into \"risk-free\" values, and it gets bigger every day of the crisis.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Discount at OIS, the rate your posted cash actually earns, and keep a separate LIBOR curve only to forecast what the floating legs pay.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the multi-curve split. Each curve does the job it is right for: OIS brings cash flows to today, and the LIBOR forward curve says what the floating leg will pay, because that is still the index in the contract.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch everything to OIS, including the floating-leg forecasts.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Right about discounting, wrong about forecasting. The floating legs still pay LIBOR, which is now far above OIS, so forecasting them off OIS understates what you will receive or pay.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Read each morning's LIBOR fixing as a clean market price of bank risk and trade the spread off it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">LIBOR is a survey of what panel banks say they could borrow at, with few real trades behind it right now, and a bank that answers high signals weakness. The fixing may be flattering the real stress.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The 3-month USD LIBOR-OIS spread peaked at roughly 364bp in October 2008. The industry moved collateralised derivatives to OIS discounting, with LIBOR kept only as the index the floating leg paid. Investigations later found that during the crisis some banks had submitted artificially low rates, and that traders had long asked for favourable fixes. Barclays agreed to pay about $450 million in June 2012 and UBS about $1.5 billion in December 2012. The transaction-based SOFR replaced LIBOR, and the main USD LIBOR panel ended on 30 June 2023.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/pubs/bulletin/2009/articles/bankprofit/dlink/fig02.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Federal Reserve Bulletin, 2009, LIBOR-OIS spread chart</a>; Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, Monetary Trends, November 2008; CFTC press release, 27 June 2012 (Barclays); US Department of Justice press release, 19 December 2012 (UBS); FCA announcement on LIBOR cessation, 5 March 2021.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"systemic-risk-networks","title":"Systemic Risk &amp; Financial Contagion Networks","date":"2008-09-15","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2008-09-15\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 15 September 2008</div> <h3>Your hedge was written by AIG</h3> <p>You run risk at a large bank. You hold super-senior slices of mortgage-backed CDOs, hedged with credit default swaps bought from AIG's Financial Products unit, so the position has been treated as close to riskless. This morning Lehman Brothers filed for bankruptcy. Today the rating agencies downgraded AIG, which triggers collateral calls it cannot meet. AIG wrote the same kind of protection to many other banks. How do you treat your position?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing has changed. The CDO risk is hedged, so the position stays near riskless.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The hedge is a claim on one node. If AIG's distress goes to 1, the protection fails at the moment you need it, and your CDOs are suddenly unhedged in a falling market.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat the hedge as exposure to AIG, assume its failure would hit every bank it wrote protection for at once, and size the position as if it might be unhedged.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the network view. AIG is a node with large impact factors into many banks at once, so its failure would not hit you alone: your counterparties would be weakened in the same week, and their distress would pass on to you in later rounds too.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Buy credit protection on AIG itself to cover the counterparty risk.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It addresses the right risk, but the seller of that protection is another node in the same web, and buying it on the day of a downgrade is expensive. It moves the exposure rather than removing it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Only count AIG's direct losses. It is a huge insurer with plenty of capital, so it will absorb them.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the \"too big to fail\" view, and it misses the point of the lesson. What mattered was not AIG's own loss but the distress its failure would induce across everyone connected to it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 16 September 2008 the Federal Reserve Bank of New York agreed to lend AIG up to $85 billion, and the US government took a stake of about four-fifths of the company. Support later grew well beyond that first loan. When the CDOs were bought out of the swaps that November, the bank counterparties received the full value of the protection they had bought. Their hedges held because of the rescue, not because of AIG.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/other20080916a.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Federal Reserve press release, 16 September 2008</a>; SIGTARP, \"Factors Affecting Efforts to Limit Payments to AIG Counterparties\", 17 November 2009; Reuters, 15–16 September 2008 (AIG downgrades).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"tail-risk-hedging-convexity","title":"Tail Risk Hedging &amp; Portfolio Convexity","date":"2020-01","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-01\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · January 2020</div> <h3>The hedge has been the worst line for years. Cut it?</h3> <p>You sit on the risk committee of a fund whose book leans short volatility, through carry and option selling. For years a small, fixed budget has bought far out-of-the-money index puts. Most years they expired worthless, and the hedge has been the single worst line in the attribution report. Stocks are near record highs and calm. A colleague proposes cutting the hedge budget. What do you decide?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Cut the budget. Years of calm show the puts are wasted money.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A run of calm years does not make the next crash any less likely. Cutting the hedge because of its recent cost is the single most common way a tail-hedge program fails to be there when it is needed.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Swap the puts for gold. It costs nothing to carry and rises in a panic.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Gold is a linear hedge that relies on a correlation holding under stress. It has no option-like convexity, and nothing in its contract makes it pay when stocks fall.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep the puts at their fixed, budgeted size, and judge them on what they do for the whole portfolio, not on their own track record.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The bleed is the premium, by design. The relevant test is the combined book across calm and crash years, and the timing of a crash is never knowable in advance.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep a hedge, but switch to put spreads to cut the cost.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible: a put spread is cheaper. But it caps the payoff once the market falls past the lower strike, so you are selling back some of the convexity you were paying for.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> From a record high on 19 February 2020, the S&amp;P 500 fell about 34% in five weeks, to 23 March. On 16 March the VIX closed at a record 82.69. Far out-of-the-money puts went deep into the money while the implied volatility they were priced on exploded, and their value rose many times over. In the mid-March dash for cash, gold fell sharply alongside stocks for about a week, and even US Treasuries sold off until the Federal Reserve stepped in with very large purchases. Anyone who tried to buy protection after the fall had started paid far more, because implied volatility had already jumped.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">S&amp;P 500 daily closes, FRED</a> (3,386.15 on 19 February, 2,237.40 on 23 March 2020); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cboe VIX daily closes, FRED</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200323b.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Federal Reserve press release, 23 March 2020</a>; CNBC, 16 March 2020 (VIX record close).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"taylor-rule","title":"The Taylor Rule","date":"2021-11-30","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2021-11-30\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 30 November 2021</div> <h3>The Fed retires \"transitory\"</h3> <p>US inflation has climbed all year. The policy rate is 0–0.25%, and the Fed is still buying bonds every month; tapering has only just begun, and its guidance says purchases end before rates rise. Today Chair Powell told the Senate it is probably time to retire the word \"transitory\". On this lesson's coefficients the Taylor Rule sits several points above the actual rate. What do you do with that gap?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Bet big on a hike at the very next meeting. The gap is huge, so the move must be imminent.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A wide gap says which way the pressure runs, not when it is released. The Fed's own sequencing meant bond buying had to finish before any hike.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Position for the front end to reprice higher over the coming months, sized to sit through a long wait before the first hike.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is how the rule is meant to be read: unresolved pressure in a known direction, with no date attached. Size and horizon have to match a gap that can stay open for months.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Follow the Fed. It says inflation is fading, and it knows more than a formula.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The committee was reacting to its forecast that inflation would fade. The rule reacts to today's numbers, and today's numbers already said policy was far behind the curve.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Lean hawkish with a small position, and drop it if nothing happens by the next meeting.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Right direction, wrong clock. A gap like this can take months to close, so a few-week deadline likely gets you out before the move.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The Fed raised rates on 16 March 2022, by a quarter point. US CPI inflation peaked at 9.1% in June 2022, and that month the FOMC raised by 75 basis points, the largest single move since 1994; three more 75s followed. By July 2023 the target range was 5.25–5.50%. EUR/USD fell below parity in July 2022 for the first time in about twenty years, and the dollar index reached a twenty-year high that September.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220615a.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FOMC statement, 15 June 2022</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20230726a.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FOMC statement, 26 July 2023</a>; <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cpi_07132022.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BLS CPI release, 13 July 2022</a>; FOMC statement, 16 March 2022; Reuters, 30 November 2021 (Powell's Senate testimony).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"theta-decay","title":"Theta Decay","date":"2018-11-14","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2018-11-14\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 14 November 2018</div> <h3>Your \"income\" calls are waking up</h3> <p>It is the morning of 14 November 2018. Your account sells far out-of-the-money call options on natural gas futures for the premium, and the theta has come in steadily for months. The positions are not delta-hedged. Natural gas has been rallying hard on early cold-weather forecasts and low storage, and the calls that looked nearly worthless are starting to gain value. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Hold. The strikes are still well above the price, and the theta keeps coming in every day.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Long theta is short gamma, by the same identity. Being far out of the money only means delta is small for now. A jump makes it large all at once, and the daily rent you collected is tiny next to that bill.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell more calls further up, since the premium is richer now.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Richer premium is the market telling you the gamma bill is getting bigger. Adding more short calls doubles down on exactly the exposure that is hurting you, with margin already under pressure.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cap the risk now: buy back the calls, or buy calls further up to turn them into spreads, so no single jump can exceed what the account can carry.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This fixes the real problem, an unlimited short-gamma position with no hedge. Paying up to close or cap it costs some of the premium collected. It keeps a jump from turning months of small gains into a margin call you cannot meet.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Delta-hedge by buying some natural gas futures.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than nothing: it offsets part of the directional loss. But you stay short gamma, and a hedge set this morning is too small after a double-digit jump. Margin calls still arrive before you can rebalance.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 14 November the front-month natural gas contract jumped about 18% in a single session, from $4.10 to $4.84 per million Btu. Calls that had looked close to worthless gained delta and value very quickly. OptionSellers.com's accounts could not meet the margin calls, and the clearing broker liquidated them into the move. About 300 client accounts fell below their margin and were liquidated, and some ended with negative balances, owing the broker money on top of everything they had deposited.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.eia.gov/dnav/ng/hist/rngc1d.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EIA, NYMEX natural gas futures contract 1, daily settlements</a>; <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/913760/000091376018000151/a8-kiff2018stmtoffincond.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">INTL FCStone Form 8-K, 27 November 2018</a> (about 300 Optionsellers.com accounts below maintenance margin in the week ended 16 November).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"time-value-of-money","title":"Time Value of Money","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative morning</div> <h3>\"The forward says the dollar will fall\"</h3> <p>USD/JPY spot is 150.00. Three-month dollar deposits pay 5% a year and yen deposits 0.1%. The 3-month forward is quoted at 148.19, about 1.81 yen below spot. A colleague says the market is pricing the dollar to fall about 1.2% over three months, and proposes selling dollars forward as a \"market-implied\" directional trade. What is your read?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Agree. The forward is the market's best forecast of where spot will be in three months.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The forward contains no forecast at all. It is spot adjusted for the interest gap between the two currencies. Selling dollars forward here is a bet on direction dressed up as a reading of the market.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">The 1.81-yen discount is just the rate gap: 150 × 1.00025 ÷ 1.0125 ≈ 148.19. Any view on direction has to come from somewhere else.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's no-arbitrage forward to the yen. The higher-rate currency has to trade forward at a discount, or borrowing yen to buy dollars and selling them forward would be free money. The discount tells you about interest rates, not about the future.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Check it with the rule of thumb, −150 × 0.049 × 0.25 ≈ −1.84 yen, see that it roughly matches, and call the forecast fair.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The check is the right instinct and the arithmetic is close. The conclusion is backwards: matching the rate gap is exactly what shows the discount is mechanical rather than a forecast.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Do the opposite and buy dollars forward at 148.19, since that locks in dollars 1.81 yen cheaper than spot.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">There is no bargain. Buying dollars at spot and holding them earns 5% instead of 0.1% on yen, and that extra interest is worth the same 1.81 yen. At the fair forward the two routes are worth the same.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson shows.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's USD/JPY numbers, not a dated event. If the forward were quoted at 150.00, borrowing 150 yen, buying $1, earning 5% for three months and selling the $1.0125 forward would leave 1.8375 yen of riskless profit. At 148.19 that profit is zero. A forward discount is a present-value adjustment, not a prediction; whether the dollar actually falls is a separate question, taken up in the Interest Rate Parity lesson.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"trade-management-exits","title":"Managing the Trade: R-Multiples, Stops &amp; Exits","date":"2016-06-23","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2016-06-23\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 23 June 2016</div> <h3>Referendum night, and your trade is in profit</h3> <p>The UK votes on leaving the European Union today, and polls close at 10pm. Earlier in the week you bought GBP/USD at 1.4700 (an illustrative position) with a stop at 1.4550, 150 pips, so 1R = 150 pips. Late in the evening sterling trades around 1.50 as markets lean towards Remain: you are up about 2R. Results will arrive overnight, in thin markets. What do you do with the position before the results?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Move the stop up to entry at 1.4700. Now the trade can't lose.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A break-even stop is an order, not a floor. If the result shocks the market, price can fall far through 1.4700 before a fill. The stop changes the hit rate on a normal night; it does not cap the loss on an abnormal one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to the position. The polls point to Remain, and adding to winners is how trends pay.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Scaling in only pays when moves persist. A binary event is not a trend: adding raises the size just before the largest gap risk of the year, and the combined position's real risk is now several times 1R.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Close the trade, or cut it to a size whose loss you could accept if the price gapped many hundreds of pips.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The trade was not built to hold a binary vote result. An event rule, like a time stop, ends exposure the plan never priced. If you keep any, size it for the gap line, not for the stop.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the trade but buy a short-dated sterling put as protection.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible: an option caps the loss whatever the gap. But protection is expensive the night everyone wants it, and it does not answer whether the trade belongs in the book at all.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Sterling traded around 1.50 against the dollar shortly after the polls closed. As results came in overnight showing a vote to leave, it fell to about 1.32 on 24 June, its lowest level since 1985, a fall of more than 10% within hours. A long from 1.4700 faced a move of roughly 1,500 pips, about 10R on a 150-pip stop; how much of that a stop saved depended on where it filled in a fast, thin market.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"transfer-entropy-causality","title":"Transfer Entropy &amp; Information-Theoretic Causality","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research review</div> <h3>Granger says nothing, transfer entropy says something</h3> <p>You tested whether the DE&#8211;US yield spread leads EUR/USD. The linear Granger test came back null in both directions. A colleague then estimated transfer entropy on the same daily data and found a clearly positive flow from the spread to EUR/USD, with almost nothing the other way. What do you do with the result?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Write it up as \"the spread causes EUR/USD\" and size a trade on it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Transfer entropy measures predictive information flow, not cause. A shared driver, such as a change in risk sentiment that reaches the bond market slightly before FX, could produce exactly this one-way pattern. And nothing has been checked out of sample yet.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Ignore it. The linear test was null, so the transfer entropy must be noise.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A null linear test is exactly the case where transfer entropy can add something: a threshold or regime-dependent link that a straight line cannot represent. Throwing it out unchecked repeats the blind spot the tool exists to fix.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Test it against shuffled series, recheck it out of sample and at other lags, and look for a common driver that could hit both.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This treats the result as what it is: a candidate nonlinear lead-lag finding. The shuffle test guards against estimation bias, the out-of-sample check guards against overfitting, and the confound check keeps the claim at \"predicts\", not \"causes\".</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Accept it as \"the spread's history predicts EUR/USD beyond its own history\" and go straight to a backtest.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The wording is honest, but you have skipped the significance test. Real-data transfer entropy estimates are biased upwards on finite samples, so the flow may not be there at all.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own spread and FX scenario, not a reported result. A clearly asymmetric transfer entropy where the linear test was null would be a more informative finding than the linear result alone, but it still needs a real out-of-sample check, a test against shuffled data, and the causal caveat stated plainly before it counts as anything more than predictive content a linear model missed.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"trend-cycle-filters","title":"Trend/Cycle Filters: Hodrick-Prescott, Butterworth &amp; Baxter-King","date":"1975","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1975\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · the mid-1970s</div> <h3>The gap says there is plenty of slack</h3> <p>You advise a US policymaker in the mid-1970s. Your potential-output trend is fitted to past output, mostly the strong growth of the 1950s and 1960s, and carried forward to the newest quarter. It shows a large negative output gap: lots of spare capacity. But productivity growth has been fading since the late 1960s, the 1973 oil shock has hit supply, and inflation is rising. What do you tell the policymaker?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The gap is large and negative, so there is room to keep policy loose.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This trusts the single least reliable number in the series: the trend at the newest quarter, estimated with no later data and anchored to growth rates that may no longer hold. If trend growth has slowed, the trend sits too high and the slack is partly an artefact.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">The newest gap reading is the least reliable point, and trend growth may have slowed. Treat the slack as uncertain and give real weight to rising inflation.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's point in practice. The end of a trend estimate revises the most, and a slowdown in trend growth shows up first as a gap that is too negative. Rising inflation is direct evidence that the slack may not be there.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch to a band-pass filter that keeps only 6 to 32 quarter cycles.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A fair instinct, since it states the cycle band openly, but a finite band-pass filter such as Baxter-King loses its last K observations, so it gives no reading at all for the newest quarters. It does not answer the question you were asked.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Raise the smoothing so the trend is less influenced by the latest wobbles.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More smoothing pushes the trend closer to a straight line drawn through the fast-growth past, so it sits even higher at the newest quarter and the gap looks even more negative. It makes this particular error worse.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Policy that believed in large slack stayed looser than an accurate gap would have called for, and inflation rose through the decade, into double digits by its end. Athanasios Orphanides (American Economic Review, 2001) rebuilt the real-time data and found the gaps policymakers had were far more negative than today's estimates for the same quarters: much of the slack had never existed. With Simon van Norden (2002) he showed that across many detrending methods, the HP filter included, revisions were about as large as the gap itself, driven mainly by the end of the sample, not by revised GDP.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.91.4.964\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orphanides, \"Monetary Policy Rules Based on Real-Time Data\", American Economic Review 91(4), 2001</a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1162/003465302760556422\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orphanides and van Norden, \"The Unreliability of Output-Gap Estimates in Real Time\", Review of Economics and Statistics 84(4), 2002</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"variance-risk-premium","title":"The Variance Risk Premium: Why Implied Volatility Usually Beats Realised","date":"2017-11-03","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2017-11-03\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 3 November 2017</div> <h3>The quietest market on record, and a perfect year</h3> <p>You have sold a one-month at-the-money S&amp;P 500 straddle every month for two years, sized to your comfort, and every month has made money. Today the VIX closes at 9.14, the lowest close in Cboe's daily history, and the index has realised only about 5% volatility over the last month. A 30-day straddle at 9.14% volatility pays about 2.1% of the index level. What do you do with your size?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add size. The gap is still positive and the strategy has not lost in two years.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The premium is thin: about 2.1% of the index for a month, against a tail that is as large as ever. Two perfect years are a short sample for a strategy whose losses come from rare events. Adding size here buys the least pay per unit of tail risk you will ever be offered.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Go the other way. Volatility cannot go lower, so buy straddles instead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Low can stay low, and the same research says buyers lose on average, month after month, to the premium they pay. \"Cheap\" is not the same as \"about to rise\". You would be paying to be early.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep size set by the worst month you could survive, not by the calm of the last two years, and consider capping the tail with bought options.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The premium on offer is at its lowest, so the case for taking more risk is at its weakest. Sizing from the worst case and paying for protection costs some of the thin premium and keeps you in the game for the month that matters.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Carry on unchanged. The long-run average says the premium is positive.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Defensible, and it is what a rule-based seller would do. But it ignores that sizes chosen in a calm market tend to be too large for a volatile one, and that a thin premium is thin pay for the same tail.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 2 February 2018 the VIX closed at 17.31. On Monday 5 February it closed at 37.32, up 20.01 points or 115.6% in a day, and the S&amp;P 500 fell 4.1%. Realised volatility over the 21 trading days from 5 February was about 25%, against about 7% across 2017. A single episode cannot prove a rule; it shows the shape. Calm months paid small amounts, then a few days returned many months of them. Later in this series we look at how short-volatility products and books failed that week.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://cdn.cboe.com/api/global/us_indices/daily_prices/VIX_History.csv\">Cboe, VIX daily historical data, 1990 onwards (closes on 3 Nov 2017, 2 Feb 2018 and 5 Feb 2018)</a>; S&amp;P 500 daily closing values, S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices. Percentage changes, the 21-day realised volatilities and the straddle price are our own calculations (straddle: Black-Scholes, zero rate).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"vega-volatility-surface","title":"Vega &amp; the Volatility Surface","date":"1987-10-19","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"1987-10-19\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 19 October 1987</div> <h3>Your fund's crash insurance is a trading plan</h3> <p>It is the weekend before Monday 19 October 1987, after a week of sharp falls in stocks. You oversee a pension fund whose equity holdings are covered by portfolio insurance: a program that replicates a put by selling stock-index futures as the market falls, tracking the put's delta. It holds no actual options. What do you do before Monday's open?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. The program does exactly what a put would do, and it was built for a week like this.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A replicated put is a delta hedge only. It has no vega and it needs a market smooth enough to keep re-hedging. If prices gap, the replication falls behind just when the protection matters, and every other insurer is trying to sell the same futures.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Accept that you are only partly insured: cut the equity exposure directly, or buy listed index puts, even at a higher price.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Real puts carry vega as well as delta, so they gain from the fall and from the jump in implied volatility, with no re-hedging needed. Cutting exposure removes the risk outright. Both cost something now; neither depends on a smooth path.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the program, but tell the traders to sell futures faster at the open.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It addresses the lag, which is the right worry. But you would be racing every other insurer to sell the same futures into a falling market, which is how the futures came to trade at a deep discount.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch the program off and buy more stocks: the fall is a bargain.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That turns a partly hedged book into an unhedged, larger one on the eve of a crash. Whatever the long-run value, you have removed the protection the fund was paying for.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 19 October 1987 the Dow fell 22.6% and the S&amp;P 500 about 20% in a single session, still the largest one-day percentage falls in their history. Index futures traded at a deep discount to the stocks underneath them, and the Brady Commission found portfolio insurers' futures selling was a large part of the selling pressure that week (how much it caused the fall is still debated). The replication fell behind and the insurance failed when it was needed, while holders of actual puts gained from the fall and from the jump in implied volatility. Out-of-the-money index puts have carried an implied-vol premium ever since.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1987\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Federal Reserve History, \"Stock Market Crash of 1987\"</a>; Report of the Presidential Task Force on Market Mechanisms (the Brady Commission), January 1988; Rubinstein, \"Implied Binomial Trees\", Journal of Finance, 1994.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"vix-uncertainty-not-fear","title":"The VIX Is an Uncertainty Index, Not a Fear Gauge","date":"2018-02-05","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2018-02-05\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 5 February 2018</div> <h3>The VIX has doubled in a day. Which way do you lean?</h3> <p>It is the close on Monday 5 February 2018. The S&amp;P 500 fell about 4% today, and the VIX closed at 37.32, up from 17.31 on Friday. You hold a long S&amp;P 500 position sized for the calm market of last week. A colleague says the fear gauge is screaming and you should flip short. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Flip short. A VIX that doubles in a day is signalling more downside.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The VIX is built from puts and calls, and its bell stays centred on zero. It says the next month's moves will be bigger, not which way they will go. Flipping on it reads a direction into a number that has none.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep the direction call separate and resize for the new range. 37.32 ÷ 16 is about 2.3% a day, against about 1.1% at 17.31, so cut the position by roughly half and widen the stop to match.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the VIX is for. The expected daily move more than doubled, so the same position now carries more than twice the risk. Cutting size keeps your risk steady, and your view on direction stays your own call.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Close everything and wait for the VIX to calm down.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safe, and better than flipping. But the VIX gave you a size message, not a reason to abandon your view. Resizing does the same risk job without throwing the position away.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell volatility. Option prices have doubled, so collect the rich premium.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Short-vol products were being forced to buy volatility back into the close, and that forced buying was making the spike bigger. Selling into it, sized for last week's calm, puts you on the side being squeezed.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Overnight, the largest inverse-VIX exchange-traded note lost more than 90% of its indicative value, and its issuer announced it would terminate the note. The forced buying from short-vol products had made the spike bigger. The S&amp;P 500 stayed volatile into the following week, so the VIX was right about size. It gave no signal about where the index would be a few months later.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cboe VIX daily closes, FRED</a> (17.31 on 2 February, 37.32 on 5 February 2018); <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">S&amp;P 500 daily closes, FRED</a> (2,762.13 to 2,648.94, −4.1%); Credit Suisse media release on the XIV ETN acceleration event, 6 February 2018.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"vpin-order-flow-toxicity","title":"Order Flow Toxicity &amp; VPIN","date":"2010-05-06","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2010-05-06\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 6 May 2010</div> <h3>Your toxicity monitor is pinned high, and the selling will not stop</h3> <p>You make markets in E-mini S&amp;P 500 futures. It is the afternoon of 6 May 2010. Volume is exploding, almost every recent bucket closes lower, and your VPIN-style monitor is near the top of its range. You cannot see who is selling or why. What do you do with your quotes?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep quoting full size at your usual spread. VPIN's track record is disputed, so ignore it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The forecasting claim is disputed. What the monitor describes is not: the flow hitting you is one-sided right now, and one-sided flow runs over whoever is providing liquidity, informed or not. Quoting full size into it is how a market maker gets run over.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Read it as informed selling and go short in size.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">VPIN measures lopsidedness, not knowledge. A huge one-way hedge or recycled volume pushes it up just as well as someone with news. Shorting a falling, thin market on that reading is a bet on information that may not exist.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Widen your spread and cut your quoted size, reading the monitor as a description of the flow now, not a forecast.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the Glosten-Milgrom response the lesson describes: protect yourself against one-sided flow while it is there, and stay in the market. It does not need VPIN to predict anything, only to describe what is hitting you.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Pull all your quotes and step away until it calms down.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It protects your book, and liquidity providers did pull back that day. But when everyone does it, nobody is left to absorb the selling, and that is how depth vanishes. Defensible for one firm, but not the measured response.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The SEC/CFTC staff report found that the big seller was a fund complex hedging an equity position, not trading on information. It sold 75,000 E-mini contracts, about $4.1 billion, through an algorithm set to sell a fixed share of the previous minute's volume, with no limit on price or time. Intermediaries passed contracts back and forth, more than 27,000 in 14 seconds for a net change of about 200. Buy-side depth in the E-mini fell to less than 1% of its morning level, and the E-mini fell more than 5% in about four and a half minutes before largely recovering the same session. The flow was toxic, but nobody in it knew anything.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/news/studies/2010/marketevents-report.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SEC/CFTC staff report, \"Findings Regarding the Market Events of May 6, 2010\", 30 September 2010</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"walk-forward-purged-cv","title":"Walk-Forward &amp; Purged Cross-Validation","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative backtest review</div> <h3>A clean split date and a strong out-of-sample Sharpe</h3> <p>A colleague's strategy uses a 20-day volatility feature and a label of \"did the trade win over the next 5 days\". They split by date: train on days 1–499, test on days 500–599, and the next fold trains from day 600. No shuffling anywhere. The out-of-sample Sharpe is strong, and they want to trade it. What do you do first?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sign it off. The split date is respected, so there is no lookahead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The date is respected; the windows are not. Days 495–499 carry labels built from test-period prices, and days 600–618 carry features built from them.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Purge days 495–499 and 600–618, add an embargo, rerun across many rolled folds, and compare.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That removes both leaks by exact rule, adds a margin for correlation no window captures, and judges the rule across many folds instead of one split.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Purge the five training days before day 500 and rerun.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It fixes the label leak, but not the feature leak on the other side of the test window, and it adds no embargo. Purging is a two-directional problem.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch to random 5-fold cross-validation, which uses more of the data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Shuffling puts later days in training while earlier days are tested: literal lookahead on every fold. It makes the leak worse, not better.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What it means.</strong> This is an illustrative review built on the lesson's worked example, not a documented case. If the Sharpe holds up once the 34 rows are held out and the rule is judged across many folds, it is an honest out-of-sample number. If it shrinks, part of the original result was the leak. Either way, a purged test only makes the number honest; it does not prove an edge.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"wavelets-time-frequency","title":"Wavelets &amp; Time-Frequency Analysis","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative ECB afternoon</div> <h3>A bright band after the press conference</h3> <p>You run a Morlet scalogram of EUR/USD 5-minute returns over one session that included an ECB press conference. It shows a bright band at short scales in the 20 minutes after the press conference starts. It also shows bright patches at long scales at the very start and the very end of the session. What do you conclude?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Both are real. Build a rule that trades the short cycle after ECB press conferences and the long cycle at the open and close.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The edge patches sit inside the cone of influence, where the wavelet hangs off the end of the data, so they mostly reflect how the edges were handled. And one day's bright band is an observation, not a tested rule.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Distrust the edge patches, then check whether the post-conference band shows up across many ECB days, with a proper significance test rather than brightness by eye.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This handles both traps in the lesson. Edge readings get far less weight, and scanning many time-scale cells for a bright spot is a multiple-comparisons problem. A band that recurs across many events, and survives a real test, is worth taking further.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Write up the band as a description of what happened that day, with no trading rule attached.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Honest, and the scalogram is good at exactly this kind of description. But a single day says little about whether the pattern repeats, and the edge patches still need flagging in the write-up.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Swap the scalogram for one periodogram of the whole session, since it is the more established tool.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That throws away the one thing you wanted to know: when. A periodogram would report a short cycle somewhere in the day, with no way to say whether it was concentrated right after the press conference.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own ECB scenario, not a reported result. A scalogram can show a shortening cycle as a narrow band right at the announcement, which a whole-session periodogram cannot. But readings near the sample edges are contaminated, isolated hot spots need a real significance framework, and turning a pattern into a rule still needs an honest out-of-sample test.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"yield-curve-construction","title":"Bootstrapping the Yield Curve","date":"2020-03-16","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2020-03-16\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 16 March 2020</div> <h3>Near-identical Treasuries no longer agree</h3> <p>You build the zero curve that a rates book is marked and hedged off. It is the dash for cash: foreign official holders, funds meeting redemptions and levered basis traders are all selling Treasuries, and for several days yields have risen while stocks fell. Dealers are out of balance sheet, bid-ask spreads are wide, and older off-the-run bonds have cheapened against on-the-run bonds of almost the same maturity. Yesterday the Fed cut rates to near zero and announced at least $500 billion of Treasury purchases. How do you build today's curve?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Feed in whichever bond prints at each maturity, on- or off-the-run, as usual.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Bootstrapping is sequential. If a cheap, illiquid bond sets one of the short rates, its error carries into every longer rate solved after it, and your hedges move for reasons that have nothing to do with rates.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Build from the most liquid inputs you trust, check the short end hard, and treat the off-the-run gap as a liquidity cost, not a rate.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The algorithm still works; its inputs disagree. Choosing clean inputs keeps the curve meaningful, and recording the scatter of other bonds around it tells you how much illiquidity you are carrying.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Lever up: buy the cheap off-the-runs and short the on-the-runs. They must converge.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">They probably will, but convergence needs someone with balance sheet to force it, and that is exactly what has run out. Levered relative-value positions like this were being forced out at the worst prices.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Fit one smooth curve through every bond and price everything off it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It gives you a single curve, which is useful for reading the shape. But it hides that some of those bonds can only be sold well below the fitted price right now.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 23 March the Fed dropped its limit and said it would buy Treasuries in the amounts needed. On 1 April it temporarily excluded Treasuries and reserves from banks' supplementary leverage ratio, freeing dealer balance sheet. Market functioning recovered over the following weeks, and near-identical bonds lined up on one curve again. The cost: leveraged relative-value funds were forced out at the worst prices, and the Fed's balance sheet grew by more than a trillion dollars in about a month.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200315a.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FOMC statement, 15 March 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200323b.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Federal Reserve press release, 23 March 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20200401a.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Federal Reserve Board press release, 1 April 2020</a>; <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/WALCL\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fed total assets, FRED</a> (4.31 trillion dollars on 11 March, 5.81 trillion on 1 April 2020).</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"yield-curve-growth-expectations","title":"What the Yield Curve Says About Growth","date":"2022-07","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2022-07\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · July 2022</div> <h3>The curve has just inverted. Do you sell?</h3> <p>It is July 2022. The Fed began hiking in March and is raising rates faster than at any time in four decades. This month the 2s10s curve has inverted. A morning note says: \"The curve just inverted. A recession is coming. Sell equities now.\" You hold an equity position. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell everything today. Inversion means recession, and recession means stocks fall.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The curve measures expectations, not timing. In 2006 to 2007 the curve was flat or inverted for more than a year before the recession, and stocks rose in between. Even when the curve is right, the fuse can be long.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Read it as expectations. The 2-year was dragged up by hikes, so the forwards are pricing cuts later. Note the expected slowdown, and judge the equity position on its own evidence.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This uses the curve for what it measures: the market's expected path of rates. A recession is only dated in hindsight, so the inversion cannot tell you when, or even whether, to sell.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Trim a little and wait for an official recession call before doing more.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A small trim is defensible risk control. But the official call comes from the NBER many months after the turning point (the December 2007 peak was announced in December 2008), so you would be waiting for a label, not a signal.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Ignore the curve. The bond market knows nothing about growth.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The curve is a genuine reading of where the market expects the Fed to take rates, and why. Throwing it away loses real information about policy, even if it is a poor clock.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> The 2s10s curve stayed inverted until around September 2024: the longest continuous inversion in the series, and at its deepest in 2023 around a full percentage point below zero. The first GDP estimates for the first half of 2022 showed two negative quarters, yet the NBER declared no recession and unemployment stayed low. As of this writing, no recession has been dated to follow that inversion. It ended with a bull steepening as the Fed began cutting.</p> <p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10Y2Y\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">10-year minus 2-year Treasury spread (T10Y2Y), FRED</a> (first sustained inversion 6 July 2022, low of −1.08 points on 3 July 2023, back above zero in September 2024; the previous longest inversion, 1978&ndash;80, lasted about 20 months against about 26 here); National Bureau of Economic Research, Business Cycle Dating Committee announcement, 1 December 2008 (peak in December 2007).</p></div> </div>"}];
